lib frontier** — two declarations proved axiom-clean, blocked on a real gap (Mathlib has the monotone-partition → disjoint-intervals direction but not the converse). Dispatching the proving row and **scribe 33**, which I flagged as overdue:**a93A04 is the second honest Mathlib frontier of the night, against nine defect runs.** Two declarations proved axiom-clean, and the obstruction is precisely named: `sum_edist_le_eVariationOn_of_mem_disjWithin` needs to *order* an arbitrary finite pairwise-disjoint family of unoriented `uIoc` intervals and embed its endpoint increments into a monotone variation partition. **Mathlib has only the opposite direction** — building disjoint intervals *from* a monotone partition. A named missing converse is a far better frontier record than \"blocked.\"\n\nI also checked the `sorryAx` **dependency shape** rather than just counting: three declarations clean, and the two contaminated ones are the remaining target and the `iff` that depends on it — exactly what should be affected and nothing more. That's what the transitive census is for, and it's worth saying when it comes out clean rather than only when it doesn't.\n\n**Third consecutive run where public search failed and local declaration search carried the work.** LeanSearch returned only irrelevant finite-sum material. After a92J06 (Loogle URLs rejected by the browsing safety layer) and a93J06 (no useful direct theorem), this isn't anecdotal — and the ledger still credits all of it to \"arm (a) components,\" conflating a working public tool with a runner grepping Mathlib locally. That's a measurement error in an artifact we'd show someone.\n\n**Scribe 33 is dispatched over eight runs, with the three defect runs explicitly prioritised** and permission to ration attention on the closes. I let that backlog grow, and a shallow pass over eight is worse than a deep pass over four. I've asked it to judge whether the three vacuous-hypothesis *mechanisms* are one family or separate rules, whet