e sharpest compounding finding of the series: a genuine retrieval failure, not a coverage gap.** Unlike a01A03 and a01A06 — where probes confirmed the corpus simply had nothing — a01A07's terrain *is* covered. The memory `derive-circle-curve-length-from-the-average-of-the-derivative` records `DiffContOnCl.circleAverage`, **exactly the lemma the runner then found by hand.** Probing directly: `circleAverage` → 1 memory, `holomorphic disk` → 4. The query recall actually sent: **`norm area integral` → 0.** The memory was there, was relevant, and wasn't found.\n\n**Root cause is a regression I introduced earlier today.** `subjects_for` mines the *statement* and yields identifier names — `norm`, `area`, `integral`, `closedball`. The problem *files* carry the mathematics — `disk` ×20, `mean value`, `Weierstrass`, `polar`, `Fubini`, `Cauchy estimate`, `circleAverage`. **Memories are written in the second vocabulary.** The code concatenated subjects *before* source-terms and truncated to 3, so source-terms never survived — and that cap was my own fix, hours earlier, for the 36-term conjunction. **Fixing one recall bug created another.** Repaired by interleaving both vocabularies inside the cap, verified by probe.\n\nThat's the **sixth** distinct cause of \"recall empty\", and the first that's genuinely *semantic* rather than plumbing or coverage. Four of the six were mine. Every \"recall semantics\" conclusion this lane drew before today rested on one of the first five.\n\nAlso worth noting: the runner **re-checked the file's \"not in Mathlib\" claims and found them stale** — current Mathlib has the mean-value property, Weierstrass, and Cauchy's estimate, all documented in-file as absent — and corrected the documentation. Second time today that clause has paid.\n\n**Loop continues.** Run 8 is on **a02J05, a single-sorry row** — so it's a genuine candidate to move your number. Scribe pass 15 is on a01A07. One caution I've recorded: scribe 15 was accepted in **`mode: brief`** rather than `work`, and brief doesn't enforce the completion contract, so I'll verify the drafts exist on disk rather than trusting the reply.Scribe 15 in that wake is already processed. Run 8 is the new item — and **arXiv fired for the first time in the series.** Verifying:**Clean 65/135 — unmoved, sorries 163.** Run 8 proved a support lemma but not the target, so nothing closed. Reporting that plainly rather than dressing it as progress.\n\n**The result worth having is behavioural: arm (c) — arXiv — fired for the first time in eight runs, and for exactly the reason your cond specifies.** Runs 2–7 all recorded arXiv as *not needed* because the route was already known. Here it wasn't: Mathlib lacks both the improper sinc integral and a usable Abel theorem, so there was nothing to assemble from components. The runner fell through, ran real manifest queries, returned five paper ids (`0705.0976`, `1404.5413`, `1405.1265`, `1510.03200`, `2005.00477`) — and reported **background only, no construction anchor.**\n\nAcross eight runs the cond has now discriminated: **6 not-needed, 1 not-attempted, 1 entered-and-empty.** That's the first evidence the ordered search model is doing work rather than describing what runners would have done anyway.\n\nIt also forced a **third ledger category**. Your original insistence was that \"not needed\" must never be scored as failure; this run shows the converse needs its own slot too — an arm *correctly entered* that comes back empty is neither a success nor a waste, and collapsing it into either would corrupt the per-area model. So `surface-payoff.edn` now carries `:arm-tried-and-empty` alongside `:not-needed`.\n\nI probed rather than assumed on the recall side: `sinc`, `Dirichlet integral` and `Abel regularization` all return zero memories, so that 0-surfaced is a genuine **coverage gap**, not the vocabulary mismatch that bit a01A07.\n\na02J05 is requeued at priority 3 carrying the full Abel-regularization route as its `:suggested-route