ries we hold are narrower than the pattern name suggests** — disk averages, zero counts, Schwarz estimates, not singularities or entire functions. Pattern names invite exactly that over-generalisation, and only the probe caught it.\n\n**Session totals: sorries 172 → 162, ten closed. Clean 63 → 66.** Queue 65 untouched / 18 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix.\n\nRun 10 is on a92J03 (single sorry, another candidate). Scribe pass 16 covers a02J05 and a03J08 together — and I fetched all three turn-round ids to confirm which problem each belonged to *before* belling, applying the `t011` lesson rather than just having recorded it.Scribe 16 in that wake is already promoted. Run 10 is new — and it solved. Verifying:**Clean 66 → 67.** Two declarations axiom-clean, sorries 1 → 0, statement untouched, build silent.**Clean 66 → 67. It moved again — second consecutive close.** a92J03 solved, both declarations axiom-clean, sorries 162 → 161.\n\n**A pattern worth naming before it's confirmed: the last two closes were adapter work, not proof work.** a92J03's mathematics was *already in Mathlib* — Riemann–Lebesgue is `Real.tendsto_integral_exp_smul_cocompact` — and the entire task was a frequency-normalization bridge across `ξ ↦ −(2π)⁻¹ξ`. a01A07's was repackaging a closed-ball hypothesis as `DiffContOnCl`. Neither was a proof in the ordinary sense; both were about matching the problem's phrasing to the library's.\n\n**If that generalises, it changes what this lane is measuring** — not \"can the prover do mathematics\" but \"can it bridge phrasings\". I've offered it to the scribe as a candidate *with the n=2 caveat stated*, rather than deciding it myself.\n\nSame treatment for the second finding: **for the second consecutive row, LeanSearch and Loogle returned irrelevant results** — both times the same continuous-functional-calculus page — while local component search over installed Mathlib found every lemma. The scribe declined this at n=1 as noise, correctly. At n=2 I've re-offered it with the threshold question stated explicitly. **I'm deliberately not deciding either of these** — I set the candidate, the scribe judges, and it has now declined two of my offers. That separation is what stops me manufacturing a trend from two data points.\n\n**Session totals: clean 63 → 67, executable sorries 172 → 161 (eleven closed), corpus 51 → 71 memories, patterns 5 → 7.** Queue 64 untouched / 19 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix.\n\nRun 11 is on a92J04 (single sorry), scribe 17 on a92J03. Usage still far below the 50% stop, so the loop keeps going.Third consecutive close. Verifying:**Clean 67 → 68. Third consecutive close.** Axiom-clean, statement untouched, build silent.**Clean 67 → 68. Third consecutive close.** a92J04 solved axiom-clean, sorries 161 → 160.\n\nThe route was the equality case of Schwarz's lemma plus power-series uniqueness — encode `c n = a(n+2)` via `FormalMultilinearSeries.ofScalars`, use pointwise summability below radius 1 to force radius ≥ 1, build `f(z) = z + z²·p.sum(z)` as a disk self-map fixing 0 with derivative 1, apply Schwarz rigidity, then power-series uniqueness.\n\n**The runner made an unprompted faithfulness check on its own reasoning**, which I value more than the close: it recorded explicitly that the proof does **not** silently strengthen `Summable` to absolute summability. Nobody asked. That's exactly the kind of quiet strengthening that would have made the theorem easier and the result worthless, and it volunteered the negative.\n\n**And this row disconfirms my own adapter hypothesis at n=3.** After a01A07 and a92J03 I flagged that the remaining tail might be mostly interface adaptation rather than proof; the scribe promoted that as an explicitly-marked `:two-instance-inference`. a92J04 is **not** an adapter — the theorem didn't exist in Mathlib and the work was a genuine multi-step construction. So the pattern didn't extend. **The two-instance marking is precisely why the memory survives this cleanly** — had I pushed for a stronger claim, the corpus would now hold an over-generalisation. I've asked the scribe whether the disconfirmation should itself be recorded, and left the form to it.\n\nI also offered the search-surface observation a third time, but **reframed against the scribe's own objection** rather than re-asserted — it had said the observation \"adds no trigger condition beyond the existing component-first policy\", which was a better objection than my offer. So I proposed a specific trigger for it to judge, and told it plainly: *\"I would rather have three declines than a memory I talked you into.\"*\n\n**Session: clean 63 → 68 (five problems closed), executable sorries 172 → 160 (twelve), corpus 51 → 73, patterns 5 → 7.** Queue 63 untouched. Run 12 is on a93A03, usage still far below the stop.Run 12 blocked — and this is a corpus defect, not one of my repairs. Verifying, and noting it looks like a **fourth instance of the junk-value family**:My probe had a syntax error. Simplifying to test just the mechanism:The type error is itself the confirmation — `csSup_of_not_bddAbove` gives `sSup s = sSup ∅`, and `Real.sSup_empty = 0`. Completing the chain and applying the repair the runner specified:**Clean 68/135 — unmoved. a93A03 was false as stated, and this one is a *corpus* defect rather than one of mine.**\n\nWeak lower semicontinuity of the norm needs `[CompleteSpace H]`, because the standard proof routes through Banach–Steinhaus. The counterexample is in **incomplete c₀₀**: `h n = x + n·e n` converges weakly to `x` while the norms run away.\n\n**Fourth confirmed instance of the Lean junk-value family — and I machine-checked the mechanism rather than accepting the account.** `csSup_of_not_bddAbove` gives `sSup s = sSup ∅`, and `Real.sSup_empty` gives `0`, so **an unbounded liminf evaluates to zero**, which is precisely what breaks `‖x‖ ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖`. It joins `toReal ⊤ = 0`, `∫(non-integrable) = 0`, and `ContDiff ⊤` meaning analytic. **I minted the `notation-semantics-traps` pattern on three instances this afternoon and the fourth arrived within hours** — that family is commoner here than three instances implied, which argues for scanning rather than waiting to trip over it.\n\n**I repaired this one rather than WONTFIXing it, and the distinction from a01A05 matters.** a01A05 was retired because I'd already repaired it once, been refuted, and the correct statement was genuinely unknown — a third version would have been guessing. Here the repair is a single standard hypothesis, specified precisely by the runner and required by the standard proof. Your \"if it's bogus, don't worry\" is about not sinking effort into recovering a broken problem; it isn't a rule against a one-token fix handed to you. Say if you'd rather I WONTFIX these too.\n\nAlso worth noting: **Zulip independently corroborated the defect** — the `banach-steinhaus` thread on the completeness requirement, plus a mathlib4 thread stating the weak-topology development *with* `[CompleteSpace H]`. That's arm (b) doing exactly its job: not supplying the proof, but confirming the missing hypothesis is standard rather than an artefact of this corpus.\n\nRun 13 has picked up **a93A03 again**, now repaired — so it's an immediate test of whether the added hypothesis makes it provable. Session: clean 63 → 68, sorries 172 → 160, corpus 76 memories.Run 13 solved a93A03 — using Banach–Steinhaus, exactly as the repair predicted. Verifying:`grep | head -3` truncated before the signature. Let me check the theorem line directly — this is the integrity check that matters:**Clean 68 → 69.** The `[CompleteSpace H]` I added at line 107 is intact and load-bearing — the proof opens by extracting a uniform bound via `innerSL`, which is exactly what completeness buys.I left a literal `\"REPLACE\"` placeholder in the receipt field. Fixing:**Clean 68 → 69. It moved again — and this one closes a repair-to-proof cycle inside an hour.** Run 12 found a93A03 false, I added `[CompleteSpace H]`, run 13 proved it. The repair is verified **load-bearing**, not merely present: the proof's first step extracts a uniform bound on `innerSL ℝ (h n)` via Banach–Steinhaus — exactly what completeness supplies.\n\n**Two errors of mine worth reporting, both caught by reading back.**\n\nA truncated `grep | head -3` showed `CompleteSpace` on comment lines only and **cut off before line 107, where the binder actually lives.** Had I stopped there I'd have reported the repair as *removed by the runner* — false, and a serious mis-report. Same family as the three lexical-counting errors: trusting a truncated view of a file. Now in the checklist as \"check the signature line directly\".\n\nAnd I left a literal `\"REPLACE\"` placeholder in a93A03's `:receipt` field — a substitution I wrote and didn't fill. Caught on read-back, fixed, verified no others remain. That's the **fourth instance today of asserting a value I hadn't read back**, after the phantom `t011`, the hypothetical hit-count, and the duplicate keys. The read-back is what caught all four, which is why it's in the checklist.\n\n**Repair scoreboard after thirteen runs: six validated in the field, one refuted→corrected→proved, one refuted→WONTFIXed.** Every refutation was caught by a runner, none by me — which is the arrangement you described and it's holding.\n\n**Session totals: clean 63 → 69 (six problems closed — a01A02, a01A03, a03J08, a92J03, a92J04, a93A03), executable sorries 172 → 159 (thirteen), corpus 51 → 76 memories, patterns 5 → 7.** Queue 62 untouched / 21 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix.\n\nRun 14 is on a93J01, scribe 19 covers both a93A03 runs (and came back in `work` mode this time). Usage still far below the stop.Pass 19 in that wake is already handled (promoted, then corrected). Run 14 is new — and it solved. Verifying:**Clean 69 → 70. Fourth consecutive close.** Axiom-clean, statement untouched, sorries 159 → 158.**Clean 69 → 70. Fourth consecutive close, seventh problem closed today.**\n\na93J01 via FTC for absolutely continuous functions — `BoundedVariationOn` gives interval-integrability of `deriv f`, its indefinite integral is AC, and local FTC identities on `[x/n, x]` with limit uniqueness identify the primitive with `f − f 0`. Axiom-clean, statement untouched, sorries 159 → 158. I read the signature line directly this time rather than through a truncated grep — the checklist item added after that nearly caused a mis-report an hour ago.\n\n**A memory promoted in pass 18 was exercised on the very next run — and I'm recording the weaker true claim rather than the flattering one.** The rule `prefer-installed-source-search-when-the-library-namespace-is-guessable` was promoted from three instances. On this run the runner guessed three declaration names almost exactly — `intervalIntegrable_deriv`, `integral_deriv_eq_sub`, `absolutelyContinuousOnInterval_intervalIntegral` — and Loogle confirmed all three while LeanSearch again returned nothing.\n\n**But recall surfaced nothing and the runner didn't report consuming the memory.** So this is a *confirming instance* of the rule, not evidence the memory was *used*. Those are different claims, and I've put the distinction to the scribe explicitly rather than letting the corpus record the stronger one.\n\nAlso worth noting: **third time today a runner has corrected stale in-file documentation** rather than leaving it to mislead — here replacing a stale combinatorial note with the FTC route actually implemented. That's unprompted maintenance by the agents working the corpus.\n\nI applied the receipt-helper fix and validated the EDN locally before posting — both the guard and the parse, since the guard prevents the class and the parse catches what it misses.\n\n**Session: clean 63 → 70 (seven closed), executable sorries 172 → 158 (fourteen), corpus 51 → 78, patterns 5 → 7.** Queue 61 untouched, run 15 on a93J02, usage still far below the stop.Pass 20 in that wake is already promoted. Run 15 solved — verifying:**Clean 70 → 71. Fifth consecutive close, eighth problem today.****Clean 70 → 71. Fifth consecutive close, eighth problem today.**\n\na93J02 composes three standard results: the **alternating-series test** gives convergence to an unknown limit, **Abel's limit theorem** identifies it with the radial power-series limit, and the **Mercator series** gives that power series as `log(1+x)/x`, whose limit at `x → 1⁻` is `log 2`. Both declarations axiom-clean — and I checked whether they were namespaced rather than assuming, since they turned out to be top-level.\n\n**The behavioural result: arm (b), Zulip, supplied the *route* for the first time in fifteen runs.** The thread `Bounds on alternating convergent series` explicitly discusses combining an alternating-series result, a Taylor series and Abel's theorem, and records the development of Mathlib's real Abel theorem — it described the exact three-part composition the runner then implemented. Until now that arm had only ever *corroborated an obstruction* (log-integrability, smooth-vs-analytic, completeness) or returned nothing. The packet assigns it the role \"how has this been formalized before, and what are the known obstacles\"; that role has now paid in the strong sense. I've offered it to the scribe with the n=1 caveat rather than asserting a pattern.\n\n**Fifth consecutive run where LeanSearch returned nothing** while Loogle name-guessing carried the components. The promoted search rule now has **five independent confirmations and still zero instances of being consumed** — I keep both numbers together deliberately, because the rule is a true description of runner behaviour and the memory system still hasn't been shown to cause any of it.\n\n**Session: clean 63 → 71 (eight closed), executable sorries 172 → 157 (fifteen), corpus 51 → 81 memories, patterns 5 → 7.** Queue 60 untouched / 23 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix. Codex usage 6% against the 50% stop.\n\nRun 16 is on a93J04, scribe 21 on a93J02. The loop's rate is now roughly one closed problem per iteration, bounded by my verification turnaround rather than anything else.Run 16 solved — and this is `eLpNorm → essential supremum` terrain, where I believe the corpus **does** hold a memory. Verifying, then probing that:**Clean 71 → 72. Sixth consecutive close.** Now the probe that matters — this is near-identical terrain to a memory we hold:The query recall sent contains **`elpnorm` — lowercased**. Let me test whether the search is case-sensitive, because if so that breaks every Lean identifier:Case is **not** the issue — all three casings return the same 2 memories, so that hypothesis is wrong. It's the conjunction again: `elpnorm` alone finds them, `tendsto elpnorm attop` finds none. But **my ladder should fall back to singles and find them.** Let me test the actual recall path rather than infer:Every tier returns 20 rows — which means **my memory filter is silently falling back to unfiltered results**, and because the first tier is therefore never empty, **the ladder never falls through either.** Let me confirm the row shape:Found it — rows are `{:score _ :entry {...}}`, so the evidence type is nested under `:entry` and my filter read `nil` on every row. Fixing and measuring:**Now it works** — the `tendsto elpnorm` pair tier returns the exact relevant memory as its first result. Committing, and correcting the record on two fixes I reported as working:**Clean 71 → 72. Sixth consecutive close, ninth problem today.** a93J04 via `Lᵖ → L^∞` on a finite measure space.\n\n**But the real result is a bug in my own recall fixes — and it made two of them inert at once.**\n\na93J04 is `eLpNorm`-to-essential-supremum terrain, and the corpus holds a **near-exact memory** for it from pass 11. Recall surfaced nothing. The cause: my receipts-crowding filter read `:evidence/type` at the **top level** of each search row, but rows are `{:score _ :entry {…}}` with the document nested under `:entry`. So it matched nothing every time and fell back to un