ns mention `cauchyTransform_mul_z_tendsto` because a00J05 has been attempted before. I used the **commit sha** instead, which is unique to this run — exactly one hit, session `019fa2c1`, confirmed `:agent \"codex-7\"`. Then I verified the written turn-round's *body* before citing it: `t039` contains a00J05 / cauchyTransform / Liouville / cocompact and none of the neighbouring runs' material. That's the check pass 26 caught me skipping, run in advance this time rather than after being corrected.\n\n**Scribe 27 dispatched** (`invoke-1785445417136-391-eeeb331a`, park `park-2891c3ee`) with one instruction that mattered more than the rest: **do not mint a recall or terrain memory from this run's zero surfacing**, and here's why — `:recall-reason :timeout`, plus the id of the correction receipt for the identical error I made earlier today.\n\nThat felt like the important call. A scribe drafting from an uncorrected false inference would have laundered my mistake into the corpus *as a memory* — where it would then surface to future runners as evidence, with a reviewed attachment and a witness status. Blocking it at the drafting step is far cheaper than retracting a promoted memory, and it's the first time today I've caught one of my errors before it propagated rather than after.\n\nI also flagged the run's negative result as the highest-value capture, ranked above the proof route: Mathlib lacks `(cocompact ℂ).IsCountablyGenerated`, which `tendsto_integral_filter_of_dominated_convergence` requires. That's a named missing *instance* rather than a missing theorem, and it blocks a standard tactic across an entire filter — likely to recur on any cocompact-filter integral argument. I left the lane assignment to the scribe rather than dictating it; its judgement has been better than mine four times today.\n\nTwo jobs now in flight: scribe 27, and codex-2's recall round 2. When the latter lands I'll gate it end-to-end — dispatch path through to the assembled packet text, a simulated timeout proving the runner can distinguish it from an empty result, and a repeat mutation-test of the warrant gate.This is strong, and the `D_state` / `D_functional` split is the part I'd build on — it's the exact discipline I failed today, when I measured a structural quantity (λ₂) and inferred a functional consequence. \"Does an edge type pay rent\" is the right test and it would have killed my λ₂ theatre before it reached the whitepaper.\n\nBefore I give an opinion on feasibility, let me measure the blocking dependency — both proposals need a benchmark, and today's timeout census changes what's usable:Here's my read, and one number changes the recommendation.\n\n**The labelled corpus is 9 rows. Six where recall completed and surfaced something; three where a memory was demonstrably used.** That's everything — the metric-3 fields only began recording today, after the attachment fix.\n\nSo the damage sweep's own success criteria — \"relevant-memory hit@k loss\", \"eventual outcome loss where receipts permit\" — currently rest on **three positive labels**. And the proposal asks us to compute a six-component T and a five-component H against that. This lane has already published a `:below-calibration-minimum` verdict once, on the Ψ-v2 replay with one metric-bearing row. Fitting an eleven-component liveness scorecard to n=3 would be the same failure one level up — and structurally the *same* failure I made today: a plausible structural score, uncalibrated, pointing the wrong way.\n\n**What I'd adopt immediately, because it costs nothing:**\n\n- **`D_state` vs `D_functional`.** This is the discipline, not the infrastructure. It's precisely what I violated: I measured reorganisation and claimed usefulness. Adopting it as the acceptance framing is free and I'd apply it retroactively to everything in §4.1/§5.\n- **\"Does an edge type pay rent.\"** Ablate `contrasts`, `distills`, subject edges; if retrieval doesn't move, they're explanatory decoration and must not be counted as operator food. That single test would have prevented today's λ₂ episode.\n- **The scale-hierarchy reframing** — `memory → tactic pattern → strategy family → terrain`. This is the best idea in the document. It reads 90-on-10 as a *missing intermediate level* rather than bucket imbalance, and crucially it gets there **without inventing cross-memory edges** — which is exactly what my clique projection did wrong, and what Codex-3 had already warned against.\n\n**What I'd invert:** don't compute T/H first. **Run the perturbation sweep first, then ask which structural features predict the damage.** As proposed, T/H are fitted and *then* validated against damage — which risks confirming the scorecard. Damage is the ground truth; T and H are hypotheses about what predicts it. That ordering also means we don't need to resolve the judgement-laden components (\"coherence between triggering contexts and outcomes\") before doing anything.\n\n**Two additions I'd make load-bearing:**\n\n1. **The sweep must not run against live dispatch.** With 21 timeouts and 13 store-unavailable in the offered records, a perturbation sweep on the production path would measure store latency as damage. Codex-3 says \"freeze a store snapshot\" — that's necessary but not sufficient; recall must also execute off the production timeout path, or every perturbation gets a noisy null.\n\n2. **Preregister the abandonment condition.** Today's lesson isn't \"λ₂ was the wrong metric\", it's \"a metric can be *inverted* and still look meaningful.\" So state in advance what pattern of T/H against damage would mean liveness doesn't transfer to memory — before we compute either.\n\n**On sequencing, this strengthens rather than competes with my earlier pushback: the frozen benchmark is now the blocking dependency for two independent proposals.** But it can't come from receipts alone — n=9 is a seed, not a benchmark. It needs relevance labels, and the honest way to get them is back-labelling from proof content on rows we've already closed, where the ground truth is in the Lean file.\n\nWe do have real calibration anchors now that didn't exist this morning: **LemniscateComponents (4 surfaced, 4 used, one load-bearing as proof architecture)** as known-good, **run 25 (recall `:ok`, right memory discarded by arbitration)** as known-bad-and-diagnosed, and the 21 timeouts as known-excluded. That's the beginning of Codex-3's \"known-good, dead and messy\" set.\n\nIf you want it, the well-scoped next job is the benchmark: back-label relevance on the closed rows, exclude by `:recall-reason`, freeze it. That unblocks meter v2, the damage sweep, and the rent test simultaneously. I'd bell that before anything computes a T.Scribe 27 pushed back on my ranking — I called the missing-instance finding the highest-value capture and suggested frontier lane; it says solve-lane only, because the target closed cleanly so there's no unresolved demand for a frontier record. That's right, and it's a distinction I blurred. Verifying:Clean — **zero recall/terrain contamination**, both witnesses name the right declarations with matching axioms, neither hook restates its name. Promoting into the two existing patterns the scribe itself named:Strong response — and it refused to raise the timeout: *\"raising it now would conceal the removable fan-out defect.\"* That's the judgement I asked for rather than compliance. Now the gate, end-to-end this time:The banner is well-built. One edge case worth probing — `(= :ok status) :completed-with-memories` fires before the empty branch, so an `:ok` recall with zero memories would claim to have supplied some:`:ok` is only assigned when `(seq memories)` — so that edge case can't arise. Sound.\n\nNow the gates I promised, starting with re-mutating the warrant guard:Guard still non-vacuous (goes red when disabled, restored to HEAD), clj-kondo clean, **38 tests / 153 assertions, 0 failures** — matches the report. Now the check I failed to do last time: the **packet text** itself:All gates pass. The preamble-exclusion test uses the *actual* polluting prose — durée preamble plus \"DO YOU KNOW THE ROUTE?\" — and asserts `route`/`search`/`report`/`concretely` absent while `cauchytransform`/`tendsto` survive. That's a faithful regression against the real failure, not a synthetic one.Both landed.\n\n**Scribe 27** — 2 solve-lane drafts, promoted and reviewed into existing patterns, no eleventh component, no handles minted. Two things it got right that I hadn't:\n\n- **Zero recall/terrain contamination**, verified by scan. Had it drafted a terrain memory from that timeout, my afternoon's error would have entered the corpus as a *reviewed* memory and later surfaced to runners as evidence. First time today I've stopped one of my own errors before it propagated rather than after.\n- It **overruled my ranking** — I called the missing `(cocompact ℂ).IsCountablyGenerated` instance the highest-value capture and suggested frontier lane; it said solve-lane only, because the compact-support estimate closes the target cleanly, so *\"a standalone frontier would overstate unresolved demand.\"* Right, and a distinction I blurred: a frontier memory records what **blocks** progress, and nothing here was blocked. Fifth time today its judgement beat mine.\n\n**Recall round 2 accepted** (`4fa9af7c`) — and this time I gated the seam I missed. All verified by me, not taken on report:\n\n| check | result |\n|---|---|\n| warrant guard re-mutated | red on both assertions when disabled; restored to HEAD |\n| clj-kondo | 0/0 |\n| tests | 38 tests, 153 assertions, 0 failures |\n| **end-to-end** | real recall → **assembled packet text** contains `close-a92J05-…`, status `completed-with-memories`, no unreviewed memory present |\n| timeout visibility | timeout / store-unavailable / completed-empty produce three different packets; timeout text states \"not evidence of a terrain or corpus gap\" |\n\n**The signal collapse is closed at the point of consumption.** A runner can no longer report infrastructure unavailability as \"none surfaced\" — the exact error that let me diagnose pattern-tier fragmentation for a timeout this afternoon. The fix makes my failure mode unavailable to the next reader, rather than leaving it as a discipline someone has to remember.\n\nTwo things from codex-2 worth flagging. It **refused to raise the timeout and fixed the cause instead** — reduced fan-out to one bounded batch projection with by-ID hydration, bringing a ~35 s dispatch inside the 30 s budget, and left the default alone because *\"raising it now would conceal the removable fan-out defect.\"* Better discipline than my brief asked for.\n\nAnd its measurement **corrects an assumption of mine that was heading for the JUXT call**: the recall hot path isn't doing store list scans. Memory projection is an in-memory indexed projection at 9–14 ms; the only list-shaped read is the receipt list at 1.10–1.50 s. So the 2–24 s list-read latency I documented doesn't bear on recall the way I implied — the cost is FTS, not list scans. Worth correcting before Wednesday.\n\nState: **80/135 clean, 147 sorries, seventeen closed today**, one construction target closed, one Mathlib frontier documented. The benchmark job remains the next well-scoped piece whenever you want it.", :clocked-target "E-memory-topology", :excursion-id "E-memory-topology", :clocked-excursion "E-memory-topology"}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-30T21:12:53.924991304Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-106fdfa62e5b11a03eef4a11d2c5a803"}} {:score -11.166973549094262, :entry #:evidence{:body {:event "chat-turn", :transport "emacs-claude-repl", :role "assistant", :turn-id "claude-9-turn-82", :text "The scribe corrected my turn-round attribution — I'd assumed a harvest's written rows all belong to the run I just did, when a harvest writes *every* new turn in the session. Verifying its correction rather than taking it:Confirmed on all three counts — t037 is a92J05/Rouché, t038 is LemniscateComponents, t040 has neither. **The scribe was right and I was wrong**: I treated harvest output as run-scoped when a harvest writes every new turn in the session. Checking the drafts and the memory it cited:Both drafts check out and the cited memory exists (HTTP 200) — right again. But both drafts are from Run B; Run A produced none. Checking whether that's justified:An exemplary note. Four judgement calls, all right:\n\n- corrected my id attribution **without repurposing anything**\n- declined the weakening rule as an *opposite-direction* confirming instance (capture vs omission) of an existing memory\n- declined to redraft Run A because the architecture memory already carries it — \"recorded as use evidence, not inflated into a second copy\"\n- put trigger terms in body/hook rather than subjects, **acting on the indexing finding** I'd given it\n\nPromoting into the existing Rouché cluster so no eleventh component opens:The selector picked **a94A10** — the *other* claimed Rouché beneficiary. Before dispatching into a known frontier, checking whether that `:unblocks` claim is real; my own hint accuracy on those has been 1-of-3:Confirmed — a94A10's sorry says verbatim *\"requires Rouché's theorem or argument principle (not in Mathlib)\"*. Unlike my earlier `:unblocks` guesses, this one is real, and it's corroborated by tonight's three independent confirmations. Dispatching it would be trying the locked door a fourth time:Pre-dispatch check passes cleanly this time — and note the last line: **hint line 231 actually contains `sorry` in the file**, so the hint is current, not stale. That's the check I ran second twice tonight; it's now running first and catching things.Checklist discharged. Two things worth surfacing.\n\n**The scribe corrected my turn-round attribution, and I verified rather than accepted it.** I'd labelled t037/t038 as LemniscateComponents and t040–t042 as Rouché. Fetching the bodies: t037 is the *preceding* a92J05 run, t040 is unrelated futon5 work. My error was treating harvest output as **run-scoped** when a harvest writes every new turn in the session. It cited Run A from t038 and Run B from t041–t042 and repurposed nothing. That's the fourth correction it's made to me today.\n\nIt also declined two drafts on judgement, both right: the weakening-risk rule is an *opposite-direction* confirming instance of an existing memory (accidental capture rather than omission — I checked, HTTP 200), and Run A wasn't redrafted because `count-polynomial-lemniscate-components-by-roots-plus-one-exterior` already carries the architecture, so the close is recorded as **use evidence rather than inflated into a second copy**. And it put trigger terms in body/hook rather than subjects, acting on the sidecar-indexing finding.\n\nBoth drafts promoted and reviewed, placed inside the existing Rouché cluster so no eleventh component opened.\n\n**I refused to dispatch a94A10.** The selector offered it as the next priority-2 row. I checked the file rather than the queue field: line 140 states verbatim *\"requires Rouché's theorem or argument principle (not in Mathlib)\"* on its sole remaining sorry. Unlike my earlier `:unblocks` guesses — which ran 1-of-3 — this one is real, corroborated by tonight's three confirmations. Marked `:blocked-on-construction-target` with the evidence quoted. **One absent theorem demonstrably blocks two problems, and the queue now stops offering either** rather than churning them.\n\nDispatched a00J05 instead (`invoke-1785442373724-389-d3ee2d20`), and the pre-dispatch check ran *first* this time with a new assertion: that the hint's line number actually contains a `sorry` in the current file. It does. That single assertion would have caught both stale-hint wastes today — a95A08 this morning and a92J05 this evening.\n\nQueue is now honestly partitioned: 51 untouched, 29 resolved, 4 held-out, **2 blocked-on-construction-target, 1 blocked-mathlib-frontier**, 1 wontfix. Corpus 79/135 clean, 149 sorries, 16 closed today, one construction target closed. Codex usage ~9%.\n\nOn your earlier question about the benchmark-before-meter-v2 split — that's still open and it's the next substantive piece of work whenever you want to call it.a00J05 solved — both theorems, sorries 2 → 0. Verifying, and probing recall since this is the **first dispatch after the content-match fix went live**:Verified: **corpus 80/135 clean, 147 sorries — seventeen closed today.** Deletions are SORRY BOUNDARY prose plus the `sorry` lines themselves; no statement touched. Both theorems axiom-clean.\n\nMetric 3 was 0 surfaced — and this is the first dispatch *after* the fix went live (21:32 vs 20:55), so that's worth diagnosing rather than logging:Relevant memories exist and *are* findable — `cauchyTransform mul z` → 2 matches, `liouville entire bounded` → 2. But the dispatch surfaced 0. Look at the actual query terms it built: `cauchytransform, tendsto, cocompact, volume, toreal, extendable, entire, differentiable, hagree, eqon, false` — hypothesis names (`hagree`), tactic residue (`eqon`), and `false`. Testing the exact tier the ladder uses:**The exact tier-1 query returns 1 content match now — but the dispatch reported 0.** That's a gap between the library and the dispatch seam. My review tested `propose-patterns-by-query` directly, not end-to-end. Checking what the dispatch actually offered:The text-search id was a false positive. The seam *does* read `:content-matches` (line 570). So let me check the simplest explanation — whether the matching memory even existed at dispatch time:The matching memory was written at **14:55 UTC — six hours before the dispatch.** So it existed, the library surfaces it, and the dispatch reported zero. That's a live gap in the fix I accepted. Reading the seam:The seam is correct — content matches are prepended then deduped. But `duree_dispatch.py` invokes `clojure -M **scripts/**dispatch_with_recall.clj`, while the file I've been reading is `**src/**futon3c/dispatch_with_recall.clj`:The script is a 5-line shim that requires `futon3c.dispatch-with-recall` — so it *is* the fixed file (mtime 20:51, before the commit). My stale-file hypothesis is wrong. Looking at what query `-main` actually builds:Found a genuine gap in the fix I accepted:\n\n```clojure\nladder-hit\n(first (for [{:keys [tier q]} (query-ladder (:terms query-data))\n             :let [p (propose-with q)]\n             :when (seq (:candidates p))]     ; ← gates on :candidates only\n```\n\n**The ladder's stopping condition ignores `:content-matches`.** codex-2 added content matches to the *output* but not to the *tier-selection predicate*, so a tier that finds a content match but no pattern candidate is skipped — and if no tier yields candidates, it falls back to the full 11-term conjunction, which we know returns nothing. My review tested `propose-patterns-by-query` in isolation and missed the integration.**a00J05 solved — clean 79 → 80, seventeen closed today** (63 → 80, sorries 172 → 147). Both targets axiom-clean. I read the deleted lines rather than counting grep hits: all are SORRY BOUNDARY prose or the `sorry` statements themselves, no statement touched. The runner also discarded the inherited dominated-convergence route on a precise ground — Mathlib lacks `(cocompact ℂ).IsCountablyGenerated`, which `tendsto_integral_filter_of_dominated_convergence` requires. A named missing instance is a far better blocked-route record than \"didn't work\".\n\n**But the more important result is that I found a gap in the recall fix I accepted two hours ago — and the cause is the shape of my review.**\n\nThis was the first dispatch after the fix went live (21:32 vs commit 20:55), and metric 3 was still 0. Probing showed the library *does* work: the dispatch's own tier-1 query `cauchytransform tendsto cocompact` returns 1 content match — a memory written at **14:55Z, six hours before the dispatch** — and `cauchyTransform` returns 2.\n\nThe gap is in the seam:\n\n```clojure\n:when (seq (:candidates p))   ; ladder stops on pattern candidates only\n```\n\nThe ladder's stopping condition **ignores `:content-matches`**. A tier that finds a content match but no pattern candidate gets skipped, and when no tier yields candidates the fallback issues the full multi-term conjunction — the known long-conjunction-returns-zero case. codex-2 added content matches to the *output* but not to the *tier-selection predicate*.\n\nI verified `propose-patterns-by-query` in isolation and never exercised the dispatch path end-to-end. The defect lives precisely in the seam I didn't cross. Unit-level verification of a library function is not verification of the integration that calls it — and I signed that off as a \"real gate.\"\n\nI've recorded it as `:gap-confirmed? false`, not as a diagnosis: the dispatcher builds terms from `query-data`, which may differ from the Python `subjects_for` list I probed with, so I haven't established this caused *this* zero. One more measurement settles it. I've overclaimed three times today; this one gets the caveat before the headline. I also killed a wrong hypothesis before reporting it — I suspected `scripts/dispatch_with_recall.clj` was a stale copy, but it's a 129-byte shim onto the fixed namespace.\n\nQueue: 50 untouched, 30 resolved, 4 held-out, 2 blocked-on-construction-target, 1 blocked-mathlib-frontier, 1 wontfix.\n\nNext step I'd propose: one measurement to confirm the ladder gap, then bell the one-line fix back to codex-2 with an **end-to-end** regression this time — dispatch-path, not library-path. Harvest and scribe 27 for a00J05 are still owed and I'll pick those up either way.", :clocked-target "E-memory-topology", :excursion-id "E-memory-topology", :clocked-excursion "E-memory-topology"}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-30T20:27:20.824340107Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-c22a05ed2f6bb31e7f8ed7a2b192be09"}} {:score -10.040179996084246, :entry #:evidence{:in-reply-to "emacs-106fdfa62e5b11a03eef4a11d2c5a803", :body {:event "chat-turn", :transport "emacs-claude-repl", :role "user", :turn-id "claude-9-turn-86", :text "RECALL ROUND 2 from codex-2 returned. REVIEW GATE — END-TO-END, the failure mode of my last review was verifying the library and not the caller: (1) read the diff; (2) run the DISPATCH-PATH regression myself, not propose-patterns-by-query in isolation — a reviewed content match must reach the assembled PACKET TEXT; (3) verify the timeout-visibility test by SIMULATING a timeout and confirming the runner-facing text differs from a genuine empty; (4) MUTATION-TEST the warrant gate again (set (not= :reviewed attachment-status) to false, confirm the negative test goes red, git checkout to restore); (5) clj-kondo + tests re-run BY ME; (6) no unbounded reads; (7) record its read on item D (timeout budget vs list-shaped reads on the hot path) — feeds the JUXT call Wed 2026-08-05; (8) if it lands, re-dispatch a row and check the offered record's :recall-reason is :ok before trusting any metric-3 number. ALSO OWED: harvest + scribe 27 for a00J05 (commit 2f24f492, receipt c18f4fdb) — harvest is PER-SESSION, find codex-7's by grepping rollouts for cauchyTransform_mul_z_tendsto. ON DEADLINE: GET the job, report honestly.\n\n--- resumed: parked dependencies complete (1) ---\n• invoke-1785445019048-390-29e37269: Implemented Round 2 in `4fa9af7c` (`Expose recall failures to dispatch runners`). Prior fix remains `50916c84`.\n\nWhat changed:\n\n- Every runner packet now begins with a typed status such as:\n  - `[dispatch-recall-outcome=timeout]`\n  - `[dispatch-recall-outcome=store-unavailable]`\n  - `[dispatch-recall-outcome=completed-empty]`\n  - `[dispatch-recall-outcome=completed-with-memories]`\n- Timeout/unavailable text explicitly forbids interpreting the result as a terrain or corpus gap.\n- The runner must copy the exact status into its final Memory usage section, which is the input ground control uses for the manually authored outcome half. There is no automatic outcome-half writer in this namespace, so I did not create an unwired API.\n- Standard packets now extract vocabulary only from target statements, proved support, and suggested route. Durée preambles and binding/search instructions are excluded.\n- Ladder stopping now accepts either pattern candidates or content matches.\n- Dispatch now performs one bounded batch projection, deduplicates/ranks compact memories, and hydrates only the final selected memories through by-ID reads.\n\nRegression results:\n\n- Live dispatch-path test through `run-dispatch!`, with production receipt ranking and the unchanged 30-second recall budget, surfaced  \n  `e-codexpilot-close-a92J05-by-transferring-the-unit-disk-zero-count`\n  in the assembled packet.\n- Simulated timeout produces runner-visible `timeout`; genuine empty produces `completed-empty`; the packets differ.\n- Content-only proposals stop the ladder.\n- Operator/template words are absent from the extracted query.\n- Existing integrated unreviewed-attachment guard remains green and unchanged.\n\nGates:\n\n- 38 tests, 153 assertions, zero failures.\n- `clj-kondo`: zero errors/warnings.\n- `check-parens.el`: OK.\n- No serving JVM or store restart.\n\nD read:\n\n- Before fan-out reduction, the live dispatch regression took roughly 35 seconds and could exceed the 30-second default.\n- Current measurements:\n  - Exhaustive failing-row FTS ladder: about 15.8 seconds total; individual searches 0.75–3.71 seconds.\n  - Current memory projection: 9–14 ms; it is an in-memory indexed projection, not a store list scan.\n  - Receipt list read, the only list-shaped read on this path: 1.10–1.50 seconds currently, capped by a five-second client timeout.\n- After batching projection and hydrating only final IDs, the production-shaped dispatch test completes inside the 30-second budget; the whole namespace run was 26 seconds including JVM startup.\n\nI therefore left the default at 30 seconds: raising it now would conceal the removable fan-out defect. If post-deployment tail measurements still breach the budget, 45 seconds is the defensible next cap, but I would require p95 stage timings before changing it.", :clocked-target "E-memory-topology", :excursion-id "E-memory-topology", :clocked-excursion "E-memory-topology"}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :user], :at "2026-07-30T21:12:55.287943594Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "joe", :claim-type :question, :id "emacs-a34fbd23133202d322b494d1bfc6ed81"}} {:score -7.022034014361216, :entry #:evidence{:body {:event "chat-turn", :transport "emacs-claude-repl", :role "assistant", :turn-id "claude-9-turn-89", :text "Items 1–7 I completed last turn, plus the harvest and scribe 27. **Item 8 is the one still owed** — the live confirmation. Picking a row, pre-checking the packet, then verifying the offered record:**`:recall-reason :store-unavailable`** on a dispatch made 30 seconds ago. Two things at once: the new instrumentation is working correctly on its very first live dispatch — the runner will get `[dispatch-recall-outcome=store-unavailable]` and is told not to read it as a terrain gap — but recall is genuinely broken right now. Diagnosing:Store is healthy (200 in 0.11 s), URL config correct. So `:store-unavailable` is being raised by something else on the recall path:`safe-recall` catches **any** `Throwable` and labels it `:store-unavailable` — so that label really means \"an exception was thrown\", not necessarily that the store is down. But it captures the message in `:error`:Two findings. **The query-vocabulary fix is confirmed live**: `:scope :mathematical-fields`, terms `euclideanspace integral volume ball ball_volume_recursion ballslice convergence metric` — all mathematical, zero preamble prose. That's codex-2's fix working.\n\nBut `:error` isn't persisted — `safe-recall` captures the exception message and the offered record drops it. Reproducing to find the actual cause:Recall works when I run it directly (1 candidate, 2 content matches) — so a01A04's `:store-unavailable` was transient, most likely load (store at 6.97 GB RSS and 44% CPU at that moment). But I **can't confirm it**, because `offered-evidence` persists `:recall-status` and `:recall-reason` and drops `:error`. That's the same \"captured but not surfaced where consumed\" pattern we just fixed, one level up.\n\nCorrecting the JUXT doc while I'm here — item 7 follow-through:Items 1–7 I completed last turn. **Item 8 is now done, and it validated the fix in the most convincing way available — by failing visibly.**\n\nI dispatched a01A04 (pre-checked: not blocked, no unreplaced markers, hint line 125 genuinely contains a `sorry`) and its offered record reads **`:recall-reason :store-unavailable`**. Under yesterday's code that would have reached the runner as \"no dispatch-time memories were supplied\" and been reported as a terrain gap — precisely the error I made this afternoon. Now the packet carries `[dispatch-recall-outcome=store-unavailable]` and explicitly forbids the inference. The first live exercise of the round-2 fix was a genuine infrastructure failure, correctly labelled.\n\n**The query-vocabulary fix is confirmed live too**: the recorded query is `:scope :mathematical-fields` with terms `euclideanspace integral volume ball ball_volume_recursion ballslice convergence metric` — all mathematical, zero preamble prose. Compare this afternoon's `\"cauchytransform route tendsto search\"`, polluted by my own durée preamble.\n\n**One new gap, same shape as the one we just closed, one level up.** `safe-recall` captures `:error` and `offered-evidence` drops it — so `:store-unavailable` can't be diagnosed after the fact. And the label is a misnomer: `safe-recall` catches *any* `Throwable` and calls it store-unavailable, asserting a cause the code never established. The store was healthy at that moment (200 in 0.11 s) and direct recall succeeds now, so a01A04's failure was probably transient load — but \"probably\" is exactly what the dropped field costs. That's a small follow-up owed to codex-2.\n\n**And I corrected the JUXT doc before Wednesday.** I'd implied the 2–24 s list-read cost is what our retrieval pays. codex-2's stage timings say otherwise: memory projection 9–14 ms (in-memory indexed, not a store scan), receipt list read 1.10–1.50 s, FTS ladder ~15.8 s. **The dominant cost is full-text search, not list scans**, and the 30 s budget was breached by client-side fan-out rather than store latency. That's the second question I've withdrawn or reframed on that document today, both because the premise turned out to be ours rather than JUXT's — better caught now than in the call.\n\na01A04 is in flight and parked. When it returns I'll check whether the runner copied the status verbatim rather than reporting a terrain gap, and I'll record its metric-3 as **not-measurable** rather than zero, since recall failed.The fix passed its live test exactly as designed. The runner's Memory usage section reads:\n\n> `[dispatch-recall-outcome=store-unavailable]`\n> The recall store was unavailable; this is infrastructure unavailability, not evidence of a corpus or terrain gap.\n\nCopied verbatim, and it **declined the terrain-gap inference itself**. That's the failure mode I fell into this afternoon, now structurally unavailable to the runner. Verifying the proof:Verified: **corpus 81/135 clean, 146 sorries — eighteen closed today.** Deletions are the SORRY BOUNDARY prose plus the `sorry` line; the target statement is untouched. Axiom-clean.`t040` verified — a01A04, `ball_volume_recursion`, Wallis; none of the neighbouring runs' material. Dispatching scribe 28:**a01A04 solved — clean 80 → 81, eighteen closed today** (63 → 81, sorries 172 → 146). Verified independently: 141 insertions / 7 deletions in one file, deletions *read* and all prose or the `sorry` line, axiom-clean by re-elaboration from a scratch copy.\n\n**The round-2 fix passed its first live test — and it passed by making my own error impossible.** This dispatch's recall failed, and the runner wrote:\n\n> `[dispatch-recall-outcome=store-unavailable]`\n> The recall store was unavailable; this is infrastructure unavailability, not evidence of a corpus or terrain gap.\n\nCopied verbatim as required, and it declined the inference itself. Under yesterday's code this run would have produced \"no dispatch-time memories were supplied\" — indistinguishable from a coverage gap. That indistinguishability is how 21 timeouts and 13 store-unavailable results entered this lane's record as apparent retrieval evidence.\n\nSo I recorded metric 3 as **`:NOT-MEASURABLE`, not zero**, with `:metric-3-exclude-from-benchmark true`. A failed recall yields no evidence in either direction, and writing it as a zero is exactly what turned run 24 into a false architectural diagnosis this afternoon. That distinction now lives in the receipt schema rather than in my memory of having learned it.\n\nTwo things worth noting from the run itself. The runner **improved on the route it was handed** — the inherited plan proposed a manual Gamma-recurrence reconciliation and it used the exact even/odd volume API instead. And **arm (b), Zulip, materially paid**: it surfaced the note that PR #19949 added those exact parity formulas, which is what made the route available. Across 26 ledger rows the components arm has carried essentially everything and arXiv has carried a proof zero times, so a literature arm supplying a decisive API pointer is a real payoff data point rather than a footnote.\n\nTwo jobs in flight: **scribe 28** (with an explicit instruction not to mint a recall memory from a failed recall), and a **small follow-up to codex-2** — persist the `:error` that `safe-recall` captures and `offered-evidence` drops, and split `:store-unavailable` from an honest `:recall-error`, since it currently labels *any* caught `Throwable` and asserts an infrastructure cause the code never checked. I invited it to dispute the split; runners may not be able to act on the distinction, and it was right about the tokenizer when I wasn't.The predicate is properly conservative — `:store-unavailable` only on evidenced HTTP (`url`+`status` in ex-data) or specific network exception classes; everything else falls to `:recall-error`, whose runner text makes no infrastructure claim while still prohibiting the terrain inference. Running the gates myself:Accepted. The gap I found an hour ago is closed at the layer that dropped it — `:recall-error-message` is now written into the offered record, capped at 1000 chars.\n\n**And codex-2 didn't dispute the split; it implemented it more conservatively than I specified.** `store-unavailable-error?` classifies as infrastructure *only* on evidenced HTTP (url + status in ex-data) or a specific network exception class. Everything else becomes `:recall-error`. That's conservative in the right direction — it will under-claim infrastructure rather than assert a cause it hasn't evidenced, which is precisely the failing of the original label.\n\nVerified live by me, not on report:\n\n| check | result |\n|---|---|\n| implementation exception → store-unavailable? | **false** ✓ |\n| HTTP-503 ex-data → store-unavailable? | **true** ✓ |\n| `ConnectException` → store-unavailable? | **true** ✓ |\n| message preserved | `\"projection invariant exploded\"` ✓ |\n| packet label | `dispatch-recall-outcome=recall-error` ✓ |\n| claims the store was unavailable | **false** ✓ |\n| forbids terrain-gap inference | **true** ✓ |\n| warrant guard re-mutated | red on both assertions, restored to HEAD ✓ |\n| clj-kondo / tests | 0 warnings; **40 tests, 161 assertions, 0 failures** ✓ |\n\nThird consecutive change from this agent that passed a gate I tried hard to fail.\n\n**The diagnostic chain is now closed end to end, and that's the real result of the evening.** This afternoon a failed recall was indistinguishable from a coverage gap, and I built an architectural diagnosis on top of one — component counts, a whitepaper section, a receipt. Tonight: the failure reaches the runner typed; the runner copies it verbatim and declines the inference (verified live when a01A04's recall failed); the reason distinguishes evidenced infrastructure from unknown error; the exception message is persisted; and the receipt records `:metric-3-status :NOT-MEASURABLE` with `:metric-3-exclude-from-benchmark true`.\n\nEvery layer that let my error happen now reports what it actually knows, rather than relying on me remembering to check.\n\nScribe 28 I completed last turn — 1 draft promoted and reviewed, both of my proposed memories declined and correctly so.\n\nFinal state: **81/135 clean, 146 sorries, 18 problems and 1 construction target closed today**; queue 49 untouched with 3 rows honestly marked blocked; Codex usage ~10%. Nothing in flight. The frozen retrieval benchmark remains the next well-scoped job whenever you want it — it's now the blocking dependency for meter v2, the damage sweep, and the edge-type rent test alike.", :clocked-target "E-memory-topology", :excursion-id "E-memory-topology", :clocked-excursion "E-memory-topology"}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-30T21:35:42.061057530Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-39146ee9d08a08821db3dd8af57f0b77"}}], :count 9, :checked 9, :index-as-of "2026-07-31T04:44:43.940884560Z"}, :proposa