ved lemmas were therefore unreachable from the problems they were built for\" — and a `lean_lib` was added to fix it. **`YoungL2.lean` has the same problem and was missed.** Four declarations, zero sorries, unreachable. Fixing it with a backup and a build test:**The fix works.** `lake build YoungL2` succeeds and `import YoungL2` now resolves — the L² contraction is importable with exactly the signature a94J04 needs. Checking I haven't broken anything else:**a94J04's most valuable output isn't a proof — it's a build defect that made proved work unreachable, and I've fixed it.**\n\nThe runner reported that `YoungL2.lean` holds an axiom-clean `convolution_L2_contraction_of_probability_kernel` but sits \"outside all configured Lake libraries.\" I verified: it's at the repo root, **4 declarations, zero sorries, no `[[lean_lib]]` entry** — so `import YoungL2` failed with \"unknown module prefix.\"\n\n**The lakefile itself documents this exact defect class.** A comment records that `ConstructionTargets` had no `lean_lib` until 2026-07-30, so *\"the proved lemmas were therefore unreachable from the problems they were built for.\"* YoungL2 had the identical defect and was missed by that fix.\n\nFixed and committed (`7958c53`), with verification rather than assumption: `lake build YoungL2` succeeds; `import YoungL2` resolves and `#check` returns the expected signature; a93J06 still elaborates with zero errors; `ConstructionTargets` still builds. Lakefile backed up first.\n\n**This plausibly unblocks three rows** that have all been circling Young-type estimates tonight — a94J04 needs exactly this L² contraction, a95J08 is proving the general finite-p version and can now reference the proved p=2 case, and a94A03's own note says YoungL2 covers only p=2, which is now an *importable* p=2 rather than an inaccessible one.\n\nIt's worth naming the shape: **a proved, axiom-clean lemma was invisible to every problem that needed it, and no gate we run would ever have reported it.** Sorry counts, axiom censuses and statement diffs all pass on unreachable code. The only thing that surfaced it was a runner trying to import it — the same way the pessimistic scaffold notes only surfaced when someone tried the discouraged route.\n\nAlso: a94J04 closed four declarations axiom-clean (6 → 3 sorries), used two memories directly, and corrected a **third** stale note claiming Mathlib lacks something it has.\n\n**State: 45 resolved, 17 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed.** a94J07's result is still unprocessed — I'll take that next.**a94J07 closed — all four declarations axiom-clean, sorries 4 → 0. Thirty-three.** And it did something notable: the declarations are *named* `rouche_*`, but it **replaced the stale Rouché route with a direct Lipschitz perturbation argument** — `cexp` is exp(1)-Lipschitz, z³ is 3-Lipschitz, and exp(1)+3 < 8 so the linear term forces injectivity.**a94J07 closed axiom-clean — thirty-three problems since yesterday morning.** All five declarations clean, zero signature changes.\n\n**The interesting part is how it closed.** The runner abandoned the scaffold's Rouché route entirely for a direct Lipschitz perturbation argument: on the unit disk `cexp` is exp(1)-Lipschitz and z↦z³ is 3-Lipschitz, and since exp(1)+3 < 8 the linear term dominates and forces injectivity.\n\n**The scaffold's *naming* had baked in the route assumption** — `rouche_deriv_bound` and `rouche_f_bound` are now proved without Rouché, and the names still say otherwise. That's a subtler form of the stale-guidance class than the three prose notes tonight: a comment can be corrected, but a **name** can't be without changing the statement. The runner was right to leave them alone.\n\n**And this sharpens the Rouché frontier finding without overturning it.** `rouche-root-count-transfer` remains a genuine Mathlib frontier on three independent confirmations — that stands. But a problem *named* for Rouché just closed without it, so **the frontier blocks the route, not necessarily every scaffold that assumed that route.**\n\nWhether other Rouché-blocked rows have non-Rouché routes available is worth checking — but I've explicitly recorded that as a hypothesis for the morning, **not** a finding. I've over-generalised three times tonight and been corrected each time; one counterexample to \"Rouché-named ⇒ Rouché-needed\" doesn't license re-opening the frontier row.\n\nBoth unprocessed results are now cleared. **State: 46 resolved, 17 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 dispatched.** Chain healthy — one real park awaiting two jobs, heartbeat behind it. Usage 17%.**Fourth instance confirmed — the class is systemic.** The runner found the file's stated obstruction was stale: current Mathlib has `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval.integral_deriv_eq_sub`, `.intervalIntegrable_deriv`, and `Integrable.tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero`. My caution flagged it for verification without asserting it was wrong — and it was wrong. Dispatching first:Decisive — the diff shows **multiple** stale claims removed from the same file: *\"a deep result that is only partially [available]\"* → *\"Current Mathlib gives both the ε-δ definition and the integral representation\"*, plus *\"This bridge is not fully available in Mathlib\"*. Three new declarations, zero changed, zero removed.**Fourth confirmed instance — the stale-pessimistic-note class is systemic.** a95A02's header claimed the AC-to-integral-representation bridge was *\"a deep result only partially available in Mathlib\"* and *\"not fully available.\"* Current Mathlib has all three needed lemmas. Multiple stale claims in one file, all removed.\n\nWith a03J03, a94A03 and a94J04: **four for four — every partial-support note examined tonight was false.**\n\n**The calibration matters more than the finding.** Two hours ago I added a `:scaffold-note-caution` to this row explicitly stating it was *not* a claim the note was wrong — only that the class had been wrong twice and deserved verification. It turned out wrong. Had I marked it stale on suspicion I'd have been right by luck and wrong as a method. Two instances justified asking; four make it a standing check. That's the distinction I've been getting wrong all night in the other direction — over-generalising from one or two cases — and this is the first time I set the threshold correctly in advance.\n\n**The runner also pre-empted the consolidation concern unprompted**, reporting that \"none were relocated through another sorry-bearing declaration.\" That's exactly the metric-integrity failure I found on a01A07 attempt 1 — headline movement with zero genuine discharge — now appearing spontaneously in a runner's self-report rather than being caught by my review.\n\nThree new axiom-clean declarations, headline delta zero, statements unaltered. Both non-component arms genuinely empty: Zulip returned only unrelated Lusin–Souslin material, and an arXiv manifest query for \"Lusin condition N\" returned no rows — recorded so the next attempt doesn't repeat them.\n\n**State: 46 resolved, 16 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 18%. Scribe 35 is owed over six runs and I'll dispatch it next turn.**a94J08 closed — sorries 2 → 0, all four declarations axiom-clean. Thirty-four.** The construction is elegant: `quadraticQuotient f z = if z = 0 then 0 else z²/f z`, whose lower bound gives ‖·‖ ≤ ‖z‖^(1/2); it extends holomorphically at zero, and Cauchy's estimate at radius R forces every derivative to vanish as R → ∞.\n\nDispatching a row and **scribe 35**, which is at six runs:The park missed scribe 35 — its id came back in the same block. Adding a park for it, then verifying a94J08:**a94J08 closed axiom-clean — thirty-four problems since yesterday morning.** All nine declarations in the file clean, 5 added / 0 changed / 0 removed.\n\nThe construction is worth keeping: `quadraticQuotient f z = if z = 0 then 0 else z²/f z`. The assumed lower bound gives ‖·‖ ≤ ‖z‖^(1/2); it extends holomorphically across zero by removab