d maximal inequality**, not in Mathlib", :round 58, :final true, :calls [], :profile :zai}, :session-id "zai-56a267fcd2a046ce85a902b5b1a004f5", :tags [:transcript :turn-round :zai], :at "2026-07-18T22:32:48.540992083Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "zai-1"}, :author "zai-1", :claim-type :step, :id "e-e8167ff0-fcb4-4a0c-b91e-5e15758a7d37"}} {:score -15.30705748019734, :entry #:evidence{:body {:event :turn-round, :turn-id "zai-turn-475effcf-69f7-44a4-81d0-b48901f4c359", :text "Now I understand the full picture. Mathlib's `𝓕` uses the convention `exp(-2πivw * I)`, while the problem uses `exp(ixξ)`. These differ by the convention constant `2π`. The key insight: Mathlib's `𝓕` with `𝐞` uses `2π` in the exponent, while the problem's convention is `e^{ixξ}` (without `2π`).\n\nThe problem statement defines `f̂(ξ) = ∫ f(x) e^{ixξ} dx`. Mathlib's `𝓕 f(w) = ∫ 𝐞(-(v*w)) • f(v) dv = ∫ exp(-2πi*v*w) • f(v) dv`. These are related by the substitution `ξ = -2πw`, but it's not a direct match.\n\nThe cleanest approach: I can define the problem's Fourier transform directly and prove the three parts using Mathlib's tools (DCT, norm integral inequality, Riemann-Lebesgue). For parts (a) and (b), I can adapt Mathlib's `fourierIntegral_continuous` and `norm_fourierIntegral_le_integral_norm`. For part (c) (Riemann-Lebesgue), I need to show the problem's convention also gives decay — this follows from a change of variables or directly from Mathlib's version.\n\nActually, the simplest approach: since the problem says `e^{ixξ}` without `2π`, and Mathlib uses `e^{-2πivw}`, I can define my own `fourierTransform` using `Complex.exp (I * x * ξ)` and prove the three parts. Let me check if Mathlib's continuity and Riemann-Lebesgue proofs are adaptable.\n\nActually, the most faithful and clean approach: the problem uses `e^{ixξ}` convention. I'll define the Fourier transform accordingly and prove:\n- (a) continuity: adapt `fourierIntegral_continuous` — the key is DCT\n- (b) norm bound: trivial from `norm_integral_le_integral_norm`\n- (c) Riemann-Lebesgue: this is the hard part; Mathlib proves it for `e^{-2πivw}`. I need to show it for `e^{ixξ}`.\n\nFor (c), the substitution `ξ = -2πw` turns `∫ f(x) e^{-2πixw} dx` into `∫ f(x) e^{ixξ} dx` with `ξ = -2πw`. As `|w| → ∞`, `|ξ| → ∞`. So `𝓕 f(w) → 0` as `|w| → ∞` implies my Fourier transform → 0 as `|ξ| → ∞`.\n\nBut actually, the Riemann-Lebesgue lemma applies to *any* additive character, including `e^{ixξ}` (without `2π`). Let me check if Mathlib has a more general version:", :round 17, :final false, :calls [{:tool "run_shell", :args "{:command \"cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \\\"tendsto_integral_exp\\\\|cocompact.*tendsto.*integral\\\\|integral.*tendsto.*cocompact\\\\|tendsto.*atTop.*fourier\\\\|zero_at_infty\\\\|vanish.*infinity\\\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Analysis/Fourier/RiemannLebesgueLemma.lean | head -10\"}", :result {:sha256-16 "6dd7d8ef96c16ca5", :chars 894, :preview "{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"exit\":0,\"out\":\"35:- `tendsto_integral_exp_inner_smul_cocompact` : for `V` a finite-dimensional real inner product\\n38:- `tendsto_integral_exp_smul_cocompact` : for `V` a finite-dimensional real vector space (endowed\\n4…[+654 chars]"}}], :profile :zai}, :session-id "zai-f936619bc2e14b9ebab25d06fe657024", :tags [:transcript :turn-round :zai], :at "2026-07-18T10:01:55.479822870Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "zai-5"}, :author "zai-5", :claim-type :step, :id "e-4e78dd06-3904-416f-a440-d4cfd21bfe0b"}} {:score -14.339104284171205, :entry #:evidence{:body {:job-id "invoke-1785418441652-322-1d7aff3b", :row-kind :hard-proof-step, :phase :outcome, :problem "a01A05-gram-schmidt", :runner-model :codex, :outcome {:statement-integrity :intact-target-and-sorry-preserved, :verified-by "claude-9", :arm-not-needed :arxiv, :wontfix-authority "Joe 2026-07-30", :counterexample-verified-by-ground-control true, :refutation-count-for-claude-9 2, :classification :partial-