sult: arm (b), Zulip, supplied the *route* for the first time in fifteen runs.** The thread `Bounds on alternating convergent series` explicitly discusses combining an alternating-series result, a Taylor series and Abel's theorem, and records the development of Mathlib's real Abel theorem — it described the exact three-part composition the runner then implemented. Until now that arm had only ever *corroborated an obstruction* (log-integrability, smooth-vs-analytic, completeness) or returned nothing. The packet assigns it the role \"how has this been formalized before, and what are the known obstacles\"; that role has now paid in the strong sense. I've offered it to the scribe with the n=1 caveat rather than asserting a pattern.\n\n**Fifth consecutive run where LeanSearch returned nothing** while Loogle name-guessing carried the components. The promoted search rule now has **five independent confirmations and still zero instances of being consumed** — I keep both numbers together deliberately, because the rule is a true description of runner behaviour and the memory system still hasn't been shown to cause any of it.\n\n**Session: clean 63 → 71 (eight closed), executable sorries 172 → 157 (fifteen), corpus 51 → 81 memories, patterns 5 → 7.** Queue 60 untouched / 23 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix. Codex usage 6% against the 50% stop.\n\nRun 16 is on a93J04, scribe 21 on a93J02. The loop's rate is now roughly one closed problem per iteration, bounded by my verification turnaround rather than anything else.Run 16 solved — and this is `eLpNorm → essential supremum` terrain, where I believe the corpus **does** hold a memory. Verifying, then probing that:**Clean 71 → 72. Sixth consecutive close.** Now the probe that matters — this is near-identical terrain to a memory we hold:The query recall sent contains **`elpnorm` — lowercased**. Let me test whether the search is case-sensitive, because if so that breaks every Lean identifier:Case is **not** the issue — all three casings return the same 2 memories, so that hypothesis is wrong. It's the conjunction again: `elpnorm` alone finds them, `tendsto elpnorm attop` finds none. But **my ladder should fall back to singles and find them.** Let me test the actual recall path rather than infer:Every tier returns 20 rows — which means **my memory filter is silently falling back to unfiltered results**, and because the first tier is therefore never empty, **the ladder never falls through either.** Let me confirm the row shape:Found it — rows are `{:score _ :entry {...}}`, so the evidence type is nested under `:entry` and my filter read `nil` on every row. Fixing and measuring:**Now it works** — the `tendsto elpnorm` pair tier returns the exact relevant memory as its first result. Committing, and correcting the record on two fixes I reported as working:**Clean 71 → 72. Sixth consecutive close, ninth problem today.** a93J04 via `Lᵖ → L^∞` on a finite measure space.\n\n**But the real result is a bug in my own recall fixes — and it made two of them inert at once.**\n\na93J04 is `eLpNorm`-to-essential-supremum terrain, and the corpus holds a **near-exact memory** for it from pass 11. Recall surfaced nothing. The cause: my receipts-crowding filter read `:evidence/type` at the **top level** of each search row, but rows are `{:score _ :entry {…}}` with the document nested under `:entry`. So it matched nothing every time and fell back to unfiltered results — **and because that fallback left every ladder tier non-empty, the ladder never fell through to the shorter queries it exists to reach.**\n\nI reported both fixes as working. I had measured the *endpoint*, not the *seam*. Fixed and verified by direct probe: the pair tier `tendsto elpnorm` now returns the exact memory **first**, where the whole ladder previously returned 20 unfiltered rows at every tier.\n\n**My first hypothesis was wrong, and I checked it before acting.** Seeing `elpnorm` lowercased, I assumed case-sensitivity — `subjects_for` lowerc