o placeholders, 3 of 5 axiom-clean with `uniform_bound` and `scaled_integral_vanishes` carrying `sorryAx` — the two the runner says are false. And **scribe 32 is done**; its park was consumed. Reading its answer to the vacuity question:**Scribe 32 corrected my headline claim, and it's right.** I wrote \"five problems, one root cause\" into the summary — at the top. It doesn't hold. The evidence separates into four distinct classes:\n\n- **`integral_undef`**: a01A10, a01J01, a02J01 — and it names an earlier one I didn't know about, a95A08\n- **constant placeholder**: a95J03\n- **missing model invariants**: a01J06\n- **pre-formal elaboration failure**: a01J02\n\nI conflated four defect classes under one banner because they all *felt* like \"the corpus is broken.\" Correcting that first:**The scribe corrected my headline claim, and it was right — the tenth time.**\n\nI wrote *\"five problems, one root cause: `integral_undef`\"* and put it at the top of the overnight summary. It doesn't hold. Scribe 32 separated the evidence into four classes:\n\n- **`integral_undef`** — a01A10, a01J01, a02J01, now a02J03, plus an earlier a95A08 I didn't know about\n- **constant placeholder** — a95J03, a scaffold stub with nothing to do with integrals\n- **missing model invariants** — a01J06\n- **pre-formal elaboration failure** — a01J02 and the audit's four\n\n**I conflated four mechanisms because they shared a feeling — \"the corpus is unsound\" — rather than a cause.** The summary is rewritten as a taxonomy.\n\nWorth naming plainly: that's the **second** time tonight I've generalised too fast from a genuine finding. Earlier I diagnosed pattern-tier fragmentation for what was an infrastructure timeout; now a single root cause for what is four. Same shape both times — a real observation over-extended into a class — and both times something else caught it, not me. That looks like my characteristic failure mode rather than two unrelated slips.\n\nThe scribe also answered the vacuity question better than I framed it. I'd explicitly told it *\"no rule, this needs a person\"* was acceptable; instead it gave the more precise answer — placeholder detection is **partly** mechanisable (unfold central definitions, flag constants, unused inputs, `True` fields), while semantic adequacy still needs human review. And it recommended a95J03 be classified `:closed-over-placeholder` to amend the existing placeholder memory rather than mint a new one. It also turned my third question into a **draft**: elaboration preflight is capturable — which is my a02J06 discovery generalised into a rule by an agent that wasn't there when I made it.\n\n**a02J03 verified**: `indicator_bound` axiom-clean, zero signature changes, no placeholders, and exactly the two reported-false targets carrying `sorryAx`. Recorded as `integral_undef` class, **argued not machine-checked**, with the Borel–Cantelli counterexample mechanism and the `LocallyIntegrable φ` repair left for you.\n\nPair 9 is dispatched and parked (dispatch-first held this time). Scribe 32's two trajectory drafts still need promoting and its four amendments applying — I'll take those next.I processed scribe 32's verdicts and corrected the summary last turn. The promotion and amendments are owed. Verifying the drafts and the patterns it names:My URL guess was wrong — `math/missing-dependency-protocol` returns 404 too and I know that one exists, so the 404s are meaningless. Checking how patterns actually resolve:`math-formalization/notation-semantics-traps` is attested (3 prior uses), but **`math/corpus-trust-protocol` appears in no prior promotion** — the scribe listed it as *reused*. Rather than risk auto-creating an eleventh component, I'll place both drafts in attested patterns and flag the discrepancy:Correction to my own scepticism: `corpus-trust-protocol` **is** in the store — my check only grepped promotion scripts, which was too narrow. The scribe was right; I just looked in the wrong place. Both drafts promoted and reviewed. Now its four amendments:Heartbeat count is zero — the previous one fired. Per the rule, arming exactly one:Scribe 32 is fully landed.\n\n**Two trajectory drafts promoted and reviewed** into attested patterns — the first trajectory-lane drafts of the night, and both are process rules extracted from *my* operational discoveries by an agent that wasn't present for them: elaboration preflight (from the a02J06 unbuilt-dependency case) and model-invariant requirements (from a01J06).\n\n**One correction to my own scepticism.** I doubted the scribe's `math/corpus-trust-protocol` handle, grepped the promotion scripts, found nothing, and was ready to record a discrepancy. The handle *is* in the store — I'd only searched promote-script history, a strict subset. Eleventh time I've been wrong to doubt it, and this time the fault was in my verification method rather than my judgement. Caught before it went anywhere durable.\n\n**Four more amendments applied — thirteen tonight**, all append-only with proposer ≠ applier:\n\n- **The Bochner memory broadened from a technique into a diagnostic**, carrying all six problems with their distinct counterexample mechanisms — and the strongest form of the check, from a02J07: *look at what the applicable Mathlib API requires. When every route demands a hypothesis the scaffold omits, that's a statement defect, not a proof gap.*\n- **a95J03 added to the placeholder memory** as a closed-over-placeholder case that is counted clean.\n- **A `:semantic-status` axis** for the proof-hole meter — all its existing quantities are syntactic and none separates a real close from a vacuous one.\n- **The four-class taxonomy**, with the point that the classes need *different* responses: premise repair, scaffold implementation, structure redesign, build fix.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean (86 genuine + 1 disputed), 126 sorries** — down from 172 this morning. Queue: 31 untouched, 37 resolved, **13 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes**, 2 in flight, 4