failure. But it's a useful wake: checking whether any real park survives.**Zero live parks — the chain had broken**, and the fabricated park's deadline is the only reason I'm awake. My mistake accidentally functioned as the safety net. That's worth making deliberate rather than accidental. Selecting the next pair first, as its own step:Three rows are now unreachable on stale hints — the pre-dispatch check correctly refuses them, but they'll accumulate. The refresher only targets rows with `:repaired-at` or `:last-commit`, and these have neither. Minimal fix — honour explicit ids:That wake was the fabricated `SCRIBE29` park firing — and it turned out to matter.\n\n**The chain had stopped.** There were zero live parks: `park-3e34db0c` was consumed when both pair-3 jobs finished, I processed them, and I ended that turn without dispatching or re-parking. The loop was dead. **The only thing that woke me was the park I fabricated by mistake** and logged as harmless noise.\n\nSo I've made it deliberate. `park-2c25905f` is now a **heartbeat**: it awaits a deliberately non-existent job so its deadline always fires (~58 min), with a payload telling the waking turn to check whether a real park exists and, if not, restart the loop from the RESUME doc. The accidental version worked; this one has instructions instead of \"placeholder\". The invariant \"every turn must park\" isn't enforceable by good intentions — this makes a missed park recoverable rather than fatal.\n\n**Second fix: three rows had become permanently undispatchable.** Their hints were stale, so the pre-dispatch assertion refused them — correctly — but the refresher couldn't reach them, because it only auto-selects rows the queue *knows* changed. A hint also goes stale for reasons never recorded (a Mathlib bump, work outside this lane). Now an explicitly named row is always refreshed: a02J05 and a01A11 are unstuck, dispatchable rows back to 43 of 45, and I regression-checked that the no-args default still selects