t, then process.** The failure was identical every time: awaited jobs complete, the completion consumes the park, the turn does its long processing work, and ends without dispatching. Now on every wake I select, pre-check, dispatch, read the ids back, park — and *only then* verify and receipt. Dispatch is short and chain-preserving; processing is long and interruptible. That makes the gap structurally impossible rather than something to remember, which is the same move as the recall banner and the heartbeat itself.\n\nApplied it this turn: **a02J02 and a02J03 were dispatched before I touched anything else**, plus scribe pass 32.\n\nI did make the batching error again in a milder form — composed the park payload before dispatching the scribe, so its id wasn't in the `awaiting` list. Caught it on read-back and added a second park. Same root cause as the two fabricated ids: composing a record before the thing it references exists. That corollary now sits next to the dispatch-first rule rather than buried.\n\n**The summary is restructured**, with the vacuity finding promoted to the top under \"READ THIS FIRST\" — because a problem counted clean that proves nothing outranks any of the closes. The junk-value tally is corrected from three problems to **five problems and seven targets**, with a per-problem proved/argued column, since filing an argued defect alongside a machine-checked one would corrupt exactly the evidence base the receipts exist to keep honest.\n\nOne line I added and believe: **five runners independently declined to silently repair a statement** — refusing to pick a repair, refusing to choose between two, refusing to weaken, refuting rather than patching, diagnosing without touching the file. None was told to. That's the strongest evidence tonight that the review discipline has transferred to the runners themselves.\n\nScribe 32 has the sharpest question of the night turned back on it: **is a vacuity check capturable at all, or is it irreducibly human judgement?** I told it explicitly that \"no rule, this needs a person\" is a good answer and not to invent an unfalsifiable checklist to please me.\n\nState: **87/135 clean (86 genuine + 1 disputed), 130 sorries, 24 closed**, 34 untouched, usage 13%, store 6.91 GB.Dispatching first, per the rule — a02J03 is the **sixth** junk-value problem and this pushes the class to nine targets:Chain armed (1 real park, 1 heartbeat with 337s — so no new heartbeat per the rule). But scribe 32's park is gone from the list. Checking it, and verifying a02J03:a02J03 verified: **zero signature changes**, no placeholders, 3 of 5 axiom-clean with `uniform_bound` and `scaled_integral_vanishes` carrying `sorryAx` — the two the runner says are false. And **scribe 32 is done**; its park was consumed. Reading its answer to the vacuity question:**Scribe 32 corrected my headline claim, and it's right.** I wrote \"five problems, one root cause\" into the summary — at the top. It doesn't hold. The evidence separates into four distinct classes:\n\n- **`integral_undef`**: a01A10, a01J01, a02J01 — and it names an earlier one I didn't know about, a95A08\n- **constant placeholder**: a95J03\n- **missing model invariants**: a01J06\n- **pre-formal elaboration failure**: a01J02\n\nI conflated four defect classes under one banner because they all *felt* like \"the corpus is broken.\" Correcting that first:**The scribe corrected my headline claim, and it was right — the tenth time.**\n\nI wrote *\"five problems, one root cause: `integral_undef`\"* and put it at the top of the overnight summary. It doesn't hold. Scribe 32 separated the evidence into four classes:\n\n- **`integral_undef`** — a01A10, a01J01, a02J01, now a02J03, plus an earlier a95A08 I didn't know about\n- **constant placeholder** — a95J03, a scaffold stub with nothing to do with integrals\n- **missing model invariants** — a01J06\n- **pre-formal elaboration failure** — a01J02 and the audit's four\n\n**I conflated four mechanisms because they shared a feeling — \"the corpus is unsound\" — rather than a cause.** The summary is rewritten as a taxonomy.\n\nWorth naming plainly: that's the **second** time tonight I've generalised too fast from a genuine finding. Earlier I diagnosed pattern-tier fragmentation for what was an infrastructure timeout; now a single root cause for what is four. Same shape both times — a real observation over-extended into a class — and both times something else caught it, not me. That looks like my characteristic failure mode rather than two unrelated slips.\n\nThe scribe also answered the vacuity question better than I framed it. I'd explicitly told it *\"no rule, this needs a person\"* was acceptable; instead it gave the more precise answer — placeholder detection is **partly** mechanisable (unfold central definitions, flag constants, unused inputs, `True` fields), while semantic adequacy still needs human review. And it recommended a95J03 be classified `:closed-over-placeholder` to amend the existing placeholder memory rather than mint a new one. It also turned my third question into a **draft**: elaboration preflight is capturable — which is my a02J06 discovery generalised into a rule by an agent that wasn't there when I made it.\n\n**a02J03 verified**: `indicator_bound` axiom-clean, zero signature changes, no placeholders, and exactly the two reported-false targets carrying `sorryAx`. Recorded as `integral_undef` class, **argued not machine-checked**, with the Borel–Cantelli counterexample mechanism and the `LocallyIntegrable φ` repair left for you.\n\nPair 9 is dispatched and parked (dispatch-first held this time). Scribe 32's two trajectory drafts still need promoting and its four amendments applying — I'll take those next.I processed scribe 32's verdicts and corrected the summary last turn. The promotion and amendments are owed. Verifying the drafts and the patterns it names:My URL guess was wrong — `math/missing-dependency-protocol` returns 404 too and I know that one exists,