ne sorry so the problem didn't close.\n\n**The headline is that my highest-risk repair was vindicated.** a01A06 was the one row where I *invented* the mathematics rather than translating it — the informal solution claimed entropy control gives exponential tails (it doesn't) and that `g` must be in L² (it needn't be). I restated part (a) as a polynomial tail bound and part (b) as the counterexample the exam actually asks for. The runner proved **three of four axiom-clean**: `distribution_polynomial_decay` by Markov, `test_function_admissible` by explicit indicator integration, `exponential_decay_implies_L2` by Cavalieri layer-cake. So the restatement wasn't merely true but *provable*, and the deliberately conservative choice — polynomial rather than a sharp entropy bound — was right.\n\n**Repair scoreboard across seven worked rows: five validated in the field, one refuted→corrected→proved, one refuted→WONTFIX.** Both refutations were caught by runners. I'd been treating my error rate as a process risk needing a new gate; measured, the existing gate held every time — which is what you said.\n\n**The compounding test failed a fifth time, but for a new and more fundamental reason: coverage, not plumbing.** The receipts-crowding fix was live and recall still surfaced nothing — so I probed rather than assumed, and `\"Markov inequality\"`, `\"Orlicz\"`, `\"distribution polynomial decay\"` and `\"layer cake exponential tail\"` each return **zero** memories from the 66-memory corpus. Same for a01A03's convolution terrain. **The corpus doesn't yet cover the terrains being worked.** That changes what would fix it: not another seam repair, but more memories in more terrains — which is exactly what the loop produces. Four plumbing faults are behind us; compounding now waits on coverage.\n\nTwo more of my own bugs, both caught by guards I'd added after earlier instances: the duplicate-key assertion fired *before* writing (I was inserting `:priority` rather than setting it, again)