er a93J07 disproved my first vacuity heuristic, I corrected the rule to *\"test the conclusion, not the hypotheses.\"* Then I implemented a scan that checks `def X := 0` and `(h : True)` binders — **subjects and hypotheses, never the conclusion itself.** The rule I wrote would have caught this immediately; the tooling I wrote could not. It's now step 0 of the preflight, ahead of everything else.\n\nWorth stating plainly why this form is the worst one: **a `True` hypothesis makes a theorem unconstrained but still falsifiable** — a01J06 and a95J04 were both *refuted* through one. **A `True` conclusion makes it unfalsifiable and closable**, and the sorry metric actively rewards closing it. Every gate we run passes on `theorem foo ... : True := trivial`.\n\nThe file documents its own blocker honestly — *\"this fundamental theorem is NOT in Mathlib... requires Banach–Zarecki\"* — so the stubs look deliberate. **That makes it more dangerous, not less: the prose says \"blocked\" while the Lean says \"provable\", and only the Lean is checked.**\n\na95J06's two genuine declarations did close axiom-clean (Cantor-set nullity by self-similar contraction, and continuous-injection monotonicity).\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 8 untouched, 21 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.[curl error (exit 28): {\"type\":\"started\"}\n{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"a96A01: three declarations axiom-clean, sorries 4 → 2. And the memory used was **again for interface normalisation** — translating `quasiNorm` through `lpNorm`]", :clocked-target "E-memory-topology", :excursion-id "E-memory-topology", :clocked-excursion "E-memory-topology"}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-31T04:26:21.365648175Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-97988bfc580bbf9cac51e01e5717cd0a"}}], :count 21, :checked 21, :index-as-o