he remaining sorry is now formally confined to 1 < p < ∞**. The frontier is narrower than I stated it. Dispatching first:Both runners busy, so capacity is full — and the selector's next pick is a95J06, the row I just flagged predictively. Now executing the pre-committed decision:Confirmed precisely: `by_cases hpeq : p = 1` discharges the p=1 branch with the proved lemma, and the sorry sits in the else branch — **formally confined to 1 < p < ∞**. Executing the pre-committed decision:**The pre-committed frontier decision fired, and I honoured it as written.** a95J08 → `:blocked-mathlib-frontier` after three independent failed attempts. The evidence quality is unusually good for such a call: three failures, a Zulip thread stating explicitly that Young's inequality and convolution infrastructure are *missing* from Mathlib, a literature anchor verified against the local manifest (Soudský 1704.08922 — scaffold, no Lean), and a precise statement of what's missing. The runner reached the same conclusion independently and labelled its own outcome the same way.\n\n**But the frontier is narrower than I'd stated it, and recording it wholesale would have hidden a proved branch.** The runner proved `young_convolution_inequality_one` axiom-clean and wired it in via `by_cases hpeq : p = 1`. I read the proof term to confirm: the p=1 branch discharges from the proved lemma, and the sorry sits in the else branch — **formally confined to 1 < p < ∞**. So the frontier is the weighted Hölder/Jensen estimate `(∫w·v)^p ≤ (∫w)^(p−1)·∫w·v^p` plus Tonelli assembly, not the lemma.\n\n**I checked myself against loosening the bar rather than just applying it.** Two problems tonight — a93A04 and a95A02 — had named gaps *constructed* on a later attempt, which is genuine evidence that \"missing\" often means \"unassembled\". But rouche went three and stayed blocked, and the alternative to a three-attempt rule is re-dispatching a confirmed gap forever.\n\nThat's the case for pre-committing at all: **the moment of decision is exactly when the reasons to bend the rule are most available.** I had two fresh counterexamples in hand and a plausible story for why this one might be different. Writing the rule down an hour earlier, when I had neither, is what made it hold.\n\na94A03's dependency is sharpened again — it needs general finite p so it stays blocked, but the row now notes that if its targets reduce to p=1, or if YoungL2's now-importable p=2 contraction suffices, it may be tractable *without* the frontier. Recorded as untested.\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 10 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed (2 now confirmed frontiers), 4 in flight.** Usage 19%.**a95J05 closed — sorries 2 → 0, all five declarations axiom-clean. Thirty-seven.** And metric 3 is measurable with **two memories used**. Dispatching a95J06 next — the row I flagged predictively for `(hf_ac_compl : True)`, which will test whether that caution helps:**The predictive caution did not reach the packet.** `:placeholder-hypothesis-caution` is a custom field the template doesn't render — so my flag is in the queue and invisible to the runner. Same class as the truncated route earlier. Checking what the template actually renders:**a95J05 closed axiom-clean — thirty-seven problems since yesterday morning.** The decisive move was bridging the file's *strict*-threshold `TendstoInMeasureZero` with Mathlib's *closed*-threshold `TendstoInMeasure` — a definitional mismatch that blocked every Mathlib API until resolved.\n\n**But I have to flag a failure of mine from this turn, because it undercuts the win I claimed an hour ago.**\n\nI said I'd finally got ahead of a defect by flagging a95J06's `(hf_ac_compl : True)` before dispatch. **The caution never reached the runner.** I recorded it as `:placeholder-hypothesis-caution`, a custom row key — and the packet template renders only eight fields. a95J06's runner is working right now with no warning at all.\n\nI found this only because I checked *after* dispatching. I've moved the caution into `:suggested-route`, verified it renders by instantiating the packet and grepping, and added the rule to the RESUME doc: **a queue field is not a communication channel.**\n\nThat's the **second time tonight** a carefully-written caution failed to reach a packet — the first was the duplicate-key truncation that dropped a01A07's `sorryAx` warning mid-string. Different mechanism, same result. **Writing the warning is the easy half; delivering it is the half that keeps failing**, and both times I only noticed by accident rather than by checking.\n\nOn the memory system, a pattern worth naming: **both memories used here addressed definitional mismatch rather than proof strategy** — how to connect the file's own predicates to Mathlib's. Across tonight, the corpus has been consumed far more for **interface translation** than for mathematics. The memories that carried proofs (a01A07's frontier pair, LemniscateComponents) are the minority.\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 9 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.a95A02's fourth attempt adds `ac_volume_image_subset_Icc_le_integral_norm_deriv` axiom-clean — the image-measure step, proved **without any measurability assumption on S or f''S** via `Real.volume_le_diam`. Both targets still open, but this exposes a refinement to my frontier rule. Dispatching first:**a95A02's fourth attempt sharpened my frontier rule, and the sharpening is the real result of this turn.**\n\nThe rule was \"three independent failed attempts justifies marking a frontier.\" That's wrong as stated. **Count attempts that fail at the same point, not attempts total.**\n\na95J08 was marked a frontier an hour ago on three attempts that all hit the *same* weighted Hölder/Jensen estimate — a genuine wall. a95A02 now has **four** attempts and is emphatically not a frontier, because each advanced a *different* piece: three AC lemmas, then the deduction plus part-(c) diagnosis, then the open-set decomposition it had itself reported missing, then this image-measure step. Four attempts, four pieces, sixteen declarations — a long **assembly**, not a wall. Marking it on attempt count alone would have been exactly wrong, and an hour ago my rule couldn't have told the two apart.\n\nThe new lemma is nicely scoped too: it bounds volume(f(S)) by the integral of ‖deriv f‖ **with no measurability assumption on S or f''S**, via `Real.volume_le_diam` — outer measure needs no measurability. That removes the separate image-diameter argument the plan had anticipated.\n\nTwo things worth flagging for the morning. **Part (c) was searched specifically this run and remains unresolved in both directions** — only the general Lusin–Souslin result under injectivity surfaced, no AC-specific Borel-image theorem *and* no counterexample. That's more useful than recording it as \"blocked\", because it tells you the decision is genuinely open rather than un-investigated. And both non-component arms were empty: an arXiv manifest query for three spellings of \"Lusin condition (N)\" returned **no records at all**.\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 9 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.a95J06 closed both requested declarations — but **it did not touch the `(hf_ac_compl : True)` declarations**, so my predictive flag is neither confirmed nor refuted; the runner simply worked elsewhere. Checking whether the remaining targets bind it:**This is more serious than the hypothesis case.** `ac_of_bv_continuous_conditionN`'s **conclusion** is `∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, True` — the conclusion itself is trivially provable. A runner could \"close\" it with `fun ε _ => ⟨1, one_pos, trivial⟩`: sorry count drops, axioms clean, **nothing proved**. That's the a95J03 vacuity waiting to happen. Scanning the corpus for this form:**Both** remaining sorried declarations in a95J06 have trivially-true conclusions — one is literally `True`. A runner could close both, the count would go 2 → 0, the problem would be marked **CLEAN**, and nothing would be proved. **That's a95J03 about to happen again, caught before dispatch.** Blocking the row:**This is the most valuable catch of the session, and it's predictive.**\n\na95J06's two remaining targets have trivially-true conclusions: `ac_of_bv_continuous_conditionN` concludes `∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, True`, and `ac_on_01_of_homeo_fixing_cantor` concludes **literally `True`** while binding a no-op `(hf_ac_compl : True)`.\n\n**A runner could close both with `trivial`** — sorry count 2 → 0, axioms clean, problem marked CLEAN, nothing proved. That's exactly the a95J03 failure, which sits in this corpus counted clean and proving nothing. The row is now `:blocked-vacuous-targets` before any runner reached them.\n\n**And the failure that let this sit undetected is mine, in a specific and instructive way.** After a93J07 disproved my first vacuity heuristic, I corrected the rule to *\"test the conclusion, not the hypotheses.\"* Then I implemented a scan that checks `def X := 0` and `(h : True)` binders — **subjects and hypotheses, never the conclusion itself.** The rule I wrote would have caught this immediately; the tooling I wrote could not. It's now step 0 of the preflight, ahead of everything else.\n\nWorth stating plainly why this form is the worst one: **a `True` hypothesis makes a theorem unconstrained but still falsifiable** — a01J06 and a95J04 were both *refuted* through one. **A `True` conclusion makes it unfalsifiable and closable**, and the sorry metric actively rewards closing it. Every gate we run passes on `theorem foo ... : True := trivial`.\n\nThe file documents its own blocker honestly — *\"this fundamental theorem is NOT in Mathlib... requires Banach–Zarecki\"* — so the stubs look deliberate. **That makes it more dangerous, not less: the prose says \"blocked\" while the Lean says \"provable\", and only the Lean is checked.**\n\na95J06's two genuine declarations did close axiom-clean (Cantor-set nullity by self-similar contraction, and continuous-injection monotonicity).\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 8 untouched, 21 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.[curl error (exit 28): {\"type\":\"started\"}\n{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"a96A01: three declarations axiom-clean, sorries 4 → 2. And the memory used was **again for interface normalisation** — translating `quasiNorm` through `lpNorm`]", :clocked-target "E-memory-topology", :excursion-id "E-memory-topology", :clocked-excursion "E-memory-topology"}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-31T04:26:21.365648175Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-97988bfc580bbf9cac51e01e5717cd0a"}}], :count 13, :checked 13, :index-as-of "2026-07-31T04:44:43.940884560Z"}, :proposal-rows [{:score -35.51200005119243, :entry #:evidence{:body {:hook "To prove an `Lᵖ*Lᑫ` convolution vanishes at infinity, approximate both factors so the core has literal compact support while translation-invariant Hölder bounds control the approximation error uniformly.", :scribe-author "codex-5", :draft-source "holes/labs/M-codex-sorry-loop/", :name "prove-Holder-convolution-vanishes-at-infinity-by-compact-support-density", :runner-model :codex, :kind :feedback, :how-to-apply "To prove an `Lᵖ*Lᑫ` convolution vanishes at infinity, approximate both factors so the core has literal compact support while translation-invariant Hölder bounds control the approximation error uniformly.", :body {:boundary "The proof uses conjugate exponents and the translation-invariant Lebesgue measure on `ℝ`; additional work is needed for non-unimodular groups or non-invariant measures.", :capture-kind :runner-executed-carried-route-and-owner-full-file-verification, :rule "To prove an `Lᵖ*Lᑫ` convolution vanishes at infinity, approximate both factors so the core has literal compact support while translation-invariant Hölder bounds control the 