nner was right to leave them alone.\n\n**And this sharpens the Rouché frontier finding without overturning it.** `rouche-root-count-transfer` remains a genuine Mathlib frontier on three independent confirmations — that stands. But a problem *named* for Rouché just closed without it, so **the frontier blocks the route, not necessarily every scaffold that assumed that route.**\n\nWhether other Rouché-blocked rows have non-Rouché routes available is worth checking — but I've explicitly recorded that as a hypothesis for the morning, **not** a finding. I've over-generalised three times tonight and been corrected each time; one counterexample to \"Rouché-named ⇒ Rouché-needed\" doesn't license re-opening the frontier row.\n\nBoth unprocessed results are now cleared. **State: 46 resolved, 17 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 dispatched.** Chain healthy — one real park awaiting two jobs, heartbeat behind it. Usage 17%.**Fourth instance confirmed — the class is systemic.** The runner found the file's stated obstruction was stale: current Mathlib has `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval.integral_deriv_eq_sub`, `.intervalIntegrable_deriv`, and `Integrable.tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero`. My caution flagged it for verification without asserting it was wrong — and it was wrong. Dispatching first:Decisive — the diff shows **multiple** stale claims removed from the same file: *\"a deep result that is only partially [available]\"* → *\"Current Mathlib gives both the ε-δ definition and the integral representation\"*, plus *\"This bridge is not fully available in Mathlib\"*. Three new declarations, zero changed, zero removed.**Fourth confirmed instance — the stale-pessimistic-note class is systemic.** a95A02's header claimed the AC-to-integral-representation bridge was *\"a deep result only partially available in Mathlib\"* and *\"not fully available.\"* Current Mathlib has all three needed lemmas. Multiple stale claims in one file, all removed.\n\nWith a03J03, a94A03 and a94J04: **four for four — every partial-support note examined tonight was false.**\n\n**The calibration matters more than the finding.** Two hours ago I added a `:scaffold-note-caution` to this row explicitly stating it was *not* a claim the note was wrong — only that the class had been wrong twice and deserved verification. It turned out wrong. Had I marked it stale on suspicion I'd have been right by luck and wrong as a method. Two instances justified asking; four make it a standing check. That's the distinction I've been getting wrong all night in the other direction — over-generalising from one or two cases — and this is the first time I set the threshold correctly in advance.\n\n**The runner also pre-empted the consolidation concern unprompted**, reporting that \"none were relocated through another sorry-bearing declaration.\" That's exactly the metric-integrity failure I found on a01A07 attempt 1 — headline movement with zero genuine discharge — now appearing spontaneously in a runner's self-report rather than being caught by my review.\n\nThree new axiom-clean declarations, headline delta zero, statements unaltered. Both non-component arms genuinely empty: Zulip returned only unrelated Lusin–Souslin material, and an arXiv manifest query for \"Lusin condition N\" returned no rows — recorded so the next attempt doesn't repeat them.\n\n**State: 46 resolved, 16 untouched, 17 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 18%. Scribe 35 is owed over six runs and I'll dispatch it next turn.**a94J08 closed — sorries 2 → 0, all four declarations axiom-clean. Thirty-four.** The construction is elegant: `quadraticQuotient f z = if z = 0 then 0 else z²/f z`, whose lower bound gives ‖·‖ ≤ ‖z‖^(1/2); it extends holomorphically at zero, and Cauchy's estimate at radius R forces every derivative to vanish as R → ∞.\n\nDispatching a row and **scribe 35**, which is at six runs:The park missed scribe 35 — its id came back in the same block. Adding a park for it, then verifying a94J08:**a94J08