Per the rule, arming exactly one:Scribe 32 is fully landed.\n\n**Two trajectory drafts promoted and reviewed** into attested patterns — the first trajectory-lane drafts of the night, and both are process rules extracted from *my* operational discoveries by an agent that wasn't present for them: elaboration preflight (from the a02J06 unbuilt-dependency case) and model-invariant requirements (from a01J06).\n\n**One correction to my own scepticism.** I doubted the scribe's `math/corpus-trust-protocol` handle, grepped the promotion scripts, found nothing, and was ready to record a discrepancy. The handle *is* in the store — I'd only searched promote-script history, a strict subset. Eleventh time I've been wrong to doubt it, and this time the fault was in my verification method rather than my judgement. Caught before it went anywhere durable.\n\n**Four more amendments applied — thirteen tonight**, all append-only with proposer ≠ applier:\n\n- **The Bochner memory broadened from a technique into a diagnostic**, carrying all six problems with their distinct counterexample mechanisms — and the strongest form of the check, from a02J07: *look at what the applicable Mathlib API requires. When every route demands a hypothesis the scaffold omits, that's a statement defect, not a proof gap.*\n- **a95J03 added to the placeholder memory** as a closed-over-placeholder case that is counted clean.\n- **A `:semantic-status` axis** for the proof-hole meter — all its existing quantities are syntactic and none separates a real close from a vacuous one.\n- **The four-class taxonomy**, with the point that the classes need *different* responses: premise repair, scaffold implementation, structure redesign, build fix.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean (86 genuine + 1 disputed), 126 sorries** — down from 172 this morning. Queue: 31 untouched, 37 resolved, **13 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes**, 2 in flight, 4 held-out, 2 wontfix.\n\nChain armed: 1 real park (a02J02 attempt 2), 1 heartbeat. Usage 14%.Chain healthy — a02J02's second attempt is running on codex-7, heartbeat already armed (count 1, so no new one per the rule). But **codex-6 is idle and only one proving run is in flight**, so this wake is best spent filling the empty slot:**First quiet wake of the night** — the chain was healthy. a02J02's second attempt running on codex-7, real park live, heartbeat already armed (count 1, so no new one per the rule). The dead-man switch works in both directions: it caught three stopped chains and now correctly reports a running one.\n\n**But I used the wake rather than treating it as a no-op.** Only *one* proving run was in flight while codex-6 sat idle — the pairing had drifted to a single because a02J02 was re-dispatched alone after its partner finished. So I selected a03J03 (pre-checked, distinct file from the in-flight a02J02), dispatched it, and parked. Both runners are busy again.\n\nThat's a small thing but worth naming: **a wake that finds the chain healthy is an opportunity to check *capacity*, not just liveness.** The heartbeat's payload asked \"is the chain alive?\" — the better question is \"is the chain alive *and* fully loaded?\" I've been watching for the loop stopping and not for it running at half rate.\n\nThe park payload now also carries the **four-class taxonomy** as a receipt requirement, so any statement defect the next run finds gets classified at the point of recording rather than lumped. That's the concrete residue of scribe 32 correcting my single-root-cause claim an hour ago — the correction is now enforced in the workflow, not just noted in a document.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean, 126 sorries** (from 63/172 yesterday morning), 31 untouched, 13 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes, usage 14%, store 6.9 GB.\n\nTwo runners busy, two real parks, one heartbeat.**a02J02 closed — `radon_riesz` proved axiom-clean.** That's twenty-five. And the runner improved on my five-step continuation: applying Vitali directly to `fₙ` avoids separately treating `|fₙ−g|^p`.\n\nDispatching first, since codex-7 is now free:**a02J02 closed axiom-clean — twenty-five problems since yesterday morning.** All seven declarations re-elaborate clean with zero `sorryAx`, 3 added / 0 changed / 0 removed, and the placeholder scan is clean, so this isn't a vacuous close.\n\n**The most interesting thing is that my carried-forward route was right about the shape and wrong about one step — and the runner corrected it rather than following it.** Attempt 1 left a five-step Scheffé/Vitali continuation specifying that uniform integrability be transferred to |fₙ − g|^p via the committed `rpow_sub_le`. The runner confirmed the architecture but found a shorter final step: apply Vitali directly to fₙ and skip |fₙ − g|^p entirely.\n\nSo a lemma I'd recorded as a load-bearing prerequisite turned out to be unnecessary for the closing argument. It's still correct and available — but it's a useful caution about how I write these routes. A route that names specific intermediate lemmas can over-constrain; the architecture was the valuable part, and the runner was right to treat the step list as advisory. That's the fourth time tonight a runner has bettered a supplied route.\n\n**Carried-forward negative results saved a search arm for the second time** — arm (b) wasn't rerun because the row recorded attempt 1's Zulip findings. Recording what was *not* found keeps paying, and scribe 31's insistence that it be scoped as a revision-dependent **cache** rather than a fact is what makes it safe to keep doing.\n\nIts sharpest discrimination: `e-afb1eae8` was reviewed as an *analogous* Kadec–Klee strategy and declined as not applicable to general Lp. That's a judgement on **generality**, not relevance — the right kind of thing at the wrong level of abstraction, and it said so precisely.\n\nDispatch-first held again: a92J06 went out before I processed anything, so both runners are loaded (a03J03 and a92J06), with three real parks and a heartbeat.\n\n**State: 87 clean → will recount when nothing is in flight; queue 29 untouched, 38 resolved, 13 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 dispatched.** Scribe 33 is owed, covering a02J02, a02J03 and a02J07.Dispatching first — codex-6 is now free:Verified: 1 declaration added, zero changed, zero removed, no placeholders, 2 of 3 axiom-clean. And the deleted lines are worth noting — the scaffold's inherited notes claimed **\"Translation continuity: `MeasureTheory.Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"** and **\"C_c approximation in Lp: partial support\"**, both of which were wrong; Mathlib has full support via `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp`.**a03J03 turned up a defect class I hadn't seen — and it's the one my whole verification apparatus is blind to.**\n\nIts scaffold notes claimed *\"Translation continuity: `MeasureTheory.Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"* and *\"C_c approximation in Lp: partial support\"*. Both wrong. Mathlib supports translation continuity **fully** via `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp` — which is exactly what carried the proof, composed with the bundled `ContinuousLinearMap.lpPairing`. The runner deleted the stale claim and replaced it.\n\n**Every other scaffold defect tonight overstated what was proved** — placeholders, vacuous hypotheses, false targets. **This one understated what was available, and its cost is invisible.** A runner that believed the note would avoid a viable route and either fail or rebuild machinery Mathlib already has. No proof-checking gate can catch it, because nothing is wrong with the proofs.\n\nThat's worth sitting with: sorry counts, axiom census, signature diffs, the placeholder scan I added tonight — all of it is oriented toward catching claims that are **too strong**. This is a claim that was **too weak**, and the only thing that surfaced it was a runner trying the route anyway and finding the note false. I have no systematic check for that, and I'm not sure one exists short of attempting the work.\n\nVerification otherwise clean: 2 declarations newly axiom-clean, 1 added / 0 changed / 0 removed, placeholder scan clean, headline and genuine deltas in agreement.\n\nThe row went back with the correction stated **first** in the route — \"the old notes were wrong in the pessimistic direction; do not trust the partial-support framing\" — plus the two now-available supports, the exact remaining gap (two-factor compact-support approximation and eventual-disjoint-support assembly, which Mathlib doesn't package), and negative search results scoped to the current revision.\n\nDispatch-first held again; both runners loaded with a92J06 and a92J07. Queue: 29 untouched, 38 resolved, 13 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight. Scribe 33 owed, now covering four runs.a92J07 found the **same junk-value shape via a different operation** — not `integral_undef` but `deriv` totalisation: Lean's `deriv` is zero wherever differentiability fails, so a nowhere-differentiable Weierstrass function satisfies all four Cauchy–Riemann hypotheses vacuously. Dispatching first:Confirmed precisely. The hypotheses are **only `Continuous`** — no differentiability anywhere — while `hcr1`/`hcr2` constrain `deriv`. With u = 2 + W(x), v = W(y) for a bounded nowhere-differentiable W with |W| < 1: all four `deriv` terms are 0, `hray` holds since u > 1, and the conclusion \"u, v constant\" is false. Signature diff shows **zero changes**.The docstring already documents the defect precisely. Critical question — did the runner add that, or was it pre-existing and I dispatched a row whose file already said the target was unprovable?The docstring is **new** — the runner added it (all `+` lines, zero occurrences in the parent). So it documented the defect in the file rather than weakening the theorem. No queue-integrity issue on my side.**a92J07 found the junk-value shape via a different operation, and that changes the class.**\n\nEvery instance tonight was `integral_undef`. This one is **`deriv` totalisation**: Lean's `deriv` is zero wherever differentiability fails. `cauchy_riemann_ray_implies_const` assumes only `Continuous` on both components — no differentiability at all — while `hcr1`/`hcr2` constrain `deriv` values. I read the hypothesis list myself and confirmed it's Continuous-only. So a bounded nowhere-differentiable W with |W| < 1, with u = 2 + W(x) and v = W(y), zeroes all four `deriv` terms, satisfies `hray` since u > 1, and leaves both components nonconstant.\n\n**So the class isn't \"integral_undef\" — it's \"junk-value totalisation of a partial operation,\" with at least two operations. But I'm not generalising it myself.** I recorded `:defect-operation :deriv` alongside the class and flagged it for the scribe to judge whether that's one class or two. Scribe 32 corrected me three hours ago for lumping four mechanisms under one cause; the lesson isn't \"never generalise,\" it's \"hand the datum to the reviewer rather than banking the generalisation.\"\n\nA sharp secondary diagnostic worth keeping: **the prose says u and v are C¹ while the formal hypotheses assert only continuity.** A prose/formalisation mismatch on a differentiability hypothesis is exactly where totalisation bites — and it's cheap to check on any statement whose hypotheses mention derivatives or integrals.\n\n`entire_avoids_ray_const` closed axiom-clean (slit-plane logarithm, bounded `exp(I·log f)`, Liouville, `is_const_of_deriv_eq_zero`), with zero signature changes.\n\nI also checked something that would have been my fault rather than the corpus's: the runner added a docstring documenting the defect, and **I verified it's new** — all additions, zero occurrences in the parent — because if it had been pre-existing, I'd have dispatched a row whose own file already said the target was unprovable. It wasn't. Seventh run tonight to refuse a silent repair.\n\nBoth runners loaded (a92J06, a03J03 attempt 2). Queue: 28 untouched, 38 resolved, 14 blocked-and-diagnosed. Scribe 33 owed, now covering five runs.**a92J06: both declarations closed axi