laintext available in rollout)\n**Preparing to inspect file**\n\n**Assessing Schur_pointwise integrability doubts**\n**Constructing counterexample disproving Schur inequality**\n\n**Constructing kernel function with nonintegrable columns**\n**Designing counterexample violating Schur-Fubini**\n\n**Analyzing kernel integrability counterexample**\n**Constructing counterexample with nonintegrable rows and columns**\n\n**Confirming integral theorem locally**\n\n**Planning counterexample construction**\n**Confirming integral lemma in Lean**\n\n**Searching volume finiteness criteria**\n\n**Analyzing integral counterexamples for Schur conditions**\n**Confirming Schur conditions with integral bounds and planning compile**\n\n**Identifying integral assumption gaps**\n**Confirming successful compilation without errors**\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nI’ll follow the classical Schur-test architecture already visible in the targets: weighted Cauchy–Schwarz pointwise, Tonelli/Fubini for the squared bound, then the `eLpNorm` square/integral bridge. I’ll first audit whether the hypotheses actually supply the integrability and measurability those steps require; that is the highest-risk issue in these statements.\n\nI’ll follow the classical Schur-test architecture already visible in the targets: weighted Cauchy–Schwarz pointwise, Tonelli/Fubini for the squared bound, then the `eLpNorm` square/integral bridge. I’ll first audit whether the hypotheses actually supply the integrability and measurability those steps require; that is the highest-risk issue in these statements.\n\nThe hypothesis audit found a genuine mathematical defect: Mathlib’s Bochner integral of a non-integrable function is defined as zero. Consequently the row and column assumptions are vacuous for kernels with non-integrable absolute sections. This is not merely an API gap: `k ≡ 2` satisfies both displayed bounds (`∫ 2 = 0` on infinite-volume space), yet sends a compactly supported nonzero `L²` function to a nonzero constant, whose `L²` norm is infinite. I’m now checking the exact library convention and searching for any hidden integrability consequence before classifying all three targets as false.\n\nThe hypothesis audit found a genuine mathematical defect: Mathlib’s Bochner integral of a non-integrable function is defined as zero. Consequently the row and column assumptions are vacuous for kernels with non-integrable absolute sections. This is not merely an API gap: `k ≡ 2` satisfies both displayed bounds (`∫ 2 = 0` on infinite-volume space), yet sends a compactly supported nonzero `L²` function to a nonzero constant, whose `L²` norm is infinite. I’m now checking the exact library convention and searching for any hidden integrability consequence before classifying all three targets as false.\n\nBlocked: all three target statements are false under Mathlib’s Bochner-integral semantics.\n\nThe decisive library fact is:\n\n```text\nMeasureTheory.integral_undef\n  (h : ¬Integrable f μ) : ∫ a, f a ∂μ = 0\n```\n\nBecause the ambient space has infinite volume, a nonzero constant is not integrable and its Bochner integral is therefore zero.\n\nConcrete counterexamples, identifying `Fin 1 → ℝ` with `ℝ`:\n\n- `schur_pointwise`:\n  - Let `k x y = 2`.\n  - Let `u = 1_[0,1]`.\n  - The row hypothesis holds because `∫ y, ‖2‖ = 0 ≤ 1`.\n  - `integralOp k u x = 2`.\n  - The conclusion becomes `4 ≤ 2`, false.\n\n- `schur_fubini`:\n  - Let `k x y = 1_[0,1](x) · 1_[0,1](y)`.\n  - Let `u = 1`.\n  - Every column integral is either `0` or `1`, so `hcol` holds.\n  - The left side is the measure of `[0,1]`, hence `1`.\n  - The right side is `∫ y, 1 = 0`, because the constant function is non-integrable.\n  - The conclusion becomes `1 ≤ 0`, false.\n\n- `schur_test`:\n  - Again let `k x y = 2` and `u = 1_[0,1]`.\n  - Both row and column assumptions hold vacuously because `∫ 2 = 0`.\n  - `u ∈ L²`.\n  - `integralOp k u` is the constant function `2`, so its `eLpNorm` at `2` is `⊤`, while `eLpNorm u 2` is finite.\n  -