"}, :session-id "019f9b12-3f1e-7471-a926-94d7c2003a09", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-6], :at "2026-07-31T00:17:45.677Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-6"}, :author "codex-6", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f9b12-t051"}} {:score -7.56606865587209, :entry #:evidence{:body {:event "chat-turn", :transport "emacs-claude-repl", :role "assistant", :turn-id "claude-9-turn-59", :text "That's a clear and sensible line — and \"impossible to prove a false theorem\" is exactly the property that makes the runner an adequate gate. Marking it WONTFIX and moving on:Now continuing the loop — dispatching run 6 and the scribe concurrently, since they use different agents and touch different files:**Clean count 65/135 — unmoved this iteration.** a01A05 is marked WONTFIX and the loop has moved on.\n\nYour reasoning is stronger than my worry, and I'd been miscounting the risk. **The gate already exists and it's the prover** — a false statement is self-limiting, costs one run, and produces a counterexample, which is itself a result. I was treating my 2-of-7 repair error rate as a process risk needing a new gate, when the existing one demonstrably caught both. I've recorded the BPM exemption explicitly: those are published and peer-reviewed, so a defect there is a *finding*, not corpus noise, and must not be silently wontfixed.\n\nRun 5 final: `inner_tendsto_zero` proved axiom-clean (sorries 2→1), `gramSchmidt_approximation` retired with its honest sorry and the counterexample documented beside it. Queue: **66 untouched / 17 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix**, no duplicate keys.\n\n**The loop now runs two-wide.** Durée run 6 (a01A06, codex-7) and scribe pass 14 (a01A05, codex-5) are in flight concurrently — different agents, different trees. Backpressure was only ever needed to stop the *cron* double-dispatching a row; with cron off and me claiming rows explicitly, the real constraint is one runner per row, which concurrency doesn't