e a park-awaiting-nothing rescued it — but the first time it was on purpose, with a payload saying what to do rather than \"placeholder\".\n\n**The failure mode is now well characterised, and it isn't carelessness in any single turn.** The chain dies whenever a turn's work happens to end exactly when the last awaited job completes: the park is consumed by that completion, and if the turn then finishes without dispatching, nothing remains to wake anything. Twice in four hours. A dead-man switch is the right *shape* of fix precisely because the invariant can't be maintained by intention — which is the same lesson as the recall-status banner earlier: put the guard where the failure happens, not in someone's memory of having learned it.\n\n**Loop restarted:** a01J01 and a01J02 dispatched, plus scribe pass 31 covering a01A12 and a02J05. All three under `park-a0665fc3`, heartbeat re-armed as `park-afdf57c7`.\n\nI gave scribe 31 two questions I genuinely can't answer: whether *\"record and carry forward what was NOT found, with enough specificity to justify skipping an arm\"* is a capturable rule or already covered by the existing search-order memory; and whether the three-attempt a02J05 arc yields anything transferable about writing up a **blocked** run for its successor. The seven-part drafting hypothesis it recorded in pass 30 now has a second case to test against, so I asked whether it holds or needs revision — which is what a hypothesis is for.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean, 130 sorries, 24 problems closed.** Queue 42 untouched · 37 resolved · 4 held-out · 3 blocked · 2 wontfix. Usage 13%, store 6.91 GB — every stop condition comfortably clear.a01J01 found the **same junk-value defect a third time** — `uniform_small_set_of_L3_bound` omits an integrability hypothesis, so `integral_undef` lets the condition hold **vacuously**. And it proved a counterexample rather than weakening the theorem. Verifying:Verified: 3 new declarations, **zero changed, zero removed**. But one precision the receipt must carry — the committed `counterexample_from_spikes` uses the **L¹** bound `∫‖f n‖ ≤ 1`, while the target's hypothesis is **L³**. So the spike mechanism is proved; the cubic-singularity step that would extend it to the L³ statement is *argued* in a comment, not formalised:Precisely confirmed: `counterexample_from_spikes` is axiom-clean, `uniform_small_set_of_L3_bound` still carries `sorryAx`, and the cubic-singularity step is a **comment** (lines 101–104). The file's own docstring even scopes the counterexample explicitly: *\"witnesses the failure of part (a) if we only assume an L¹ bound.\"* So the defect is **argued with a proved L¹ component**, not machine-checked like a01A10's.All three pair-6 jobs processed. Continuing the loop — selecting, then dispatching as a separate step:Two heartbeats now — `park-afdf57c7` hadn't fired yet when I armed `park-905d7e01`. That breaks my own \"exactly one\" rule. Harmless (an extra wake), but the rule as written invites it. Correcting it and updating the summary:All three pair-6 jobs are processed and the loop continues.\n\n**a01J01 — third junk-value defect tonight, and this one is vacuity rather than falsehood.** `uniform_small_set_of_L3_bound` omits an integrability hypothesis, so `integral_undef` lets a cubic-non-integrable family satisfy the L³ bound trivially while violating the conclusion. The runner proved a counterexample and left the frontier in a comment rather than adding the missing hypothesis to make the target provable.\n\n**But I recorded what's proved versus what's argued, because the two defects aren't equivalent evidence.** a01A10's refutation is complete and machine-checked — the exact negation, axiom-clean. a01J01's is not: the committed counterexample uses the **L¹** bound (the file's own docstring says so), and the L³ extension is a source comment at lines 101–104. Filing an argued defect alongside a proved one would corrupt exactly the evidence base these receipts exist to keep honest.\n\nSo there's now a **defect class**: three APM statements broken by one Lean convention. And **three runners independently declined to silently repair a statement** — a01A10 refused to pick a repair, a01J02 refused to choose between two, a01J01 refused to weaken. None was told to.\n\n**One error of mine:** I armed a second heartbeat while the first still had 2600 s left, breaking my own \"exactly one\" rule. Harmless, but the rule as written invited it — it stated the invariant without a trigger. Rewritten in the RESUME doc as a procedure: count parks whose `awaiting[0]` doesn't start with `invoke-`, arm only if that count is zero. **An invariant stated without a trigger is a wish.**\n\n**Summary updated** with both structural findings and current figures: **87/135 clean, 130 sorries, twenty-four problems closed.** Queue is 36 untouched · 37 resolved · 4 held-out · **8 blocked-and-diagnosed** · 2 wontfix.\n\nThree decisions wait for you in the morning, all with diagnosis done and none acted on unilaterally: a01A10's Fresnel restatement, a01J02's repair choice (one option is a vacuity trap), and whether to formalise a01J01's argued defect.\n\nPair 7 (a01J06, a02J01) is running under `park-55c86809`, with the heartbeats behind it.a02J01 is the **fifth** junk-value defect and the largest — **all three targets false**, again via `integral_undef`. But no commit and sorries 3 → 3, so like a01J01 these counterexamples are **described, not formalised**. Verifying the reasoning holds:Independently confirmed — the counterexample arithmetic is sound. For `schur_pointwise` with `k ≡ 2`, `u = 1_[0,1]` on infinite-volume `ℝᵈ`: the hypothesis `∫ y, ‖2‖ ≤ 1` holds **vacuously** (0 ≤ 1), LHS = 2² = 4, RHS = `∫ 2·1_[0,1] = 2`, so the claim reduces to 4 ≤ 2. False.**a02J01: all three targets are false** — the fifth junk-value defect, and the largest. Same mechanism: on infinite-volume `ℝᵈ` a nonzero constant is non-integrable, so `integral_undef` makes a hypothesis like `∫ y, ‖k x y‖ ≤ 1` hold **vacuously**. I confirmed the `schur_pointwise` arithmetic myself: hypothesis reduces to 0 ≤ 1, and the claim to 4 ≤ 2.\n\n**But these are argued, not machine-checked** — prose plus arithmetic I checked by hand, with no commit. That distinction is now load-bearing across the whole class, so I've recorded it per-problem rather than lumping them:\n\n| problem | defect | proof status | disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| a01A10 | 1 target FALSE | **machine-checked** (exact negation, axiom-clean) | wontfix pending repair |\n| a95J03 | 1 target VACUOUS | confirmed by inspection | **closed and counted clean — disputed** |\n| a01J01 | 1 target VACUOUS | argued (L¹ part proved, L³ step a comment) | blocked |\n| a01J06 | 1 VACUOUS + 1 FALSE | **machine-checked** counterexample | blocked |\n| a02J01 | 3 targets FALSE | argued | blocked |\n\n**Five problems, seven targets, one root cause.** This isn't five unlucky problems — it's a systematic mismatch between how the APM statements were authored and how Lean totalises integrals. Anyone writing `∫ f ≤ C` as a hypothesis over an infinite-measure space has written something that a non-integrable `f` satisfies for free.\n\nThat reframes tonight's headline: **24 problems closed is real, but the corpus contains a defect class that no gate we run detects** — and one already-clean problem (a95J03) sits inside it.\n\nTwo runner behaviours worth noting: a02J01's declined the weak-L²/Vitali memory as *\"directionally related but the targets fail earlier, through `integral_undef`\"* — a near-miss judged on **where in the argument** the failure occurs, which is finer than relevance. And all five runners diagnosed rather than attempted, without being told.\n\nQueue is now 34 untouched, with 11 rows blocked-and-diagnosed across six distinct causes. I'll fold the tally into the summary and keep the loop running — the remaining untouched rows are unaffected by this class.Heartbeat fired again. Checking the chain — and this time counting heartbeats properly before re-arming, per the rule I fixed:**Chain stopped a third time** — 0 real parks, all agents idle. The heartbeat caught it again. One heartbeat alive with 864s left, so per the corrected rule I won't arm another.\n\nBut three occurrences means the ordering is wrong, not my attention. **Dispatching first, before any processing** — so the chain can never depend on remembering to dispatch at the end:The park awaits only the two proving jobs — I composed it before the scribe's id existed, the same batching error in a milder form. Adding a park for the scribe, and writing the dispatch-first rule into the contract:Pair 8 and scribe 32 are running. Now updating the summary — it still says three junk-value problems and the vacuity finding isn't at the top where it belongs:Chain stopped a third time; the heartbeat caught it again. But three occurrences of one shape isn't inattention — it's a bad ordering, so I fixed it structurally.\n\n**Dispatch first, then process.** The failure was identical every time: awaited jobs complete, the completion consumes the park, the turn does its long processing work, and ends without dispatching. Now on every wake I select, pre-check, dispatch, read the ids back, park — and *only then* verify and receipt. Dispatch is short and chain-preserving; processing is long and interruptible. That makes the gap structurally impossible rather than something to remember, which is the same move as the recall banner and the heartbeat itself.\n\nApplied it this turn: **a02J02 and a02J03 were dispatched before I touched anything else**, plus scribe pass 32.\n\nI did make the batching error again in a milder form — composed the park payload before dispatching the scribe, so its id wasn't in the `awaiting` list. Caught it on read-back and added a second park. Same root cause as the two fabricated ids: composing a record before the thing it references exists. That corollary now sits next to the dispatch-first rule rather than