guard caught it before writing — a01A06 already had a `:priority`. And the refresher exposed a real bug of its own: it dropped only 2 of the 3 proved declarations. Both need fixing:Working directory drifted again from the earlier `cd`:The bounded search now correctly identifies proved declarations across all rows. Dispatching run 7:**Clean 65/135 — unmoved, but sorries 168 → 164.** a01A06 kept one sorry so the problem didn't close.\n\n**The headline is that my highest-risk repair was vindicated.** a01A06 was the one row where I *invented* the mathematics rather than translating it — the informal solution claimed entropy control gives exponential tails (it doesn't) and that `g` must be in L² (it needn't be). I restated part (a) as a polynomial tail bound and part (b) as the counterexample the exam actually asks for. The runner proved **three of four axiom-clean**: `distribution_polynomial_decay` by Markov, `test_function_admissible` by explicit indicator integration, `exponential_decay_implies_L2` by Cavalieri layer-cake. So the restatement wasn't merely true but *provable*, and the deliberately conservative choice — polynomial rather than a sharp entropy bound — was right.\n\n**Repair scoreboard across seven worked rows: five validated in the field, one refuted→corrected→proved, one refuted→WONTFIX.** Both refutations were caught by runners. I'd been treating my error rate as a process risk needing a new gate; measured, the existing gate held every time — which is what you said.\n\n**The compounding test failed a fifth time, but for a new and more fundamental reason: coverage, not plumbing.** The receipts-crowding fix was live and recall still surfaced nothing — so I probed rather than assumed, and `\"Markov inequality\"`, `\"Orlicz\"`, `\"distribution polynomial decay\"` and `\"layer cake exponential tail\"` each return **zero** memories from the 66-memory corpus. Same for a01A03's convolution terrain. **The corpus doesn't yet cover the terrains being worked.** That changes what would fix it: not another seam repair, but more memories in more terrains — which is exactly what the loop produces. Four plumbing faults are behind us; compounding now waits on coverage.\n\nTwo more of my own bugs, both caught by guards I'd added after earlier instances: the duplicate-key assertion fired *before* writing (I was inserting `:priority` rather than setting it, again), and the hint refresher was attributing a later declaration's sorry to an earlier proved one. **The guard I wrote after the first duplicate-key incident is what stopped the third.**\n\nRun 7 is in flight on a01A07 (five sorries, so expect a partial). Queue 66 untouched / 17 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix, usage far below the 50% stop.This wake re-delivers two bellbacks I've already processed — scribe pass 14 (blocked on my bad id → corrected → completed → promoted) and run 6 (verified, receipted, requeued, run 7 dispatched). Let me confirm state rather than redo any of it:Everything in that wake was already processed — it's a re-delivery of the park's two job ids after I'd handled both. Confirming rather than redoing:\n\n- **a01A05** → `:wontfix` (per your direction), scribe pass 14 promoted, memory read-back 200\n- **a01A06** → requeued `:untouched` at priority 3 with its inherited route, after 3 of 4 proved axiom-clean\n- **a01A07** → `:dispatched`, run 7 running at ~1 min\n\n**Clean 65/135. Sorries now 163**, down from 168 at the start of run 6.\n\nNo action taken on the duplicate. Worth noting the mechanism, since it's the crossing hazard CLAUDE.md warns about: my park awaited two jobs, I processed each as its individual bellback arrived, and the park then fired again with both. The guard that made it safe was checking queue state *first* — a01A05 already `:wontfix`, a01A06 already requeued, the pass-14 memory already in the store. Had I acted on the payload directly I'd have written a second receipt for run 6, re-dispatched a scribe pass, and possibly re-queued a row that was already in flight.\n\nOne thing that payload also shows: it still carried the **stale `t011`** instruction for scribe 14. That id never existed — the real one was `t010`, which the scribe caught and I corrected in the live thread. So the re-delivered payload contains an instruction I'd already superseded, which is a good argument for treating a park payload as a *checklist to verify against current state* rather than a script to execute.\n\nThe loop continues on run 7's bellback. Queue 65 untouched / 17 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix, usage well under the 50% stop.Run 7: partial, 1 of 5 closed. And this time the terrain *does* have memories, so the compounding check is meaningful. Verifying:**This is the finding.** The corpus *does* have the right memory — `derive-circle-curve-length-from-the-average-of-the-derivative`, which records `DiffContOnCl.circleAverage`, **exactly what the runner independently found and used.** But the query was `norm area integral` (0 memories), while `circleAverage` finds it immediately.\n\nLet me confirm the mechanism before fixing:Confirmed, and **it's a regression I introduced.** The statement gives variable names (`norm`, `area`, `integral`); the problem *file* carries the mathematics — `disk` ×20, `mean value` ×3, `weierstrass`, `polar`, `fubini`, `cauchy estimate`, `circleaverage`. But `subjects` comes first in the concat, so my 3-term cap truncates before any source-term survives. Fixing by interleaving:Both dispatched. One thing to flag: the scribe job came back as **`mode: brief`** rather than `work`, and brief mode doesn't enforce the completion contract — so I'll verify the drafts file actually exists rather than trusting the reply:**Clean 65/135 — unmoved. Sorries 164 → 163.** a01A07 closed 1 of 5, so no problem completed.\n\n**But run 7 gave the sharpest compounding finding of the series: a genuine retrieval failure, not a coverage gap.** Unlike a01A03 and a01A06 — where probes confirmed the corpus simply had nothing — a01A07's terrain *is* covered. The memory `derive-circle-curve-length-from-the-average-of-the-derivative` records `DiffContOnCl.circleAverage`, **exactly the lemma the runner then found by hand.** Probing directly: `circleAverage` → 1 memory, `holomorphic disk` → 4. The query recall actually sent: **`norm area integral` → 0.** The memory was there, was relevant, and wasn't found.\n\n**Root cause is a regression I introduced earlier today.** `subjects_for` mines the *statement* and yields identifier names — `norm`, `area`, `integral`, `closedball`. The problem *files* carry the mathematics — `disk` ×20, `mean value`, `Weierstrass`, `polar`, `Fubini`, `Cauchy estimate`, `circleAverage`. **Memories are written in the second vocabulary.** The code concatenated subjects *before* source-terms and truncated to 3, so source-terms never survived — and that cap was my own fix, hours earlier, for the 36-term conjunction. **Fixing one recall bug created another.** Repaired by interleaving both vocabularies inside the cap, verified by probe.\n\nThat's the **sixth** distinct cause of \"recall empty\", and the first that's genuinely *semantic* rather than plumbing or coverage. Four of the six were mine. Every \"recall semantics\" conclusion this lane drew before today rested on one of the first five.\n\nAlso worth noting: the runner **re-checked the file's \"not in Mathlib\" claims and found them stale** — current Mathlib has the mean-value property, Weierstrass, and Cauchy's estimate, all documented in-file as absent — and corrected the documentation. Second time today that clause has paid.\n\n**Loop continues.** Run 8 is on **a02J05, a single-sorry row** — so it's a genuine candidate to move your number. Scribe pass 15 is on a01A07. One caution I've recorded: scribe 15 was accepted in **`mode: brief`** rather than `work`, and brief doesn't enforce the completion contract, so I'll verify the drafts exist on disk rather than trusting the reply.Scribe 15 in that wake is already processed. Run 8 is the new item — and **arXiv fired for the first time in the series.** Verifying:**Clean 65/135 — unmoved, sorries 163.** Run 8 proved a support lemma but not the target, so nothing closed. Reporting that plainly rather than dressing it as progress.\n\n**The result worth having is behavioural: arm (c) — arXiv — fired for the first time in eight runs, and for exactly the reason your cond specifies.** Runs 2–7 all recorded arXiv as *not needed* because the route was already known. Here it wasn't: Mathlib lacks both the improper sinc integral and a usable Abel theorem, so there was nothing to assemble from components. The runner fell through, ran real manifest queries, returned five paper ids (`0705.0976`, `1404.5413`, `1405.1265`, `1510.03200`, `2005.00477`) — and reported **background only, no construction anchor.**\n\nAcross eight runs the cond has now discriminated: **6 not-needed, 1 not-attempted, 1 entered-and-empty.** That's the first evidence the ordered search model is doing work rather than describing what runners would have done anyway.\n\nIt also forced a **third ledger category**. Your original insistence was that \"not needed\" must never be scored as failure; this run shows the converse needs its own slot too — an arm *correctly entered* that comes back empty is neither a success nor a waste, and collapsing it into either would corrupt the per-area model. So `surface-payoff.edn` now carries `:arm-tried-and-empty` alongside `:not-needed`.\n\nI probed rather than assumed on the recall side: `sinc`, `Dirichlet integral` and `Abel regularization` all return zero memories, so that 0-surfaced is a genuine **coverage gap**, not the vocabulary mismatch that bit a01A07.\n\na02J05 is requeued at priority 3 carrying the full Abel-regularization route as its `:suggested-route` — evaluate `∫₀^∞ e^{-ax} sinc x = arctan(1/a)`, let `a → 0`, then use the now-proved integration-by-parts identity to remove the regularization uniformly.\n\n**Run 9 is in flight on a03J08** (single sorry, so another candidate to move the number). Queue 65 untouched, usage far below the stop.Run 9 solved it — single sorry closed. Verifying, and this should move your number:**Clean 65 → 66.** a03J08 closed — sorries 1 → 0, axiom-clean, build silent, statement untouched.All three turn-rounds verified by fetch before use. Dispatching scribe pass 16 for both unscribed rows, and run 10:**Clean 65 → 66. It moved.** a03J08 solved axiom-clean, sorries 163 → 162.\n\nThe route is a clean classical argument: extend `(f − w₀)⁻¹` by **zero** across the finite puncture set, apply the **Riemann removable singularity theorem**, then **Liouville** since the extension vanishes at infinity — the contradiction being that the reciprocal is nonzero off `F`. Build exit 0 with no output at all, axioms re-elaborated from a scratch copy, no statement line touched.\n\n**Two findings worth more than the closure itself.**\n\n**The search result inverted.** LeanSearch returned irrelevant results for both queries — chiefly an unrelated continuous-functional-calculus page — and Loogle returned nothing useful, while **local component search over the installed Mathlib source found every lemma the proof needed.** That's the reverse of the usual pattern, where those external tools carried six of nine runs. One row isn't a trend, but it's the first counter-example to \"arm (a) means the external declaration-search tools\", and the packet currently conflates the two.\n\n**And I nearly mis-attributed the recall failure.** I expected a *retrieval* failure here, since a03J08 is complex analysis and we hold a `math/holomorphic-disk-api` pattern with four memories. But `removable singularity`, `Liouville`, `punctured neighborhood` and `analytic blowup surjective` all return **zero**. **The holomorphic memories we hold are narrower than the pattern name suggests** — disk averages, zero counts, Schwarz estimates, not singularities or entire functions. Pattern names invite exac