ched** — the pre-existing partials at normal priority, which is what \"the others as we were doing before\" amounts to:\n\n| row | runner | remaining |\n|---|---|---|\n| a94J04 | codex-1 | approximate-identity estimate; Lebesgue-point theorem |\n| a95A02 | codex-2 | Lusin condition (N) assembly |\n| a96A01 | codex-3 | dominated convergence + squeeze; negative part of log‖f‖ |\n| a97A07 | codex-4 | monomial expansion; circleIntegral reparameterisation |\n\nAll four preflighted clean on 0a/0b/0c **and the new opaque check**, which is now part of the routine.\n\nTwo things worth flagging from the setup:\n\n**a94J04 tripped the hint-refresh guard again** — \"every hinted declaration is transitively sorried, repoint by hand.\" That guard has now fired twice today and been right both times; I repointed to lines 220/236 (`poissonConv_tendsto_L2`, `poissonConv_tendsto_ae`) and asserted a sorry sits at the hint line before dispatching. Without it the row would have shipped pointing at `poissonConv_L2_contraction`, which was closed this morning.\n\n**These packets are *not* SUPERSEDED-tagged**, and that's deliberate: these four statements weren't repaired today, so their recorded diagnoses are current. Mixing them up would have told four runners to ignore diagnoses that still apply. Each park instead carries the named next route from its previous attempt, so the runner starts where the last one stopped rather than re-deriving.\n\nOnly a94A03 remains `:untouched`, still dependency-blocked on the a95J08 Young frontier.a94J04 partial, 2→2 — but the recall outcome is `store-unavailable`, a different failure from the timeouts. Checking the store first, since that would also block receipts:Store is fine — my 404 was a truncated UUID, not an outage; it responded in 0.22s and text-search in 13.3s with 13G free. So `store-unavailable` was transient at that dispatch's recall moment. And I guessed the other job IDs wrong — using the recorded ones:Verified: 2 sorries, lemma axiom-clean, 0 opaque holes. (Also: my `while read` loop silently dropped a97A07 — no trailing newline in the file. It *is* dispatched; the loop lost it, not the queue.)\n\nOne thing worth checking before I write this up:a94J04: partial, 2→2, but with a real lemma and a **sharpened** frontier — not a same-point failure.\n\nThe runner consumed the named route exactly: it used `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp` as specified and proved `eLpNorm_translation_tendsto` (strong continuity of translations in L²), axiom-clean. What remains is now much more precise:\n\n- **tendsto_L2** → Minkowski's *integral* inequality, `‖∫ P_y(t)(τ_t f − f)dt‖₂ ≤ ∫ P_y(t)‖τ_t f − f‖₂ dt`, to average the now-proved continuity\n- **tendsto_ae** → the Hardy–Littlewood maximal theorem, with the sharp reason: L² convergence alone gives only *subsequential* a.e. convergence\n\nZulip independently confirms integral Minkowski was historically absent from Mathlib.\n\n**The finding worth acting on: a94J04 and a96A04 are blocked on the same two pieces, and a94J04 just proved one of them.** a96A04's recorded route is \"translation continuity + integral Minkowski, or Fourier–Plancherel.\" That translation-continuity half now exists — **but it's in a `problems/` file, so a96A04 can't import it.**\n\nThat's the module-path defect for the fourth time today. The difference matters: ConstructionTargets, YoungL2 and a02J05's sinc library were all *pre-existing* damage. **This one we created ourselves, this afternoon** — the loop proved a result and immediately made it unreachable to the problem that needs it. Recommend promoting `eLpNorm_translation_tendsto` (and integral Minkowski when it lands) into ConstructionTargets. Same advice as for the sinc library; it's now a pattern, not an incident.\n\nOn the recall: `store-unavailable` is a **fourth** outcome, distinct from timeout / completed-empty / completed-with-memories. I checked rather than assumed — the store answers in 0.22s, text-search in 13.3s, 13G free, JVM 7.1