an benchmark negative too: 5 surfaced, 0 used, all five declined **by subject area**.\n\n**And I've dispatched a95J08 attempt 3 — the decisive frontier test.** Two prior failures on `young_convolution_inequality`, the gap community-confirmed by Zulip, a literature anchor found, and the hint repointed by hand after the refresher couldn't. The park payload **pre-commits the decision**: if this attempt fails, mark `:blocked-mathlib-frontier` naming the lemma (matching rouche's three-attempt precedent); if it succeeds, a94A03 unblocks immediately.\n\nI wrote it that way deliberately — I've spent the night discovering that I judge better in advance than in the moment, and leaving this to whatever I feel on seeing the result is exactly how a93A04 nearly got mis-marked as a permanent frontier.\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 11 untouched, 18 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%.a95A02 attempt 3: **the \"missing topological decomposition\" is now proved axiom-clean** — four new declarations. And the runner's note is the key: *\"Mathlib has no single packaged open-set decomposition theorem, but its component and separability APIs are sufficient to construct one.\"* Dispatching first:**a95A02's third attempt proved the \"missing\" open-set decomposition axiom-clean** — four new declarations, headline delta zero. Built from `ordConnectedComponent`/`ordConnectedSection` plus `Set.PairwiseDisjoint.countable_of_isOpen`, rather than by manually sorting arbitrary intervals — which is exactly what attempt 2 assumed was required and stopped on.\n\n**And it corrects my own record.** Attempt 2 reported, and I filed in the row, that Mathlib has \"no packaged decomposition of an arbitrary open real set into ordered disjoint intervals\" — recorded as a genuine *proof gap*, distinct from the part (c) statement defect. The observation was true; the inference wasn't. The runner's correction is exact: no single packaged theorem exists, but the component and separability APIs suffice to construct one. **I turned \"not found as a unit\" into \"not available.\"**\n\n**That's 2 for 2 tonight on named gaps being constructed**, after a93A04. And it bears directly on a decision I pre-committed an hour ago: a95J08's third attempt is in flight under the rule that a third failure marks it `:blocked-mathlib-frontier`.\n\n**I'm not loosening that rule on two counterexamples.** Rouché went three attempts and stayed genuinely blocked. But the distinction is now sharp, and I've written it into the record: *\"Mathlib has no packaged X\" is a claim about **effort**, not possibility. A frontier is what survives repeated **attempts**, not what a **search** failed to find.* That's a meaningfully different bar from the one I was applying at the start of the night.\n\nThe row now carries a concrete four-step plan built *on top* of the proved decomposition — endpoints, outer-measure bound, sum over the countable family, `tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero` — plus both rejected routes with reasons, and part (c)'s Borel defect kept separate and flagged \"do not attempt as stated.\"\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 11 untouched, 18 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%. a95J08's decisive third attempt is still running.a95J04's target is false — and the cause is a **`True`-typed hypothesis**: `hR : True`, a no-op where \"radius of convergence exactly one\" was intended. Counterexample `D = Set.univ`, `f ≡ 0`. That's the second `True` placeholder after a01J06's `hEntire`, and it exposes a gap in my own scan. Dispatching first:Confirmed — and the scan found a **second** one I'd have missed: **a95J06 has `(hf_ac_compl : True)`**. That's a no-op hypothesis in a row that hasn't been attempted yet, so for the first time tonight I can flag a defect **before** a runner hits it:**a95J04's target is false because of a `True`-typed hypothesis — and finding it let me flag a second problem before any runner touched it.**\n\n`exists_pole_on_boundary` binds `(hR : True)`, a no-op where a non-extendability condition belongs. So `D = Set.univ` with `f ≡ 0` satisfies every hypothesis while being differentiable at every boundary point. The runner **compiled** that counterexample (exit 0) but didn't commit it — so I've recorded the status as **verified-but-not-in-repo**, its own value between \"argued\" and \"machine-checked\" rather than rounding to either.\n\n**My placeholder scan had a gap, and fixing it paid immediately.** I was matching `True`-typed structure *fields* (a01J06's `hEntire`) but not inline binders `(h : True)`. Grepping for the inline form found **a95J06's `(hf_ac_compl : True)`** — in a row not yet attempted.\n\n**So for the first time tonight I've flagged a statement defect before a runner hit it.** Every other defect this session cost a dispatch to discover — roughly ten runner-hours across the night. The caution on a95J06 names the pattern and its two prior instances, says what to verify, and states explicitly that it is *not* a claim the target is false. That's the same calibration that worked on a95A02, where the flagged note did turn out stale.\n\nThree placeholder forms are now covered in the RESUME doc's vacuity section: bare-constant `def`, `True`-typed field, `True`-typed inline binder — with the grep.\n\nWorth noting what this changes: the statement preflight has been a *per-run* check the runner performs. Scanning the queue directly makes it a **corpus-level** check I can run once and act on across every remaining row. That's the difference between finding defects at one per dispatch and finding them in a batch.\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 10 untouched, 19 blocked-and-diagnosed, 5 in flight.** Usage 19%. a95J08's decisive third attempt is still running.The decisive test resolved — and with a refinement that matters. **The third attempt failed on the general case, so the pre-committed rule fires: `:blocked-mathlib-frontier`.** But the runner proved `young_convolution_inequality_one` axiom-clean and wired it in, so **the remaining sorry is now formally confined to 1 < p < ∞**. The frontier is narrower than I stated it. Dispatching first:Both runners busy, so capacity is full — and the selector's next pick is a95J06, the row I just flagged predictively. Now executing the pre-committed decision:Confirmed precisely: `by_cases hpeq : p = 1` discharges the p=1 branch with the proved lemma, and the sorry sits in the else branch — **formally confined to 1 < p < ∞**. Executing the pre-committed decision:**The pre-committed frontier decision fired, and I honoured it as written.** a95J08 → `:blocked-mathlib-frontier` after three independent failed attempts. The evidence quality is unusually good for such a call: three failures, a Zulip thread stating explicitly that Young's inequality and convolution infrastructure are *missing* from Mathlib, a literature anchor verified against the local manifest (Soudský 1704.08922 — scaffold, no Lean), and a precise statement of what's missing. The runner reached the same conclusion independently and labelled its own outcome the same way.\n\n**But the frontier is narrower than I'd stated it, and recording it wholesale would have hidden a proved branch.** The runner proved `young_convolution_inequality_one` axiom-clean and wired it in via `by_cases hpeq : p = 1`. I read the proof term to confirm: the p=1 branch discharges from the proved lemma, and the sorry sits in the else branch — **formally confined to 1 < p < ∞**. So the frontier is the weighted Hölder/Jensen estimate `(∫w·v)^p ≤ (∫w)^(p−1)·∫w·v^p` plus Tonelli assembly, not the lemma.\n\n**I checked myself against loosening the bar rather than just applying it.** Two problems tonight — a93A04 and a95A02 — had named gaps *constructed* on a later attempt, which is genuine evidence that \"missing\" often means \"unassembled\". But rouche went three and stayed blocked, and the alternative to a three-attempt rule is re-dispatching a confirmed gap forever.\n\nThat's the case for pre-committing at all: **the moment of decision is exactly when the reasons to bend the rule are most available.** I had two fresh counterexamples in hand and a plausible story for why this one might be different. Writing the rule down an hour earlier, when I had neither, is what made it hold.\n\na94A03's dependency is sharpened again — it needs general finite p so it stays blocked, but the row now notes that if its targets reduce to p=1, or if YoungL2's now-importable p=2 contraction suffices, it may be tractable *without* the frontier. Recorded as untested.\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 10 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed (2 now confirmed frontiers), 4 in flight.** Usage 19%.**a95J05 closed — sorries 2 → 0, all five declarations axiom-clean. Thirty-seven.** And metric 3 is measurable with **two memories used**. Dispatching a95J06 next — the row I flagged predictively for `(hf_ac_compl : True)`, which will test whether that caution helps:**The predictive caution did not reach the packet.** `:placeholder-hypothesis-caution` is a custom field the template doesn't render — so my flag is in the queue and invisible to the runner. Same class as the truncated route earlier. Checking what the template actually renders:**a95J05 closed axiom-clean — thirty-seven problems since yesterday morning.** The decisive move was bridging the file's *strict*-threshold `TendstoInMeasureZero` with Mathlib's *closed*-threshold `TendstoInMeasure` — a definitional mismatch that blocked every Mathlib API until resolved.\n\n**But I have to flag a failure of mine from this turn, because it undercuts the win I claimed an hour ago.**\n\nI said I'd finally got ahead of a defect by flagging a95J06's `(hf_ac_compl : True)` before dispatch. **The caution never reached the runner.** I recorded it as `:placeholder-hypothesis-caution`, a custom row key — and the packet template renders only eight fields. a95J06's runner is working right now with no warning at all.\n\nI found this only because I checked *after* dispatching. I've moved the caution into `:suggested-route`, verified it renders by instantiating the packet and grepping, and added the rule to the RESUME doc: **a queue field is not a communication channel.**\n\nThat's the **second time tonight** a carefully-written caution failed to reach a packet — the first was the duplicate-key truncation that dropped a01A07's `sorryAx` warning mid-string. Different mechanism, same result. **Writing the warning is the easy half; delivering it is the half that keeps failing**, and both times I only noticed by accident rather than by checking.\n\nOn the memory system, a pattern worth naming: **both memories used here addressed definitional mismatch rather than proof strategy** — how to connect the file's own predicates to Mathlib's. Across tonight, the corpus has been consumed far more for **interface translation** than for mathematics. The memories that carried proofs (a01A07's frontier pair, LemniscateComponents) are the minority.\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 9 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.a95A02's fourth attempt adds `ac_volume_image_subset_Icc_le_integral_norm_deriv` axiom-clean — the image-measure step, proved **without any measurability assumption on S or f''S** via `Real.volume_le_diam`. Both targets still open, but this exposes a refinement to my frontier rule. Dispatching first:**a95A02's fourth attempt sharpened my frontier rule, and the sharpening is the real result of this turn.**\n\nThe rule was \"three independent failed attempts justifies marking a frontier.\" That's wrong as stated. **Count attempts that fail at the same point, not attempts total.**\n\na95J08 was marked a frontier an hour ago on three attempts that all hit the *same* weighted Hölder/Jensen estimate — a genuine wall. a95A02 now has **four** attempts and is emphatically not a frontier, because each advanced a *different* piece: three AC lemmas, then the deduction plus part-(c) diagnosis, then the open-set decomposition it had itself reported missing, then this image-measure step. Four attempts, four pieces, sixteen declarations — a long **assembly**, not a wall. Marking it on attempt count alone would have been exactly wrong, and an hour ago my rule couldn't have told the two apart.\n\nThe new lemma is nicely scoped too: it bounds volume(f(S)) by the integral of ‖deriv f‖ **with no measurability assumption on S or f''S**, via `Real.volume_le_diam` — outer measure needs no measurability. That removes the separate image-diameter argument the plan had anticipated.\n\nTwo things worth flagging for the morning. **Part (c) was searched specifically this run and remains unresolved in both directions** — only the general Lusin–Souslin result under injectivity surfaced, no AC-specific Borel-image theorem *and* no counterexample. That's more useful than recording it as \"blocked\", because it tells you the decision is genuinely open rather than un-investigated. And both non-component arms were empty: an arXiv manifest query for three spellings of \"Lusin condition (N)\" returned **no records at all**.\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 9 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.a95J06 closed both requested declarations — but **it did not touch the `(hf_ac_compl : True)` declarations**, so my predictive flag is neither confirmed nor refuted; the runner simply worked elsewhere. Checking whether the remaining targets bind it:**This is more serious than the hypothesis case.** `ac_of_bv_continuous_conditionN`'s **conclusion** is `∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, True` — the conclusion itself is trivially provable. A runner could \"close\" it with `fun ε _ => ⟨1, one_pos, trivial⟩`: sorry count drops, axioms clean, **nothing proved**. That's the a95J03 vacuity waiting to happen. Scanning the corpus for this form:**Both** remaining sorried declarations in a95J06 have trivially-true conclusions — one is literally `True`. A runner could close both, the count would go 2 → 0, the problem would be marked **CLEAN**, and nothing would be proved. **That's a95J03 about to happen again, caught before dispatch.** Blocking the row:**This is the most valuable catch of the session, and it's predictive.**\n\na95J06's two remaining targets have trivially-true conclusions: `ac_of_bv_continuous_conditionN` concludes `∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, True`, and `ac_on_01_of_homeo_fixing_cantor` concludes **literally `True`** while binding a no-op `(hf_ac_compl : True)`.\n\n**A runner could close both with `trivial`** — sorry count 2 → 0, axioms clean, problem marked CLEAN, nothing proved. That's exactly the a95J03 failure, which sits in this corpus counted clean and proving nothing. The row is now `:blocked-vacuous-targets` before any runner reached them.\n\n**And the failure that let this sit undetected is mine, in a specific and instructive way.** After a93J07 disproved my first vacuity heuristic, I corrected the rule to *\"test the conclusion, not the hypotheses.\"* Then I implemented a scan that checks `def X := 0` and `(h : True)` binders — **subjects and hypotheses, never the conclusion itself.** The rule I wrote would have caught this immediately; the tooling I wrote could not. It's now step 0 of the preflight, ahead of everything else.\n\nWorth stating plainly why this form is the worst one: **a `True` hypothesis makes a theorem unconstrained but still falsifiable** — a01J06 and a95J04 were both *refuted* through one. **A `True` conclusion makes it unfalsifiable and closable**, and the sorry metric actively rewards closing it. Every gate we run passes on `theorem foo ... : True := trivial`.\n\nThe file documents its own blocker honestly — *\"this fundamental theorem is NOT in Mathlib... requires Banach–Zarecki\"* — so the stubs look deliberate. **That makes it more dangerous, not less: the prose says \"blocked\" while the Lean says \"provable\", and only the Lean is checked.**\n\na95J06's two genuine declarations did close axiom-clean (Cantor-set nullity by self-similar contraction, and continuous-injection monotonicity).\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 8 untouched, 21 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.[curl error (exit 28): {\"type\":\"started\"}\n{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"a96A01: three declarations axiom-clean, sorries 4 → 2. And the memory used was **again for interface normalisation** — translating `quasiNorm` through `lpNorm`]", :clocked-target "E-memory-topology", :excursion-id "E-memory-topology", :clocked-excursion "E-memory-topology"}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-31T04:26:21.365648175Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-97988bfc580bbf9cac51e01e5717cd0a"}}], :count 16, :checked 16, :index-as-of "2026-07-31T04:44:43.940884560Z"}, :proposal-rows [{:score -18.033192998000462, :entry #:evidence{:body {:hook "For holomorphic parameter integrals with an `L^p` input, separate the proof into conjugate-exponent product integrability and a locally uniform derivative dominator.", :scribe-author "codex-5", :why "Prove that the Laplace transform of an `L^p` function on the positive half-line is analytic on the right half-plane.", :draft-source "holes/labs/M-codex-sorry-loop/", :name "prove-Laplace-analyticity-by-conjugate-Holder-and-local-derivative-domination", :runner-model :codex, :kind :feedback, :how-to-apply "For holomorphic parameter integrals with an `L^p` input, separate the proof into conjugate-exponent product integrability and a locally uniform derivative dominator.", :body {:hook "Put the decaying exponential kernel in the conjugate `L^q` space, obtain integrability by Hölder, then dominate the parameter derivative on a small complex ball and differentiate under the integral.", :capture-kind :runner-proof-component-search-and-ground-control-full-namespace-axiom-verification, :problem-class "Prove that the Laplace transform of an `L^p` function on the positive half-line is analytic on the right half-plane.", :key-api ["ENNReal.conjExponent" "MemLp.integrable_mul" "hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le" "Complex.hasDerivAt_exp" "Metric.ball_mem_nhds"], :witness {:axioms ["propext" "Classical.choice" "Quot.sound"], :sorryax false, :declarations ["laplace_integrand_integrable" "laplace_transform_analytic"], :statement-integrity :lean-checked-current-api-equivalent}, :proof-shape {:kernel-integrability "`f ∈ L^p`, `exp(-re(z)t) ∈ L^q`, and conjugacy imply their product is integrable.", :local-domination "A parameter ball staying inside `re(z)>0` supplies one exponential dominator for the integrand derivative.", :analyticity "Locally dominated differentiation supplies a complex derivative at every point."}, :rule "For holomorphic parameter integrals with an `L^p` input, separate the proof into conjugate-exponent product integrability and a locally uniform derivative dominator.", :boundary "The decay estimate requires the parameter's real part to stay positive. Entire analyticity needs the separate compact-support argument.", :formalization-arcs ["Normalize the kernel norm before establishing its `MemLp` power integral." "Use the escaped measure identifier consistently; the earlier token was a lexer/API compatibility failure, not a changed measure." "State entire analyticity with current Mathlib's `AnalyticOn ℂ f Set.univ`; the removed bare `Analytic` predicate is not a reason to weaken the theorem."], :how-to-apply ["For `p > 1`, set `q = p.conjExponent` and prove the exponential kernel belongs to `MemLp q` on the positive half-line." "Combine the input's `MemLp p` hypothesis with the kernel's `MemLp q` membership through `MemLp.integrable_mul`." "At a parameter `z₀` with positive real part, choose a ball whose radius is strictly smaller than `z₀.re`." "Bound both the Laplace integrand and its parameter derivative uniformly on that ball by an integrable exponential-decay function." "Verify the pointwise derivative of `f(t)