 :recall-status :ok, :job-id "invoke-1785463217420-437-12ef7c36", :phase :offered, :problem "hard-problems-a94a01-lean-main-lean", :recall-system :v1-enriched, :recall-query {:recall-system :v1-enriched, :term-sources [{:source :stdin-packet, :terms ["volume" "indicator" "measurableset" "attop" "convergence" "nhds" "tendsto" "line"], :scope :mathematical-fields}], :terms ["limit" "volume" "indicator" "measurableset"], :query "limit volume indicator measurableset", :receipt-ranking {:enabled true, :alpha 0.5, :stats-found? false}}}, :session-id "invoke-1785463217420-437-12ef7c36", :tags [:memory :memory-use :memory-offered], :at "2026-07-31T02:00:18.509983007Z", :type :pattern-outcome, :subject #:ref{:type :apm-problem, :id "hard-problems-a94a01-lean-main-lean"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "e-94350da1-8f08-4ad7-ab0e-627f59f17f24"}} {:score -1.5625192511100685, :entry #:evidence{:in-reply-to "emacs-c9ac3e4bca63c1dafa298b3fb4638838", :body {:event "chat-turn", :transport "emacs-claude-repl", :role "assistant", :turn-id "claude-9-turn-141", :text "Good — the park chain has been the mechanism all evening, so it can keep running unattended. Let me make it robust and leave you something readable in the morning. First the protocol doc, so a continuation (or a fresh session) can pick it up without reconstructing context:Now dispatching. Running **two proving runs in parallel** to roughly double overnight throughput — different rows, different runners, so no file conflict. Pre-checking both packets before either bell:Armed. Two runs in flight and the chain is self-sustaining.\n\n**What's running:** a01A06 (codex-7) and a01A07 (codex-6) in parallel — different files, so no conflict — parked awaiting both (`park-8ef4bc02`, 45-min deadline). I switched to two-at-a-time because sequential dispatch was ~4 round trips per problem, which won't clear 49 rows overnight. Both packets passed the pre-dispatch asserts *before* either bell: not blocked, no unreplaced markers, and hint lines 160 and 84 each verified to contain a real `sorry`.\n\n**The contract is written down** — `holes/ops/RESUME-overnight-2026-07-30.md`. It carries the state, the chain mechanism, stop conditions, the three blocked rows, the pre-dispatch and verification checklists, and the receipt rules, so a continuation or a fresh session can inherit the role without reconstructing tonight.\n\nThe one invariant I wrote as an imperative rather than a checklist item: **a turn that does not park ends the loop silently.** Everything else is recoverable — a lost bellback is caught by the deadline wake, a bad row by the pre-dispatch asserts, usage and queue exhaustion by `duree_dispatch.py` (exit 10 / exit 11, not to be bypassed). Only a missing park is unrecoverable.\n\nThree things from today are encoded so they don't depend on my remembering them:\n\n- **Read deleted lines, don't grep-count them** — prose comments contain \"theorem\" and \"lemma\"; that produced four false alarms today.\n- **A failed recall is `:NOT-MEASURABLE`, never zero** — with `:metric-3-exclude-from-benchmark true`. Recording one as zero is what produced this afternoon's false architectural diagnosis.\n- **The scribe's drafting judgement beats mine** — seven declines, seven correct. Supply evidence, let it judge, record declines as good outcomes.\n\n**In the morning:** there'll be a summary at `holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md` with failures and uncertainties first, and the ⊸ register grows as it goes. To check in directly:\n\n```\nscripts/count_sorries.sh --corpus\ncurl -s localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked | python3 -m json.tool\ntail -40 holes/ops/claude-6.md\n```\n\nOne thing to watch that I can't fix from here: the store was ~7 GB RSS and climbing, and it regrew +4.3 GB in six hours after your restart. If it OOMs, recall fails — but the packets now say so explicitly and runners won't misreport it as a terrain gap, so the loop degrades honestly rather than silently. I won't restart it.\n\nStarting from 81/135 clean, 146 sorries. Good night.That changes an instruction in the overnight contract — the RESUME doc currently says not to restart the store and to report it instead. That would have governed the loop all night. Fixing that first, keeping the serving JVM distinction intact:Confirmed — `futon1b-server` MainPID is the store I've been tracking (7.13 GB after 8.5 h). Updating the contract, with a decision rule so the overnight loop doesn't restart reflexively:The park list is **empty** — but I parked `park-8ef4bc02` minutes ago awaiting both jobs. If that's real, nothing will wake me when they finish and the chain is already broken. Investigating before anything else:False alarm on the park, but a real problem with what I gave you. The park is alive — `?agent=claude-9` shows `park-8ef4bc02` awaiting both jobs with a sane deadline (~43 min out). **The bare `/api/alpha/parked` endpoint returns `[]` and only the agent-filtered query shows it** — so the visibility command I handed you would have told you the loop was dead when it wasn't. Fixing that:Store restart authorised and folded into the contract — thank you, that removes the one condition I couldn't recover from.\n\n**The correction mattered more than the permission.** The RESUME doc told the overnight loop *\"do not restart the serving JVM or the store, report it\"* — that instruction would have governed every hour of tonight and left the loop degrading around something recoverable. Now split properly: `futon1b-server` (7073/7072) is restartable; the futon3c serving JVM (7070/6768/3100) stays under I-0.\n\nI wrote a **decision rule** rather than a bare permission, so it doesn't restart reflexively: two consecutive dispatches recording `:store-unavailable`/`:recall-error`/`:timeout`, **or** RSS > ~10 GB, **or** host available < ~4 GB. Plus two things learned tonight — prefer the gap *between* runs, since runners issue error-recall mid-run and a restart fails those; and health-check `:7073` for a 200 before dispatching, because early post-restart reads can 502, and dispatching into that window would manufacture exactly the false \"recall empty\" this whole evening was spent eliminating.\n\nI left it alone for now: 7.13 GB RSS, host 14.4 GB available, below all three thresholds, and two runs in flight. Recorded what a restart does and doesn't buy — resets RSS and metaspace, but returns list reads to an already-slow baseline. **Restart to reclaim memory, not to fix latency.**\n\n**And running my own instructions before leaving caught a bad one.** The operator-visibility command I gave you — `curl -s localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked` — returns `{\"parked\":[]}` even with a live park. Only `?agent=claude-9` shows it. You'd have checked in the morning, seen an empty list, and concluded the chain had died. Both forms are now in the doc with the misleading one labelled.\n\nThat's the same failure shape as the recall-status collapse three hours ago: **a surface that reports \"nothing\" for two different reasons — nothing exists, versus nothing matched an unstated filter — gets read as the alarming one.** Twice today that ambiguity has done real inferential damage. Worth deciding later whether the unfiltered endpoint should return all parks or refuse the query.\n\n**Armed and verified:** `park-8ef4bc02` awaiting both jobs, deadline 2470 s out, a01A06 and a01A07 both running. Corpus at 81/135 clean, 146 sorries.\n\nMorning check:\n\n```\ncurl -s 'localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked?agent=claude-9' | python3 -m json.tool\nscripts/count_sorries.sh --corpus\ncat holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md\n```\n\nGood night.Items 1–5 and 8 I completed across the last two turns. **Harvest and scribe are owed.** Finding both sessions by commit sha, not mtime:Five new turn-rounds — but these sessions are *also* running the current pair, so some may belong to in-flight work. Verifying each body before citing any:Third time this check has earned itself: **`019fa2c1-t043` is a01A09 — the *in-flight* run**, not a01A06. Citing it would have handed the scribe a run that hasn't been verified yet. Dispatching with only the verified ids:I fumbled that park command — it may have created one awaiting a nonexistent job id, which is a mistake I've made before. Checking and fixing:`park-21a2eb14` is awaiting the literal string `SCRIBE29` — a fabricated id, exactly the mistake I made earlier today. Creating the correct park and trying to retire the bogus one:Harvest and scribe are now done, and the checks caught two more things.\n\n**The turn-round body check caught a third misattribution, subtler than the last two.** Harvesting both sessions produced five new turn-rounds; `019fa2c1-t043` sits right alongside a01A06's t041/t042 but its body is **a01A09 — the run still in flight**. Citing it would have handed the scribe an unverified, half-finished run. That's a parallel-specific hazard on top of the per-session one: when runners share long-lived sessions and the loop runs two at a time, harvest output interleaves completed and running work, and only the body distinguishes them.\n\n**And I fabricated a park job id again** — the literal string `SCRIBE29`. Same error as earlier today, same cause: composing the park in the same command block as the dispatch, before the real id existed. There's no unpark endpoint (`DELETE` and `POST /parked/<id>` both 404), so `park-21a2eb14` will burn its full deadline and fire with payload \"placeholder\". I created the correct park separately and named the bogus one in its payload so the wake isn't misread.\n\nThat's now three instances of one root cause tonight — the fabricated id, the earlier one, and the pre-dispatch check that ran in the same breath as its dispatch. **Never compose a step in the same command block as the action it depends on or gates.** Dispatch, read the id back, then park. It's in the register.\n\n**State: 82/135 clean, 142 sorries.** (142 now — a01A09's runner has landed something; I'll verify it properly when its job reports rather than reading the working tree.)\n\nChain armed with three parks:\n- `park-dd282a89` — a01A07 retry + a01A09, ~40 min\n- `park-5e5832b6` — scribe 29, ~45 min\n- `park-21a2eb14` — bogus, will fire and be ignored\n\nScribe 29 has both runs and two genuine judgement calls I flagged as *my* uncertainty rather than steering it: whether the transitive-`sorryAx` distinction is capturable or already covered, and whether a third route-correction instance changes its earlier \"confirming instance\" verdict. I told it not to defer to me on either.Scribe 29 answered both judgement calls by proposing to *strengthen existing memories* rather than mint new ones — and for the transitive-`sorryAx` question it named a different memory than I guessed. Verifying, including that the cited memory exists:The cited memory resolves (HTTP 200): **`separate-lexical-sorry-count-from-real-proof-hole-count`**. So the corpus already knew what I presented tonight as a novel metric-integrity finding — the scribe retrieved it and correctly classified my \"discovery\" as a confirming instance. Promoting:Pass 29 promoted and reviewed — 4 drafts (2 solve, 2 frontier), all into existing patterns, no eleventh component, no handles minted, contamination scan clean for the third consecutive pass.\n\nTwo things from it worth your attention in the morning.\n\n**The corpus already knew my \"metric-integrity finding,\" and the scribe retrieved it.** I reported tonight that `EXECUTABLE-SORRIES` can't distinguish consolidation from discharge as a fresh discovery. `separate-lexical-sorry-count-from-real-proof-hole-count` has been promoted in this corpus for some time, says exactly that, and the scribe classified a01A07 as a confirming instance rather than a new rule. The memory system knew something I didn't and surfaced it — which is precisely the thing I've spent the day repairing. I've logged it as a miss, because I presented a rediscovery as a finding.\n\n**And the scribe invented a third option I hadn't offered it.** I framed both judgement calls as mint-or-decline. It proposed **amend** — raise the existing memory's confidence and add the observed case (direct sorries 4→2, newly-usable declarations 0, both rewrites still transitively `sorryAx`, one genuine helper) rather than draft a near-duplicate. For a corpus with 34% concentration and no mid-tier, strengthening existing memories is strictly better than adding neighbours, and it's the move I should have been asking for all day.\n\nThat surfaces a real gap: **the pipeline can promote but cannot amend.** Those two amendments are owed and undone; I noted it in the promote script's header rather than dropping it silently. It's the first concrete demand for memory amendment as distinct from promotion, and it came from the scribe rather than from the design — worth folding into the Librarian work in E-memory-topology, which is about exactly this kind of curatorial move.\n\n**Overnight state: 83/135 clean, 140 sorries** — twenty closed since this morning's 63, sorries down from 172. The a01A07 retry and a01A09 are still running under `park-dd282a89` (~33 min), and the chain continues from their wake. The bogus park will fire harmlessly and its successor payload says to ignore it.a01A07's retry delivered real content this time — **six new axiom-clean declarations including `norm_le_area_integral_div_area`**, which was remaining-obstruction #1. Two things need checking: whether the truncated route caused it to lean on the transitively-sorried declarations, and its claim that it altered no statements while fixing a custom `volume` instance.Statements fully intact: **six new declarations, zero changed, zero removed** — the runner's claim that it altered no statements while fixing the custom `volume` mismatch is verified, and every volume-related line is an addition. Now the genuine-discharge accounting:Clean accounting, and it contrasts sharply with the first attempt:\n\n| | attempt 1 | attempt 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| headline sorry delta | −2 | −1 |\n| **genuine discharges** | **0** | **−1** (`norm_le_area_integral_div_area` sorried → clean) |\n| new axiom-clean declarations | 1 | **6** |\n\nNine declarations now clean; the four still carrying `sorryAx` are all downstream of the single remaining sorry. And the truncated route did **no** harm — the part that got through was obstruction #1, which is exactly what the runner proved.The guard fired and stopped me writing a duplicate route again — the row still had **two** `:suggested-route` keys from my earlier bug, so replacing one left the other. Removing both and writing one, with a backup first:Route now single-keyed with the caution placed **first** so truncation can't lose it again; hint refreshed to line 393. Selecting the next pair — as a separate step from dispatch, per tonight's lesson:Pair 2 is fully turned round and pair 3 is running.\n\n**a01A09 solved** — clean 83, twenty problems closed since morning. Its four \"compatibility repairs\" touched theorem signatures, so I diffed every signature and asserted the one substantive change in Lean: `AnalyticOn ℂ f Set.univ ↔ ∀ z, AnalyticAt ℂ f z` elaborates, so the restatement is faithful to \"entire\" and the bare `Analytic` predicate genuinely no longer exists in Mathlib. That's the case where a migration could silently weaken a target with every axiom gate still green.\n\n**a01A07's second attempt is the clean contrast that vindicates splitting the two deltas:**\n\n| | attempt 1 | attempt 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| headline | −2 | −1 |\n| **genuine discharges** | **0** | **−1** |\n| new axiom-clean decls | 1 | **6** |\n\n`norm_le_area_integral_div_area` moved `sorryAx` → clean, so the radial disk formula — obstruction #1 — is proved. One sorry left. A metric that had been indistinguishable across two runs now separates them correctly.\n\n**And the guard I added an hour ago caught the exact bug it was written for.** Replacing a01A07's route left a *second* `:suggested-route` behind, because the row already had two from my earlier mistake — the assertion stopped the write. Redone by removing every route pair and inserting one, verified by key counts, row count and read-back. The route now leads with the caution, so the next truncation can't drop it.\n\nWorth recording honestly: the truncation *this* time did no harm, but only because the surviving fragment happened to be the part that mattered. Lucky, not fine.\n\n**State: 83/135 clean, 139 sorries, usage 12%.** Pair 3 dispatched (a01A07 attempt 3, a01A10) under `park-3e34db0c`. Scribe pass 30 for a01A09 and a01A07-attempt-2 is owed and named in the park payload.\n\nThe overnight summary will be at `holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md` before you're up, failures first.**a01A07 is closed** — and this is the loop compounding on itself completely. It used both frontier memories that scribe pass 29 drafted from its *own previous attempt* and I promoted an hour ago: `upgrade-diskwise-L1-convergence-to-local-uniform-convergence` (\"its local half-disk route carried the proof\") and `lift-the-circle-submean-bound-to-a-disk-area-bound` (\"already axiom-clean, so it was not rebuilt\"). Verifying:Complete verification: **sorries 0, four declarations added, zero changed, zero removed, and all 17 declarations axiom-clean with zero `sorryAx` occurrences.** The four that carried transitive `sorryAx` are now all clean.**a01A07 closed — clean 84, twenty-one problems closed since this morning** (63 → 84, sorries 172 → 136). All 17 declarations axiom-clean, zero `sorryAx`, 4 added / 0 changed / 0 removed.\n\n**And this is the result I'd most want you to see.** Attempt 3 used *both* frontier memories that scribe pass 29 drafted from a01A07's own attempt 2 — promoted and reviewed about an hour before the dispatch. The runner reports `upgrade-diskwise-L1-convergence-to-local-uniform-convergence` **carried the proof**, via the local half-disk route that avoided the finite-cover construction attempt 1 had discarded; and `lift-the-circle-submean-bound-to-a-disk-area-bound` was used as verified prerequisite support it therefore didn't rebuild.\n\nLemniscateComponents earlier tonight was a *solve*-lane memory supplying architecture. This is a **frontier** memory — a record of what *remained* — closing the very thing it described. The loop wrote down its own unfinished business, read it back, and finished it. That's the mechanism the whole day's repair work was for.\n\n**The three attempts also give the cleanest proof yet that the two sorry-metrics diverge in both directions:**\n\n| | headline | genuine |\n|---|---|---|\n| attempt 1 | −2 | **0** |\n| attempt 2 | −1 | −1, +6 clean |\n| attempt 3 | −1 | **5 declarations unblocked** |\n\nA metric that *overstated* progress on attempt 1 *understates* it on attempt 3 — and splitting those two fields in the receipt schema tonight is the only reason it's visible.\n\nAlso this round: **a01A10** — two targets solved, third proved false by machine-checked refutation, marked `:wontfix-statement-false-pending-repair` with `:decision-owner \"joe\"`. The mathematics is true; only the Bochner-integral formalisation is wrong, and the repair is a one-line decision for you with the proof already done.\n\nQueue: 45 untouched, 34 resolved, 4 held-out, 3 blocked, 2 wontfix. Usage 12%.\n\nScribe pass 30 is owed and now covers three runs (a01A09, a01A07 attempts 2–3, a01A10's refutation). I'll dispatch it with the next pair and park.That's the fabricated `SCRIBE29` park firing on its deadline — exactly as predicted and documented, so it's noise, not a failure. But it's a useful wake: checking whether any real park survives.**Zero live parks — the chain had broken**, and the fabricated park's deadline is the only reason I'm awake. My mistake accidentally functioned as the safety net. That's worth making deliberate rather than accidental. Selecting the next pair first, as its own step:Three rows are now unreachable on stale hints — the pre-dispatch check correctly refuses them, but they'll accumulate. The refresher only targets rows with `:repaired-at` or `:last-commit`, and these have neither. Minimal fix — honour explicit ids:That wake was the fabricated `SCRIBE29` park firing — and it turned out to matter.\n\n**The chain had stopped.** There were zero live parks: `park-3e34db0c` was consumed when both pair-3 jobs finished, I processed them, and I ended that turn without dispatching or re-parking. The loop was dead. **The only thing that woke me was the park I fabricated by mistake** and logged as harmless noise.\n\nSo I've made it deliberate. `park-2c25905f` is now a **heartbeat**: it awaits a deliberately non-existent job so its deadline always fires (~58 min), with a payload telling the waking turn to check whether a real park exists and, if not, restart the loop from the RESUME doc. The accidental version worked; this one has instructions instead of \"placeholder\". The invariant \"every turn must park\" isn't enforceable by good intentions — this makes a missed park recoverable rather than fatal.\n\n**Second fix: three rows had become permanently undispatchable.** Their hints were stale, so the pre-dispatch assertion refused them — correctly — but the refresher couldn't reach them, because it only auto-selects rows the queue *knows* changed. A hint also goes stale for reasons never recorded (a Mathlib bump, work outside this lane). Now an explicitly named row is always refreshed: a02J05 and a01A11 are unstuck, dispatchable rows back to 43 of 45, and I regression-checked that the no-args default still selects only known-changed rows.\n\nWorth noting the pre-dispatch check has now paid for itself four times tonight. It converts a silent waste into a visible SKIP — which is the only reason the accumulation was noticeable at all.\n\n**Running now:** a02J05 + a01A11, plus scribe pass 30 covering three runs. That scribe owes me restated **evidence amendments** in a form I can apply by hand, since the pipeline can promote but not amend — and `override-a-documented-proof-route…` has now been used a fourth time while its amendment sits unapplied.\n\nState: **84/135 clean, 136 sorries**, 21 closed since morning, usage 12%, store 6.9 GB (below the 10 GB restart threshold). Two parks armed: the real one and the heartbeat.a01A11 and scribe 30 I handled in the last two turns. **a02J05 is new**: blocked, but the entire convergence/tail-control half is now axiom-clean — and metric 3 shows **2 memories used**, both drafted from a02J05's *own* earlier attempt this morning. Verifying:Verified: 4 new declarations, **zero changed, zero removed** — target statement intact; 5 of 6 axiom-clean with only the target carrying `sorryAx`. Headline delta **0**, genuine gain **+4 usable declarations** — a third distinct shape of the metric divergence.a02J05 is turned round and the summary is written at `holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md`, failures first.\n\n**a02J05: the whole convergence half is now axiom-clean, and the headline metric reports nothing.** Sorries 1 → 1, but four new clean declarations including the uniform tail bound `|∫ₐᵇ sinc| ≤ 2/a` and the Cauchy-criterion convergence. Verified 4 added / 0 changed / 0 removed. That's a **third divergence shape** — headline 0, four declarations newly usable — after a01A07's −2/0 and −1/5. Three shapes in one night is exactly why the amendment splitting that metric into three quantities was worth applying.\n\n**And it's the third compounding instance, this one a row feeding itself across the day.** Both memories it used were drafted from a02J05's *own* failed attempt this morning — the run that first blocked on this row. One was used to evaluate the inherited Abel route and locate the precise remaining bridge; the other directly informed the tail estimate. A morning failure became the evening's proof of half the problem.\n\n**The one thing waiting on you** is a01A10: `fresnel_integral` is false as stated and the runner *proved* it. Unlike a01A05, the mathematics is true — only the Bochner-integral formalisation is wrong, and the repair is a `Tendsto` restatement. Row marked `:decision-owner \"joe\"` with the proof attached; the other two targets are closed.\n\n**Where it stands: 84/135 clean, 136 sorries, twenty-two problems closed** (from 63/172), plus a construction target and a documented Mathlib frontier. Usage 12%.\n\nThe summary is candid about my own errors — the chain stopping and being rescued by my own mistake, the queue corruption and restore, the duplicate keys I created and then generalised a guard for. It also flags the uncertainty I think matters most: **two compounding channels are running and only one is instrumented.** Proof-corpus reuse happened twice tonight (a00J04 → a01A08, a00J05 → a01A11) and is counted nowhere, so the loop's real self-reinforcement rate is under-reported by an unknown amount.\n\na02J05 attempt 2 and a01A12 are running under `park-3e308208`, heartbeat behind them.**a02J05 closed** — the Dirichlet integral, sorries 1 → 0. And note: *\"Arms (b) Zulip and (c) arXiv were not rerun, as the packet explicitly recorded their previous negative results and instructed against repetition.\"* The negative results I wrote into the route saved two whole search arms. Verifying:Verified: 11 new declarations, **zero removed, zero changed**, all 17 reporting with **zero `sorryAx`**. But the corpus reads **87**, and I predicted 86 — let me check that rather than assert it:Reconciled, and it corrects me: 84 (after a01A07) → **a01A11** → 85 → a01A12 → **86** → a02J05 → **87**. I wrote `:hud-clean-after-committed 85` in the a01A12 receipt, computing from a baseline that had already moved when a01A11 closed. I correctly flagged the contamination and then did the arithmetic off a stale number.**a02J05 closed — twenty-four problems, corpus 87 clean / 130 sorries, measured with nothing in flight.** 11 declarations added, zero changed, zero removed, zero `sorryAx` anywhere in the file.\n\nTwo results from this run are worth more than the close itself.\n\n**The negative results I wrote into the route saved two entire search arms.** The runner: *\"Arms (b) Zulip and (c) arXiv were not rerun, as the packet explicitly recorded their previous negative results and instructed against repetition.\"* Attempt 1 had established that Mathlib's `Sinc` and `ImproperIntegrals` files carry no sinc/Abel theorem, Zulip has no formalised Dirichlet integral, and those two arXiv papers are background only. Recording what was *not* found turned out to be as valuable as recording what was — and it's far cheaper to carry forward than a proof.\n\n**And the same two memories carried this row across all three attempts, spanning a full day.** Both were drafted from durée run 8 yesterday morning — the run that *first* blocked here — used overnight to prove the convergence half, and used again tonight to close it. A single failed run's memories carried a problem to completion. That's the fourth compounding instance and the most persistent; the other three were single-hop.\n\n**One correction of mine.** I recorded `:hud-clean-after-committed 85` on the a01A12 receipt; the true figure was 86. I correctly detected the working-tree reading was contaminated by a02J05 being mid-flight — and then computed a replacement from a stale baseline, forgetting a01A11 had already moved it. The lesson is narrower than the trap I was avoiding: **detecting that a reading is contaminated doesn't license substituting an arithmetic estimate for it.** The right move was to record it as unavailable until nothing was in flight. Correction receipt written.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean, 130 sorries** (from 63/172). Queue: 42 untouched · 37 resolved · 4 held-out · 3 blocked · 2 wontfix. Usage ~12%.\n\nBoth pair-5 jobs are done, so I owe the next dispatch and a scribe for a01A12 + a02J05. I'll take those now and re-park — the heartbeat is still standing behind it.The heartbeat fired as designed. Checking whether the chain is healthy or stopped:**The chain had stopped** — zero parks, all eight agents idle. The heartbeat caught it exactly as designed. Second time the loop died tonight; first time something deliberate rescued it. Restarting — selecting and pre-checking first:The heartbeat fired and **caught a genuinely stopped chain** — validated on its first deliberate use.\n\nAt 02:02: zero live parks, all eight codex agents idle, nothing running. Both pair-5 jobs had completed, I processed them, and the turn ended without a re-dispatch. That's the second time tonight the loop died and the second time a park-awaiting-nothing rescued it — but the first time it was on purpose, with a payload saying what to do rather than \"placeholder\".\n\n**The failure mode is now well characterised, and it isn't carelessness in any single turn.** The chain dies whenever a turn's work happens to end exactly when the last awaited job completes: the park is consumed by that completion, and if the turn then finishes without dispatching, nothing remains to wake anything. Twice in four hours. A dead-man switch is the right *shape* of fix precisely because the invariant can't be maintained by intention — which is the same lesson as the recall-status banner earlier: put the guard where the failure happens, not in someone's memory of having learned it.\n\n**Loop restarted:** a01J01 and a01J02 dispatched, plus scribe pass 31 covering a01A12 and a02J05. All three under `park-a0665fc3`, heartbeat re-armed as `park-afdf57c7`.\n\nI gave scribe 31 two questions I genuinely can't answer: whether *\"record and carry forward what was NOT found, with enough specificity to justify skipping an arm\"* is a capturable rule or already covered by the existing search-order memory; and whether the three-attempt a02J05 arc yields anything transferable about writing up a **blocked** run for its successor. The seven-part drafting hypothesis it recorded in pass 30 now has a second case to test against, so I asked whether it holds or needs revision — which is what a hypothesis is for.\n\n**State: 87/135 clean, 130 sorries, 24 problems closed.** Queue 42 untouched · 37 resolved · 4 held-out · 3 blocked · 2 wontfix. Usage 13%, store 6.91 GB — every stop condition comfortably clear.a01J01 found the **same junk-value defect a third time** — `uniform_small_set_of_L3_bound` omits an integrability hypothesis, so `integral_undef` lets the condition hold **vacuously**. And it proved a counterexample rather than weakening the theorem. Verifying:Verified: 3 new declarations, **zero changed, zero removed**. But one precision the receipt must carry — the committed `counterexample_from_spikes` uses the **L¹** bound `∫‖f n‖ ≤ 1`, while the target's hypothesis is **L³**. So the spike mechanism is proved; the cubic-singularity step that would extend it to the L³ statement is *argued* in a comment, not formalised:Precisely confirmed: `counterexample_from_spikes` is axiom-clean, `uniform_small_set_of_L3_bound` still carries `sorryAx`, and the cubic-singularity step is a **comment** (lines 101–104). The file's own docstring even scopes the counterexample explicitly: *\"witnesses the failure of part (a) if we only assume an L¹ bound.\"* So the defect is **argued with a proved L¹ component**, not machine-checked like a01A10's.All three pair-6 jobs processed. Continuing the loop — selecting, then dispatching as a separate step:Two heartbeats now — `park-afdf57c7` hadn't fired yet when I armed `park-905d7e01`. That breaks my own \"exactly one\" rule. Harmless (an extra wake), but the rule as written invites it. Correcting it and updating the summary:All three pair-6 jobs are processed and the loop continues.\n\n**a01J01 — third junk-value defect tonight, and this one is vacuity rather than falsehood.** `uniform_small_set_of_L3_bound` omits an integrability hypothesis, so `integral_undef` lets a cubic-non-integrable family satisfy the L³ bound trivially while violating the conclusion. The runner proved a counterexample and left the frontier in a comment rather than adding the missing hypothesis to make the target provable.\n\n**But I recorded what's proved versus what's argued, because the two defects aren't equivalent evidence.** a01A10's refutation is complete and machine-checked — the exact negation, axiom-clean. a01J01's is not: the committed counterexample uses the **L¹** bound (the file's own docstring says so), and the L³ extension is a source comment at lines 101–104. Filing an argued defect alongside a proved one would corrupt exactly the evidence base these receipts exist to keep honest.\n\nSo there's now a **defect class**: three APM statements broken by one Lean convention. And **three runners independently declined to silently repair a statement** — a01A10 refused to pick a repair, a01J02 refused to choose between two, a01J01 refused to weaken. None was told to.\n\n**One error of mine:** I armed a second heartbeat while the first still had 2600 s left, breaking my own \"exactly one\" rule. Harmless, but the rule as written invited it — it stated the invariant without a trigger. Rewritten in the RESUME doc as a procedure: count parks whose `awaiting[0]` doesn't start with `invoke-`, arm only if that count is zero. **An invariant stated without a trigger is a wish.**\n\n**Summary updated** with both structural findings and current figures: **87/135 clean, 130 sorries, twenty-four problems closed.** Queue is 36 untouched · 37 resolved · 4 held-out · **8 blocked-and-diagnosed** · 2 wontfix.\n\nThree decisions wait for you in the morning, all with diagnosis done and none acted on unilaterally: a01A10's Fresnel restatement, a01J02's repair choice (one option is a vacuity trap), and whether to formalise a01J01's argued defect.\n\nPair 7 (a01J06, a02J01) is running under `park-55c86809`, with the heartbeats behind it.a02J01 is the **fifth** junk-value defect and the largest — **all three targets false**, again via `integral_undef`. But no commit and sorries 3 → 3, so like a01J01 these counterexamples are **described, not formalised**. Verifying the reasoning holds:Independently confirmed — the counterexample arithmetic is sound. For `schur_pointwise` with `k ≡ 2`, `u = 1_[0,1]` on infinite-volume `ℝᵈ`: the hypothesis `∫ y, ‖2‖ ≤ 1` holds **vacuously** (0 ≤ 1), LHS = 2² = 4, RHS = `∫ 2·1_[0,1] = 2`, so the claim reduces to 4 ≤ 2. False.**a02J01: all three targets are false** — the fifth junk-value defect, and the largest. Same mechanism: on infinite-volume `ℝᵈ` a nonzero constant is non-integrable, so `integral_undef` makes a hypothesis like `∫ y, ‖k x y‖ ≤ 1` hold **vacuously**. I confirmed the `schur_pointwise` arithmetic myself: hypothesis reduces to 0 ≤ 1, and the claim to 4 ≤ 2.\n\n**But these are argued, not machine-checked** — prose plus arithmetic I checked by hand, with no commit. That distinction is now load-bearing across the whole class, so I've recorded it per-problem rather than lumping them:\n\n| problem | defect | proof status | disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| a01A10 | 1 target FALSE | **machine-checked** (exact negation, axiom-clean) | wontfix pending repair |\n| a95J03 | 1 target VACUOUS | confirmed by inspection | **closed and counted clean — disputed** |\n| a01J01 | 1 target VACUOUS | argued (L¹ part proved, L³ step a comment) | blocked |\n| a01J06 | 1 VACUOUS + 1 FALSE | **machine-checked** counterexample | blocked |\n| a02J01 | 3 targets FALSE | argued | blocked |\n\n**Five problems, seven targets, one root cause.** This isn't five unlucky problems — it's a systematic mismatch between how the APM statements were authored and how Lean totalises integrals. Anyone writing `∫ f ≤ C` as a hypothesis over an infinite-measure space has written something that a non-integrable `f` satisfies for free.\n\nThat reframes tonight's headline: **24 problems closed is real, but the corpus contains a defect class that no gate we run detects** — and one already-clean problem