ingle packaged open-set decomposition theorem, but its component and separability APIs are sufficient to construct one.\"* Dispatching first:**a95A02's third attempt proved the \"missing\" open-set decomposition axiom-clean** — four new declarations, headline delta zero. Built from `ordConnectedComponent`/`ordConnectedSection` plus `Set.PairwiseDisjoint.countable_of_isOpen`, rather than by manually sorting arbitrary intervals — which is exactly what attempt 2 assumed was required and stopped on.\n\n**And it corrects my own record.** Attempt 2 reported, and I filed in the row, that Mathlib has \"no packaged decomposition of an arbitrary open real set into ordered disjoint intervals\" — recorded as a genuine *proof gap*, distinct from the part (c) statement defect. The observation was true; the inference wasn't. The runner's correction is exact: no single packaged theorem exists, but the component and separability APIs suffice to construct one. **I turned \"not found as a unit\" into \"not available.\"**\n\n**That's 2 for 2 tonight on named gaps being constructed**, after a93A04. And it bears directly on a decision I pre-committed an hour ago: a95J08's third attempt is in flight under the rule that a third failure marks it `:blocked-mathlib-frontier`.\n\n**I'm not loosening that rule on two counterexamples.** Rouché went three attempts and stayed genuinely blocked. But the distinction is now sharp, and I've written it into the record: *\"Mathlib has no packaged X\" is a claim about **effort**, not possibility. A frontier is what survives repeated **attempts**, not what a **search** failed to find.* That's a meaningfully different bar from the one I was applying at the start of the night.\n\nThe row now carries a concrete four-step plan built *on top* of the proved decomposition — endpoints, outer-measure bound, sum over the countable family, `tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero` — plus both rejected routes with reasons, and part (c)'s Borel defect kept separate and flagged 