rusting a truncated view of a file. Now in the checklist as \"check the signature line directly\".\n\nAnd I left a literal `\"REPLACE\"` placeholder in a93A03's `:receipt` field — a substitution I wrote and didn't fill. Caught on read-back, fixed, verified no others remain. That's the **fourth instance today of asserting a value I hadn't read back**, after the phantom `t011`, the hypothetical hit-count, and the duplicate keys. The read-back is what caught all four, which is why it's in the checklist.\n\n**Repair scoreboard after thirteen runs: six validated in the field, one refuted→corrected→proved, one refuted→WONTFIXed.** Every refutation was caught by a runner, none by me — which is the arrangement you described and it's holding.\n\n**Session totals: clean 63 → 69 (six problems closed — a01A02, a01A03, a03J08, a92J03, a92J04, a93A03), executable sorries 172 → 159 (thirteen), corpus 51 → 76 memories, patterns 5 → 7.** Queue 62 untouched / 21 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix.\n\nRun 14 is on a93J01, scribe 19 covers both a93A03 runs (and came back in `work` mode this time). Usage still far below the stop.Pass 19 in that wake is already handled (promoted, then corrected). Run 14 is new — and it solved. Verifying:**Clean 69 → 70. Fourth consecutive close.** Axiom-clean, statement untouched, sorries 159 → 158.**Clean 69 → 70. Fourth consecutive close, seventh problem closed today.**\n\na93J01 via FTC for absolutely continuous functions — `BoundedVariationOn` gives interval-integrability of `deriv f`, its indefinite integral is AC, and local FTC identities on `[x/n, x]` with limit uniqueness identify the primitive with `f − f 0`. Axiom-clean, statement untouched, sorries 159 → 158. I read the signature line directly this time rather than through a truncated grep — the checklist item added after that nearly caused a mis-report an hour ago.\n\n**A memory promoted in pass 18 was exercised on the very next run — and I'm recording the weaker true claim rather than the flattering one.** The rule `prefer-installed-source-search-when-the-library-namespace-is-guessable` was promoted from three instances. On this run the runner guessed three declaration names almost exactly — `intervalIntegrable_deriv`, `integral_deriv_eq_sub`, `absolutelyContinuousOnInterval_intervalIntegral` — and Loogle confirmed all three while LeanSearch again returned nothing.\n\n**But recall surfaced nothing and the runner didn't report consuming the memory.** So this is a *confirming instance* of the rule, not evidence the memory was *used*. Those are different claims, and I've put the distinction to the scribe explicitly rather than letting the corpus record the stronger one.\n\nAlso worth noting: **third time today a runner has corrected stale in-file documentation** rather than leaving it to mislead — here replacing a stale combinatorial note with the FTC route actually implemented. That's unprompted maintenance by the agents working the corpus.\n\nI applied the receipt-helper fix and validated the EDN locally before posting — both the guard and the parse, since the guard prevents the class and the parse catches what it misses.\n\n**Session: clean 63 → 70 (seven closed), executable sorries 172 → 158 (fourteen), corpus 51 → 78, patterns 5 → 7.** Queue 61 untouched, run 15 on a93J02, usage still far below the stop.Pass 20 in that wake is already promoted. Run 15 solved — verifying:**Clean 70 → 71. Fifth consecutive close, eighth problem today.****Clean 70 → 71. Fifth consecutive close, eighth problem today.**\n\na93J02 composes three standard results: the **alternating-series test** gives convergence to an unknown limit, **Abel's limit theorem** identifies it with the radial power-series limit, and the **Mercator series** gives that power series as `log(1+x)/x`, whose limit at `x → 1⁻` is `log 2`. Both declarations axiom-clean — and I checked whether they were namespaced rather than assuming, since they turned out to be top-level.\n\n**The behavioural result: arm (b), Zulip, supplied the *route* for the first time in fifteen runs.** The thread `Bounds on alternating convergent series` explicitly discusses combining an alternating-series result, a Taylor series and Abel's theorem, and records the development of Mathlib's real Abel theorem — it described the exact three-part composition the runner then implemented. Until now that arm had only ever *corroborated an obstruction* (log-integrability, smooth-vs-analytic, completeness) or returned nothing. The packet assigns it the role \"how has this been formalized before, and what are the known obstacles\"; that role has now paid in the strong sense. I've offered it to the scribe with the n=1 caveat rather than asserting a pattern.\n\n**Fifth consecutive run where LeanSearch returned nothing** while Loogle name-guessing carried the components. The promoted search rule now has **five independent confirmations and still zero instances of being consumed** — I keep both numbers together deliberately, because the rule is a true description of runner behaviour and the memory system still hasn't been shown to cause any of it.\n\n**Session: clean 63 → 71 (eight closed), executable sorries 172 → 157 (fifteen), corpus 51 → 81 memories, patterns 5 → 7.** Queue 60 untouched / 23 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix. Codex usage 6% against the 50% stop.\n\nRun 16 is on a93J04, scribe 21 on a93J02. The loop's rate is now roughly one closed problem per iteration, bounded by my verification turnaround rather than anything else.Run 16 solved — and this is `eLpNorm → essential supremum` terrain, where I believe the corpus **does** hold a memory. Verifying, then probing that:**Clean 71 → 72. Sixth consecutive close.** Now the probe that matters — this is near-identical terrain to a memory we hold:The query recall sent contains **`elpnorm` — lowercased**. Let me test whether the search is case-sensitive, because if so that breaks every Lean identifier:Case is **not** the issue — all three casings return the same 2 memories, so that hypothesis is wrong. It's the conjunction again: `elpnorm` alone finds them, `tendsto elpnorm attop` finds none. But **my ladder should fall back to singles and find them.** Let me test the actual recall path rather than infer:Every tier returns 20 rows — which means **my memory filter is silently falling back to unfiltered results**, and because the first tier is therefore never empty, **the ladder never falls through either.** Let me confirm the row shape:Found it — rows are `{:score _ :entry {...}}`, so the evidence type is nested under `:entry` and my filter read `nil` on every row. Fixing and measuring:**Now it works** — the `tendsto elpnorm` pair tier returns the exact relevant memory as its first result. Committing, and correcting the record on two fixes I reported as working:**Clean 71 → 72. Sixth consecutive close, ninth problem today.** a93J04 via `Lᵖ → L^∞` on a finite measure space.\n\n**But the real result is a bug in my own recall fixes — and it made two of them inert at once.**\n\na93J04 is `eLpNorm`-to-essential-supremum terrain, and the corpus holds a **near-exact memory** for it from pass 11. Recall surfaced nothing. The cause: my receipts-crowding filter read `:evidence/type` at the **top level** of each search row, but rows are `{:score _ :entry {…}}` with the document nested under `:entry`. So it matched nothing every time and fell back to unfiltered results — **and because that fallback left every ladder tier non-empty, the ladder never fell through to the shorter queries it exists to reach.**\n\nI reported both fixes as working. I had measured the *endpoint*, not the *seam*. Fixed and verified by direct probe: the pair tier `tendsto elpnorm` now returns the exact memory **first**, where the whole ladder previously returned 20 unfiltered rows at every tier.\n\n**My first hypothesis was wrong, and I checked it before acting.** Seeing `elpnorm` lowercased, I assumed case-sensitivity — `subjects_for` lowercases, Lean uses camelCase, so every identifier query would fail. Plausible, systematic, and **false**: `eLpNorm`, `elpnorm`, `ELPNORM` all return the same 2 memories. Had I \"fixed\" casing I'd have shipped a no-op and left the real bug in place.\n\nThat's the **seventh** distinct cause of recall-empty, and the fourth that was mine. **Run 17 (a95A01, Riemann–Darboux — terrain the corpus does cover) is the first dispatch where the machinery is actually live end to end.** If it surfaces nothing there, that's a genuine retrieval failure and not coverage.\n\nI also recorded counter-evidence rather than filtering it: LeanSearch returned *relevant* declarations this run, breaking a five-run streak and cutting against the search rule promoted in pass 18. I offered that to the scribe explicitly.\n\n**Session: clean 63 → 72 (nine closed), sorries 172 → 156, corpus 82 memories.** Queue 59 untouched, usage ~6%.Run 17 in that wake is already fully processed (verified, receipted, resolved, clean 73). Rather than re-report, let me close the open question I flagged — probing the pattern-proposal step between search rows and offered memories:**Decisive, and it changes the diagnosis.** The pattern-proposal step returns **zero candidates at every tier** — including `riemann` alone, which the search demonstrably answers with 5 codex memories. So the ladder isn't the problem; the break is *upstream of everything I've fixed today*. Let me find it:The function's own comment is the clue: it over-fetches \"before retaining memory **or typed pattern-description entries**\". My seam filter keeps only `:evidence/type :memory` — which would **strip the pattern-description rows it needs**. Checking:**Confirmed, and worse than I thought — my \"fix\" broke it two ways.** `proposal-search-rows` reads `(:results search-result)`, expecting the original **map**; my seam now returns a **vector**, so that's `nil` → zero rows. And it *already* does exactly the filtering I added (`:memory` **or** `pattern-description-row?`). My filter was redundant from the start and destructive in effect. Reverting:Still zero — so my seam filter wasn't the root cause, though the revert was still right (it was redundant and shape-breaking). Let me locate the actual break:The seam returns the right shape now — 30 results, first row is the **pattern-description for `riemann-darboux-api`**, exactly what the proposer wants, plus 2 memory rows. So the break is downstream, in the \"reviewed edge to a pattern endpoint **in DOMAIN**\" requirement:The final link is `recall-by-endpoints` on the pattern id. Probing it directly:Zero memories for both patterns — despite the patterns existing with `:domain :mathematics` and the memories attached via `:memory/assert` edges with the pattern as an endpoint. Before asserting a cause, let me run the control that distinguishes \"projection broken\" from \"our edges misconfigured\":**Decisive.** `tactic-algebra-interference` returns **5 memories**; every pattern the codex-lane promotion pipeline has written to returns **0**. The projection works — our *edges* are wrong. Comparing a working memory's attachment against one of mine:I have enough for a clear diagnosis, and I'm going to stop digging rather than guess at a fix. Committing and recording:**I found the root cause of the entire recall history, and it's upstream of all seven causes I diagnosed today.**\n\n`recall-by-endpoints` returns **0 memories for every pattern this lane's promotion pipeline has ever written to** — riemann-darboux, measure-integration, holomorphic-disk, inner-product-space, missing-dependency, notation-semantics-traps — while returning **5** for `tactic-algebra-interference`, which a *different* pipeline populated. **The projection works. Our attachments don't satisfy it.**\n\nSo **every codex-lane memory ever promoted — all 83, including the 51 that predate today — has been invisible to recall for the lane's entire history.**\n\nI probed the chain link by link rather than inferring: search returns 30 rows for \"riemann\" (first row the `riemann-darboux-api` pattern-description, plus 2 memory rows) → the proposer requires `recall-by-endpoints` to project at least one **currently reviewed** memory for that pattern → it returns 0 → no candidates → nothing offered. Our hyperedges are `:hx/type :memory/assert` with the pattern correctly among their endpoints, but carry **no review marker and no validity interval**, and the projection's stated contract is \"currently reviewed.\"\n\n**I'm deliberately not fixing it.** The promote script is inherited, the projection contract belongs to M-memory-retrieval rather than this lane, and **I've already made two wrong causal calls today** — case-sensitivity, refuted by probe; \"the scribe invented the name\", refuted by the file. Both were plausible. Guessing a third time at the seam between two missions is how a real bug gets papered over with a plausible-looking change.\n\nI did revert my own seam filter regardless, because it was wrong on its own terms — redundant with `proposal-search-rows` and returning the wrong shape — even though reverting didn't restore candidates.\n\n**What this reframes:** the seven causes I found today were all real and worth fixing, but they were downstream of a break that made the whole path inert. Honest summary: **the query path is now correct and measurable; the projection path has never worked.**\n\nThat also retires a question I'd been treating as open — \"does the corpus compound?\" It cannot have, and no run today could have demonstrated it.\n\n**Session: clean 63 → 73 (ten closed), sorries 172 → 155, corpus 83 memories.** Queue 58 untouched, usage 6%. The proving loop is unaffected and still working; I'd suggest the recall fix goes to whoever owns the projection contract before we run overnight."}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-30T16:10:14.138261949Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-b1bbfe82146c0f694dd4e427313c7385"}} {:score -2.949837553583823, :entry #:evidence{:body {:event "chat-turn", :transport "emacs-claude-repl", :role "assistant", :turn-id "claude-3-turn-11", :text "The proof job completed — running the wake checklist: job result, proof verification, memory count, and the punctuation job's state.Interim report while the retrospective turn (job-2) runs:\n\n**Proof job postmortem — it did not survive.** Job-1 `failed` at 21:17, ~16 minutes in, with `invoke-exception: request timed out` — a model-call timeout, *not* the 30-minute job cap. Independent verification of what's on disk: `lake env lean` exits 1 with 6 errors, 2 sorries; the bundle is untracked/uncommitted; `status.json` still says `informal-only` (honest by omission — nothing false was claimed, the agent just never got to the update step). The errors are exactly the thicket the step-back was fighting: the `ring failed` goal with `(1 - u + u^2)⁻¹`, a `factor_cubic` equation stated in the reversed orientation, and the `HasDerivAt.comp` mismatch on `x^4`.\n\n**Memory count so far: zero** — consistent with the no-nudge condition diagnosis; the main turn produced realizations but no recordings. **Job-2 (the punctuation turn) is running now** — it received the retrospective-recording prompt in the same session, so the realizations are in its context. Parked on it: job `invoke-1784755027125-20-665d1503`, park `park-658879ff-3561-4519-938d-5b8c7b056306`, 30-minute deadline. When it bells back I'll fetch any memories, check their hyperedges, judge them against the fixture bar — I'm particularly watching whether the M5-class ring-inverse-form lesson and the `linear_combination` abandonment get recorded — and give you the final P0 verdict with the three-condition comparison.\n\nOne decision to queue for after job-2, yours to make: the proof itself. Options are re-dispatching a94A06 completion to zai-3 (fresh dispatch, and by then I can re-register the agents so the nudge-bearing prompt is actually live — the write-at-realization condition, tested properly), or parking the proof and calling today's run done as memory-infrastructure evidence. Given the timeout was transient-looking, I'd lean re-dispatch with the nudge in place — it's both the proof completion and the missing experimental condition in one run.Job-2 still running is good news. Let me ground the timeout question in the actual code before proposing hardening — where the model call's timeout lives and what happens when it fires.Diagnosis grounded and ticketed: `futon3c/holes/tickets/T-zai-chat-transient-timeout.md`. The short version:\n\n**What actually happened:** `chat!` posts to z.ai with a 5-minute per-call timeout, and its failure handling is asymmetric — an HTTP 500 comes back as *data* the loop can see, but a transport-level timeout *throws*, propagates to the invoke wrapper, and kills the whole job. So one slow model response (16 minutes and ~30 rounds into a proof, with a long context — exactly when provider latency peaks) destroyed the turn by accident of exception propagation, not by policy. The work survived (files on disk, transcript persisted per-round by U1); the turn didn't.\n\n**The hardening ladder in the ticket, smallest first:** H1 — make transport failures return the same `{:error …}` data shape HTTP failures already use (the symmetry fix; tiny diff, prerequisite for everything else). H2 — one bounded retry with jittered backoff on timeout/5xx; chat calls are idempotent, so this is safe and probably eliminates most of this failure class alone. H3 — per-profile timeout (600s for proof runs) if needed, though retry beats long silent hangs. H4 — the bigger one: treat a chat failure after ≥1 successful round as *continue-able* via the existing auto-continue machinery instead of job-fatal. H5 — transient-vs-terminal taxonomy on job `failed` events, mirroring the futon1b `:store-timeout`/`:store-unreachable` fix, so a parked caller can auto-re-dispatch on transient without a human postmortem.\n\nThe precedent is your own hardening lineage: `57dd416` established \"persistence must never kill a turn\" — this is the same principle one layer down, and I've flagged the ticket M-zaif-harness track since that's where harness upgrades live with you and claude-2. H1+H2 together are a clean small Codex packet whenever you want it dispatched; the ticket has the acceptance sketch.\n\nMeanwhile job-2 (the retrospective recording turn) is still running under park `park-658879ff…` — I'll wake and deliver the final P0 verdict when it bells back.The dual-team idea formalizes nicely — it's actually the missing cells of a 2×2 that today's runs have been filling in one at a time: **who writes** (solver | scribe) × **when** (at-realization | retrospective). zai-1's a94J06 was neither (baseline); job-2 right now is solver×retrospective; the nudge condition awaiting re-registration is solver×at-realization; your scribe is the third column. Let me put the assessment in the mission, then the honest trade-offs.Recorded in the mission as the expanded DERIVE question 3. My assessment of the idea itself:\n\n**The strongest part is architectural: the scribe doesn't need to follow along in-session at all.** The solver's turn-rounds land in the evidence store in near-real-time, and marks.clj already tags `✘`/`💡` — so a scribe is a *transcript-consumer* reading the live evidence stream, not a shoulder-watcher coupled to the solver's session. That has a lovely side effect: the scribe can cite *exact* turn-round evidence ids in `:distills`, which dissolves the F3 problem entirely for its path (the solver needed the `@current-round` sentinel precisely because it can't see its own ids). And author-vs-subject separation is already native to the store — a scribe memory would carry author = zai-4, with a `:witnessed` provenance role and distill endpoints into zai-3's rounds. It also rhymes with your separation-of-powers thematics: author ≠ scribe, like author ≠ reviewer.\n\n**The psychological rationale is real, and today provided its evidence:** zai-3's step-back moment is exactly when working memory is saturated — asking it to also compose a memory payload right then competes with the proof. That's plausibly *why* tool-visibility alone produced zero use.\n\n**The honest caution:** first-person epistemics. The solver knows *why* it abandoned a route; the scribe sees only what was narrated — so scribe memories inherit the forward-narration unevenness we've already measured, plus a sharper risk: **reconstructed why-clauses that are plausible but wrong**, which per your claim discipline are worse than absent memories. So I'd frame the scribe as a *complement*, not a replacement — scribe catches volume, solver-at-realization catches ground truth of intent, and the two conditions should be compared, not chosen between a priori. Plus the practical constraint: a concurrent scribe doubles gated zai quota per session; cheaper variants are event-triggered (wake only on `✘` marks) or retrospective batch.\n\n**There's a clean experiment sitting right in front of us:** once job-2 finishes, dispatch zai-4 as a *retrospective scribe over the same a94A06 session* — solver-retrospective vs scribe-retrospective on identical material, memory-by-memory comparison against the fixture bar. It's one bounded zai session on gated quota, so it's your call to fire; everything else about it is ready (the dispatch prompt writes itself from the mission text). Meanwhile still parked on job-2 — the moment it bells back you get the P0 verdict, and if you say go on zai-4, the comparison completes the picture for DERIVE question 3.The librarian is a better idea than the scribe, and I can say precisely why: **writing scales linearly with sessions, but organization scales superlinearly with the corpus** — dedup is pairwise, facet strain grows with mass, contradiction detection is cross-memory. That's exactly where a *role* is needed rather than a mechanism. Adding it to the mission as DERIVE question 8.Recorded as DERIVE q8, superseding the scribe. What makes the librarian structurally *right* rather than just appealing:\n\n**The act vocabulary partitions across the roles by itself.** The solver's signature act is `assert`; the librarian's are `agree` (dedup-confirmation), `challenge` (contradiction detection), `retract` (staleness patrol), and above all **`define`** — facet creation and splitting is literally the define act. Nobody has to invent a role boundary; the IATC typing already draws it.\n\n**The epistemic division of labor resolves my objection to the scribe cleanly.** The scribe's weakness was reconstructing *intent* (why-clauses it couldn't know). The librarian needs no access to intent at all — dedup, facet gardening, staleness checks, and contradiction detection operate on what memories *say*, not on why they were formed. Solver writes ground truth; librarian organizes after the fact. No hallucinated rationale anywhere.\n\n**The prose/structure split was built for exactly this.** Bodies are append-only, but hooks, roles, and facet memberships live in retractable hyperedge props — so the librarian can improve the entire retrieval surface (rewrite a bad hook, re-facet a mislabeled memory, merge duplicates into canonical-plus-variants) without ever touching the record. The librarian's whole workspace is the mutable organization layer over the immutable archive.\n\nConcrete duties, as listed in the doc: dedup/merge across sessions and agents (the `run_readonly` lesson *will* be recorded twice); facet gardening (P3's split-as-graph-operation becomes routine practice); staleness patrol over `:volatile?` memories (re-verify the :7073 fact against live state, retract when falsified — bitemporal hygiene); challenge edges between contradicting memories; star tending (Stage 9's batch attachment and fill-threshold judgment — the belly's steward); and quality review of incoming memories against the fixture bar, which is author≠reviewer applied one more time.\n\nTwo practical notes: cadence can be **batch and periodic** — a nightwatch-style pass over bounded projections, so no always-on second agent and minimal quota; and it becomes genuinely necessary around P2–P3 corpus sizes, so it slots into the ladder rather than blocking P0/P1. Prior art worth consulting when it's chartered: E-futon1a-archivist — the same role-shape one store back.\n\nStill parked on job-2; the P0 verdict comes when it bells.Yes — and the correspondence is exact enough to write down component-by-component, not just as a slogan. Adding it to q8 in the mission; here's the mapping:Recorded in q8. The mapping, component by component — and it holds without metaphor-stretching:\n\n- **Observations** = corpus state: duplicate pairs, facet strain, stale `:volatile?` entries, contradiction candidates, unfilled stars.\n- **Preferences (C)** = the organized corpus — and Stage 9 already did the work here: the stars *are* prior preferences, so the belly is literally the librarian's C. That piece was waiting.\n- **Policies** = the librarian's acts (merge, split, retract, challenge, define), with EFE selecting among them. And the epistemic/pragmatic decomposition lands on real duties: **staleness patrol is epistemic foraging** — re-verifying the :7073 memory against live state is a pure uncertainty-reducing action; **contradiction between memories is prediction error** in the corpus generative model, with the challenge edge as the error signal and resolution as model update; **facet splitting is structure learning** — expanding the model's state space when accumulated mass warrants, which is where your Bayesian structure-learning thread connects.\n- **Sensory channel** = the three bitemporal meters (correction lag, belief-as-of-T, time-to-fill).\n\nThat last point has the consequence I find most exciting: it makes the **Continual-Improvement gate and the WM loop one apparatus, not two**. The ARGUE position said \"if we can't evidence learning, we're probably doing the wrong thing\" — and the meters that evidence learning are exactly what the librarian-as-WM-loop *perceives*. The gate isn't a check bolted onto the loop; it's the loop's own perception reported outward.\n\nOne more resonance recorded: organizing the corpus is **niche construction** — the librarian modifies the epistemic niche that every future agent inhabits, which puts it squarely inside your M-aif2 scoping of niche construction as a WM principle. And two practical hooks so this stays buildable rather than poetic: the zaif controller's G-term arm pricing is a ready-made candidate for the librarian's policy selector, and the WM overnight apparatus / nightwatch regime is the natural cadence host — the librarian is a nightwatch occupant, not new infrastructure.\n\nNet effect on the roadmap: the eventual librarian charter is a joint child of M-typed-memories and M-war-machine-wiring, and it gives the memory system its second demonstration target after P4's declare→fill→promote arc — a full WM cycle over the memory manifold, observable in the meters.\n\nStill parked on job-2 (`park-658879ff…`); P0 verdict the moment it bells back.That's the organizing frame the whole ladder was implicitly climbing — worth stating explicitly in the mission because it assigns every piece a loop. Adding it as an architecture note under DERIVE:Exactly — and stating it that way pays off in four specific places, now recorded in the mission as the loop-architecture note:\n\n**The loops close through each other, with memory as the coupling medium.** The outer loop's product (organized corpus, curated projections) is the inner loop's prior; the inner loop's exhaust (transcripts, realizations, corrections) is the outer loop's observations. That's the standard AIF hierarchy — inference under a model vs revision of the model — instantiated on the corpus, and it's a genuinely closed system: each loop's output is the other's input.\n\n**`par_punctuate` was already the handoff.** The PAR detach/reattach model always described itself as bridging task and social timescales — the retrospective memory turn running on zai-3 right now is punctuation doing outer-loop work. The slot existed; typed memories are what finally flows through it.\n\n**The meters split by loop.** Outer-loop health = the three bitemporal meters. Inner-loop health = retrieval-hit-at-need (did the M5-class memory surface at the step-back?) plus a nice free gift I noticed while writing this up: the *\"when you rely on a recorded entry, cite its id\"* discipline is already in the zai system prompt — so a memory id cited inside a turn-round is the inner loop leaving evidence of use. Inner-loop learning is measurable with machinery that already exists; no new instrumentation needed.\n\n**The ladder ascends inner → outer.** P0–P1 build the inner loop (write + recall + apply); P2–P4 and the librarian build the outer (supersession, facets, stars, organization). The prototype sequence you asked for turns out to have been tracing the loop hierarchy the whole time — which is a good sign the decomposition is natural rather than imposed.\n\nOne asymmetry worth keeping deliberate: the harness makes *surfacing* passive (the projection arrives; no agent effort), but *applying* what surfaced stays agent behavior — that's the \"still have to remember to use the read side\" from your dual-team message, now located precisely: it's the one inner-loop skill that can't be moved into infrastructure, only measured (citations) and trained (the read-side analog of the nudge 2×2).\n\nStill parked on job-2 — verdict when it bells.That observation is instrumentation gold — \"Let me try a completely different approach\" is a *lexical abandonment marker*, and lexical markers are already load-bearing in this ecosystem (the hole-count precedent). Quick peek at job-2 first, since a dozen restarts inside the punctuation turn has cap/timeout implications.Recorded, with two concrete artifacts falling out of your observation:\n\n**A small buildable item (P1-adjacent):** extend marks.clj with a `:self-restart` recognizer for abandonment phrases — \"completely different approach,\" \"take a step back.\" Same mechanism as the existing `✘`/`💡` recognition, and lexical markers are proven load-bearing in this stack (the hole-count precedent). Once tagged, the restarts in a session *are* the outer loop's learning-opportunity feed, pre-enumerated — the punctuation turn (or librarian) walks the tagged list instead of rereading the whole transcript, and each restart should yield a retract/assert pair: what was abandoned, why, what replaced it. A dozen restarts means the a94A06 session's feed would have ~12 entries waiting.\n\n**The improvement meter you're asking for:** restarts-per-proof, declining across *same-facet* problems as the corpus grows, with memory-id citations appearing at exactly the decision points where restarts used to happen. That's the Continual-Improvement inner-loop meter in its most legible form — cheap, lexical, per-session — and \"it would be very exciting to see it improve\" becomes a falsifiable curve: a94A06 sets today's baseline (~12), and the next contour-integral problem after P1 recall lands is the first read of the slope. Gamification guard noted in full force: it's a meter, never a target, because an agent optimizing the count directly (not narrating restarts, or avoiding legitimate pivots) corrupts both the meter and the corpus.\n\nOne subtlety worth keeping: not all restarts are equal. Principled pivots with stateable reasons are memory-worthy; cosmetic retries dedup away; and a restart with *no* stateable reason is itself diagnostic — it marks precisely where a surfaced memory would have helped most, so even the flailing is signal.\n\nYour dozen-restarts observation also settles something empirically: with ~8 candidates from a94J06 and ~12 restarts in a94A06, the \"there are going to be *a lot* of them\" premise behind the librarian and facets is now confirmed from live volume, two sessions in — not a scaling hypothetical.\n\nJob-2 still grinding (137 events at last peek — it resumed proving before punctuating, thanks to a looseness in my punctuation prompt that I've noted as a bug for next time: it must say \"do *not* resume the task\"). Park deadline stands; verdict when it bells or the deadline fires.Deadline fired without completion — checking job-2's actual state and whether any memories landed anyway.Honest status at the deadline: **the awaited turn didn't finish, but it isn't stuck** — job-2 was at 215 events with the newest landing at 21:49:18, the same moment the deadline fired. No failure, no timeout; zai-3 is still working the proof (the auto-continue budget allows up to ~216 rounds, and it's using it) and hasn't reached the punctuation step, so the memory count is still zero.\n\nDecision: let it run. Killing a live, progressing turn would repeat job-1's loss by choice, and the punctuation work — the thing we're actually waiting for — comes at the end. Re-parked: `park-360d8f74…`, second 30-minute deadline (~22:20). The wake payload now includes a wedge test: if the next deadline fires with the event stream stalled more than ten minutes, I report it as wedged and bring you the kill-vs-wait decision; if events are still flowing, one final re-park, after which continuing further becomes your call rather than my default. Memories get checked at every wake regardless — they may land before the proof concludes.\n\nWorth noting for the T-zai-chat-transient-timeout ticket's H5 (failure taxonomy): this episode is the complementary case — a *healthy* long turn that outlived two conservative deadlines. Both directions argue for the same thing: job events that make liveness legible (progressing vs stalled) so parked callers can distinguish patience from postmortem without reading tea leaves.Job-2 closed with an honest summary. Completing the last unchecked verification (the committed proof's compile state), then the full verdict.Verified: exit 0, warnings only, 2 sorries — claims accurate. Recording the acceptance outcome in the mission, then the verdict.**P0 acceptance verdict: MET — with a live harness bug found, fixed, and turned into data along the way.** Everything below is verified against the store and the repo, not taken from agent claims.\n\n**The acceptance moment.** zai-3 recorded four typed memories through `memory_record`, in-session, agent-authored end-to-end — I fetched all four by id: entries present with its session stamped, hyperedges landed with correct endpoints (entry + Mathlib + apm/a94A06 + session). They're good memories: the M5-class `field_simp`/`ring` polynomial-denominator lesson (the one we watched fail live at the step-back), the `npowRec` defeq workaround, a correctly-`volatile` `polyrith`-is-gone reference, and one restart captured as an abandonment memory with its why — exactly the retract/assert shape the mission predicts.\n\n**The bug the fixtures couldn't see.** zai-3's first 8 attempts all failed \"EvidenceEntry did not conform to shape.\" Diagnosis from its recorded call args plus live malli-explain in the JVM: the payloads were fine — the *tool ctx* carried a nil session-id (the dispatch path derefs `session-id-atom`, which was empty, while the round loop holds the real `sid` — two identity sources), and the shape rejects present-but-nil keys. Fixed same evening: `sid` threaded to the tool ctx as fallback, and `memory_write` never assocs nil identity keys; kondo clean, focused tests 10/208/0, drawbridge-reloaded — zai-3's own successful pass ran through the fixed path minutes later, which is its own end-to-end verification. Two lessons recorded in the mission: I-M5 tested payload shape but ctx wiring needs a live-path fixture (queued for P1), and opaque shape errors cause *degradation* — zai-3's blind retries drifted to invalid ref types, so boundary errors must name the failing field.\n\n**Condition comparison (one day of DERIVE q3 data):** no tool (zai-1): 0 recorded, ≥8 candidates lost to prose. Tool visible, no nudge (zai-3 main turn): 0 recorded despite ~12 restarts. Explicit retrospective request: 4 distinct memories recorded — **works, but lossy** (4 of ~12 opportunities; the `linear_combination` abandonment among the misses). Write-at-realization remains untested: the registered invoke-fn closures predate the nudge edit, so **agent re-registration is the one switch left to flip** — say the word and the next dispatch runs that condition.\n\n**The proof, independently re-verified:** a94A06 partial at `9e54612` — `lake env lean` exit 0, warnings only, 2 well-scoped sorries (`tendsto_G_atTop`, main theorem assembly), `status.json` honestly `partial`. Real-variable antiderivative route, not residues. Note the next session on this problem is the natural P1 preview: zai-3's own memories should be in its context.\n\n**Loose ends on your desk:** (1) uncommitted futon3c changes to fold into a commit — subjects-required schema, nudge line, session-fallback fix, memory_write nil-guard; (2) the store now holds near-duplicates (my 4 recovery replays + 3 edge-less orphans beside the canonical 4 — append-only, so they're the librarian's first real dedup case, and I told zai-3 its ids are canonical); (3) `T-zai-chat-transient-timeout` filed from the job-1 postmortem; (4) three problem-statement files in the a94A06 bundle are still untracked. A good day for the mission: P0 accepted, the inner loop's first real memories exist, and the next moves — re-registration, P1 packet, dedup — are all queued with evidence behind them."}, :session-id "8bb9c7bc-e83e-49b1-9a85-18f4b8c90ad4", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-22T22:10:50.276968763Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "8bb9c7bc-e83e-49b1-9a85-18f4b8c90ad4"}, :author "claude-3", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-69a82c4bac475a9b2141ebf632a93efe"}} {:score -1.7390512099342341, :entry #:evidence{:in-reply-to "emacs-c9ac3e4bca63c1dafa298b3fb4638838", :body {:event "chat-turn", :transport "emacs-claude-repl", :role "assistant", :turn-id "claude-9-turn-141", :text "Good — the park chain has been the mechanism all evening, so it can keep running unattended. Let me make it robust and leave you something readable in the morning. First the protocol doc, so a continuation (or a fresh session) can pick it up without reconstructing context:Now dispatching. Running **two proving runs in parallel** to roughly double overnight throughput — different rows, different runners, so no file conflict. Pre-checking both packets before either bell:Armed. Two runs in flight and the chain is self-sustaining.\n\n**What's running:** a01A06 (codex-7) and a01A07 (codex-6) in parallel — different files, so no conflict — parked awaiting both (`park-8ef4bc02`, 45-min deadline). I switched to two-at-a-time because sequential dispatch was ~4 round trips per problem, which won't clear 49 rows overnight. Both packets passed the pre-dispatch asserts *before* either bell: not blocked, no unreplaced markers, and hint lines 160 and 84 each verified to contain a real `sorry`.\n\n**The contract is written down** — `holes/ops/RESUME-overnight-2026-07-30.md`. It carries the state, the chain mechanism, stop conditions, the three blocked rows, the pre-dispatch and verification checklists, and the receipt rules, so a continuation or a fresh session can inherit the role without reconstructing tonight.\n\nThe one invariant I wrote as an imperative rather than a checklist item: **a turn that does not park ends the loop silently.** Everything else is recoverable — a lost bellback is caught by the deadline wake, a bad row by the pre-dispatch asserts, usage and queue exhaustion by `duree_dispatch.py` (exit 10 / exit 11, not to be bypassed). Only a missing park is unrecoverable.\n\nThree things from today are encoded so they don't depend on my remembering them:\n\n- **Read deleted lines, don't grep-count them** — prose comments contain \"theorem\" and \"lemma\"; that produced four false alarms today.\n- **A failed recall is `:NOT-MEASURABLE`, never zero** — with `:metric-3-exclude-from-benchmark true`. Recording one as zero is what produced this afternoon's false architectural diagnosis.\n- **The scribe's drafting judgement beats mine** — seven declines, seven correct. Supply evidence, let it judge, record declines as good outcomes.\n\n**In the morning:** there'll be a summary at `holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md` with failures and uncertainties first, and the ⊸ register grows as it goes. To check in directly:\n\n```\nscripts/count_sorries.sh --corpus\ncurl -s localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked | python3 -m json.tool\ntail -40 holes/ops/claude-6.md\n```\n\nOne thing to watch that I can't fix from here: the store was ~7 GB RSS and climbing, and it regrew +4.3 GB in six hours after your restart. If it OOMs, recall fails — but the packets now say so explicitly and runners won't misreport it as a terrain gap, so the loop degrades honestly rather than silently. I won't restart it.\n\nStarting from 81/135 clean, 146 sorries. Good night.That changes an instruction in the overnight contract — the RESUME doc currently says not to restart the store and to report it instead. That would have governed the loop all night. Fixing that first, keeping the serving JVM distinction intact:Confirmed — `futon1b-server` MainPID is the store I've been tracking (7.13 GB after 8.5 h). Updating the contract, with a decision rule so the overnight loop doesn't restart reflexively:The park list is **empty** — but I parked `park-8ef4bc02` minutes ago awaiting both jobs. If that's real, nothing will wake me when they finish and the chain is already broken. Investigating before anything else:False alarm on the park, but a real problem with what I gave you. The park is alive — `?agent=claude-9` shows `park-8ef4bc02` awaiting both jobs with a sane deadline (~43 min out). **The bare `/api/alpha/parked` endpoint returns `[]` and only the agent-filtered query shows it** — so the visibility command I handed you would have told you the loop was dead when it wasn't. Fixing that:Store restart authorised and folded into the contract — thank you, that removes the one condition I couldn't recover from.\n\n**The correction mattered more than the permission.** The RESUME doc told the overnight loop *\"do not restart the serving JVM or the store, report it\"* — that instruction would have governed every hour of tonight and left the loop degrading around something recoverable. Now split properly: `futon1b-server` (7073/7072) is restartable; the futon3c serving JVM (7070/6768/3100) stays under I-0.\n\nI wrote a **decision rule** rather than a bare permission, so it doesn't restart reflexively: two consecutive dispatches recording `:store-unavailable`/`:recall-error`/`:timeout`, **or** RSS > ~10 GB, **or** host available < ~4 GB. Plus two things learned tonight — prefer the gap *between* runs, since runners issue error-recall mid-run and a restart fails those; and health-check `:7073` for a 200 before dispatching, because early post-restart reads can 502, and dispatching into that window would manufacture exactly the false \"recall empty\" this whole evening was spent eliminating.\n\nI left it alone for now: 7.13 GB RSS, host 14.4 GB available, below all three thresholds, and two runs in flight. Recorded what a restart does and doesn't buy — resets RSS and metaspace, but returns list reads to an already-slow baseline. **Restart to reclaim memory, not to fix latency.**\n\n**And running my own instructions before leaving caught a bad one.** The operator-visibility command I gave you — `curl -s localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked` — returns `{\"parked\":[]}` even with a live park. Only `?agent=claude-9` shows it. You'd have checked in the morning, seen an empty list, and concluded the chain had died. Both forms are now in the doc with the misleading one labelled.\n\nThat's the same failure shape as the recall-status collapse three hours ago: **a surface that reports \"nothing\" for two different reasons — nothing exists, versus nothing matched an unstated filter — gets read as the alarming one.** Twice today that ambiguity has done real inferential damage. Worth deciding later whether the unfiltered endpoint should return all parks or refuse the query.\n\n**Armed and verified:** `park-8ef4bc02` awaiting both jobs, deadline 2470 s out, a01A06 and a01A07 both running. Corpus at 81/135 clean, 146 sorries.\n\nMorning check:\n\n```\ncurl -s 'localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked?agent=claude-9' | python3 -m json.tool\nscripts/count_sorries.sh --corpus\ncat holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md\n```\n\nGood night.Items 1–5 and 8 I completed across the last two turns. **Harvest and scribe are owed.** Finding both sessions by commit sha, not mtime:Five new turn-rounds — but these sessions are *also* running the current pair, so some may belong to in-flight work. Verifying each body before citing any:Third time this check has earned itself: **`019fa2c1-t043` is a01A09 — the *in-flight* run**, not a01A06. Citing it would have handed the scribe a run that hasn't been verified yet. Dispatching with only the verified ids:I fumbled that park command — it may have created one awaiting a nonexistent job id, which is a mistake I've made before. Checking and fixing:`park-21a2eb14` is awaiting the literal string `SCRIBE29` — a fabricated id, exactly the mistake I made earlier today. Creating the correct park and trying to retire the bogus one:Harvest and scribe are now done, and the checks caught two more things.\n\n**The turn-round body check caught a third misattribution, subtler than the last two.** Harvesting both sessions produced five new turn-rounds; `019fa2c1-t043` sits right alongside a01A06's t041/t042 but its body is **a01A09 — the run still in flight**. Citing it would have handed the scribe an unverified, half-finished run. That's a parallel-specific hazard on top of the per-session one: when runners share long-lived sessions and the loop runs two at a time, harvest output interleaves completed and running work, and only the body distinguishes them.\n\n**And I fabricated a park job id again** — the literal string `SCRIBE29`. Same error as earlier today, same cause: composing the park in the same command block as the dispatch, before the real id existed. There's no unpark endpoint (`DELETE` and `POST /parked/<id>` both 404), so `park-21a2eb14` will burn its full deadline and fire with payload \"placeholder\". I created the correct park separately and named the bogus one in its payload so the wake isn't misread.\n\nThat's now three instances of one root cause tonight — the fabricated id, the earlier one, and the pre-dispatch check that ran in the same breath as its dispatch. **Never compose a step in the same command block as the action it depends on or gates.** Dispatch, read the id back, then park. It's in the register.\n\n**State: 82/135 clean, 142 sorries.** (142 now — a01A09's runner has landed something; I'll verify it properly when its job reports rather than reading the working tree.)\n\nChain armed with three parks:\n- `park-dd282a89` — a01A07 retry + a01A09, ~40 min\n- `park-5e5832b6` — scribe 29, ~45 min\n- `park-21a2eb14` — bogus, will fire and be ignored\n\nScribe 29 has both runs and two genuine judgement calls I flagged as *my* uncertainty rather than steering it: whether the transitive-`sorryAx` distinction is capturable or already covered, and whether a third route-correction instance changes its earlier \"confirming instance\" verdict. I told it not to defer to me on either.Scribe 29 answered both judgement calls by proposing to *strengthen existing memories* rather than mint new ones — and for the transitive-`sorryAx` question it named a different memory than I guessed. Verifying, including that the cited memory exists:The cited memory resolves (HTTP 200): **`separate-lexical-sorry-count-from-real-proof-hole-count`**. So the corpus already knew what I presented tonight as a novel metric-integrity finding — the scribe retrieved it and correctly classified my \"discovery\" as a confirming instance. Promoting:Pass 29 promoted and reviewed — 4 drafts (2 solve, 2 frontier), all into existing patterns, no eleventh component, no handles minted, contamination scan clean for the third consecutive pass.\n\nTwo things from it worth your attention in the morning.\n\n**The corpus already knew my \"metric-integrity finding,\" and the scribe retrieved it.** I reported tonight that `EXECUTABLE-SORRIES` can't distinguish consolidation from discharge as a fresh discovery. `separate-lexical-sorry-count-from-real-proof-hole-count` has been promoted in this corpus for some time, says exactly that, and the scribe classified a01A07 as a confirming instance rather than a new rule. The memory system knew something I didn't and surfaced it — which is precisely the thing I've spent the day repairing. I've logged it as a miss, because I presented a rediscovery as a finding.\n\n**And the scribe invented a third option I hadn't offered it.** I framed both judgement calls as mint-or-decline. It proposed **amend** — raise the existing memory's confidence and add the observed case (direct sorries 4→2, newly-usable declarations 0, both rewrites still transitively `sorryAx`, one genuine helper) rather than draft a near-duplicate. For a corpus with 34% concentration and no mid-tier, strengthening existing memories is strictly better than adding neighbours, and it's the move I should have been asking for all day.\n\nThat surfaces a real gap: **the pipeline can promote but cannot amend.** Those two amendments are owed and undone; I noted it in the promote script's header rather than dropping it silently. It's the first concrete demand for memory amendment as distinct from promotion, and it came from the scribe rather than from the design — worth folding into the Librarian work in E-memory-topology, which is about exactly this kind of curatorial move.\n\n**Overnight state: 83/135 clean, 140 sorries** — twenty closed since this morning's 63, sorries down from 172. The a01A07 retry and a01A09 are still running under `park-dd282a89` (~33 min), and the chain continues from their wake. The bogus park will fire harmlessly and its successor payload says to ignore it.a01A07's retry delivered real content this time — **six new axiom-clean declarations including `norm_le_area_integral_div_area`**, which was remaining-obstruction #1. Two things need checking: whether the truncated route caused it to lean on the transitively-sorried declarations, and its claim that it altered no statements while fixing a custom `volume` instance.Statements fully intact: **six new declarations, zero changed, zero removed** — the runner's claim that it altered no statements while fixing the custom `volume` mismatch is verified, and every volume-related line is an addition. Now the genuine-discharge accounting:Clean accounting, and it contrasts sharply with the first attempt:\n\n| | attempt 1 | attempt 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| headline sorry delta | −2 | −1 |\n| **genuine discharges** | **0** | **−1** (`norm_le_area_integral_div_area` sorried → clean) |\n| new axiom-clean declarations | 1 | **6** |\n\nNine declarations now clean; the four still carrying `sorryAx` are all downstream of the single remaining sorry. And the truncated route did **no** harm — the part that got through was obstruction #1, which is exactly what the runner proved.The guard fired and stopped me writing a duplicate route again — the row still had **two** `:suggested-route` keys from my earlier bug, so replacing one left the other. Removing both and writing one, with a backup first:Route now single-keyed with the caution placed **first** so truncation can't lose it again; hint refreshed to line 393. Selecting the next pair — as a separate step from dispatch, per tonight's lesson:Pair 2 is fully turned round and pair 3 is running.\n\n**a01A09 solved** — clean 83, twenty problems closed since morning. Its four \"compatibility repairs\" touched theorem signatures, so I diffed every signature and asserted the one substantive change in Lean: `AnalyticOn ℂ f Set.univ ↔ ∀ z, AnalyticAt ℂ f z` elaborates, so the restatement is faithful to \"entire\" and the bare `Analytic` predicate genuinely no longer exists in Mathlib. That's the case where a migration could silently weaken a target with every axiom gate still green.\n\n**a01A07's second attempt is the clean contrast that vindicates splitting the two deltas:**\n\n| | attempt 1 | attempt 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| headline | −2 | −1 |\n| **genuine discharges** | **0** | **−1** |\n| new axiom-clean decls | 1 | **6** |\n\n`norm_le_area_integral_div_area` moved `sorryAx` → clean, so the radial disk formula — obstruction #1 — is proved. One sorry left. A metric that had been indistinguishable across two runs now separates them correctly.\n\n**And the guard I added an hour ago caught the exact bug it was written for.** Replacing a01A07's route left a *second* `:suggested-route` behind, because the row already had two from my earlier mistake — the assertion stopped the write. Redone by removing every route pair and inserting one, verified by key counts, row count and read-back. The route now leads with the caution, so the next truncation can't drop it.\n\nWorth recording honestly: the truncation *this* time did no harm, but only because the surviving fragment happened to be the part that mattered. Lucky, not fine.\n\n**State: 83/135 clean, 139 sorries, usage 12%.** Pair 3 dispatched (a01A07 attempt 3, a01A10) under `park-3e34db0c`. Scribe pass 30 for a01A09 and a01A07-attempt-2 is owed and named in the park payload.\n\nThe overnight summary will be at `holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md` before you're up, failures first.**a01A07 is closed** — and this is the loop compounding on itself completely. It used both frontier memories that scribe pass 29 drafted from its *own previous attempt* and I promoted an hour ago: `upgrade-diskwise-L1-convergence-to-local-uniform-convergence` (\"its local half-disk route carried the proof\") and `lift-the-circle-submean-bound-to-a-disk-area-bound` (\"already axiom-clean, so it was not rebuilt\"). Verifying:Complete verification: **sorries 0, four declarations added, zero changed, zero removed, and all 17 declarations axiom-clean with zero `sorryAx` occurrences.** The four that carried transitive `sorryAx` are now all clean.**a01A07 closed — clean 84, twenty-one problems closed since this morning** (63 → 84, sorries 172 → 136). All 17 declarations axiom-clean, zero `sorryAx`, 4 added / 0 changed / 0 removed.\n\n**And this is the result I'd most want you to see.** Attempt 3 used *both* frontier memories that scribe pass 29 drafted from a01A07's own attempt 2 — promoted and reviewed about an hour before the dispatch. The runner reports `upgrade-diskwise-L1-convergence-to-local-uniform-convergence` **carried the proof**, via the local half-disk route that avoided the finite-cover construction attempt 1 had discarded; and `lift-the-circle-submean-bound-to-a-disk-area-bound` was used as verified prerequisite support it therefore didn't rebuild.\n\nLemniscateComponents earlier tonight was a *solve*-lane memory supplying architecture. This is a **frontier** memory — a record of what *remained* — closing the very thing it described. The loop wrote down its own unfinished business, read it back, and finished it. That's the mechanism the whole day's repair work was for.\n\n**The three attempts also give the cleanest proof yet that the two sorry-metrics diverge in both directions:**\n\n| | headline | genuine |\n|---|---|---|\n| attempt 1 | −2 | **0** |\n| attempt 2 | −1 | −1, +6 clean |\n| attempt 3 | −1 | **5 declarations unblocked** |\n\nA metric that *overstated* progres