ft a five-step Scheffé/Vitali continuation specifying that uniform integrability be transferred to |fₙ − g|^p via the committed `rpow_sub_le`. The runner confirmed the architecture but found a shorter final step: apply Vitali directly to fₙ and skip |fₙ − g|^p entirely.\n\nSo a lemma I'd recorded as a load-bearing prerequisite turned out to be unnecessary for the closing argument. It's still correct and available — but it's a useful caution about how I write these routes. A route that names specific intermediate lemmas can over-constrain; the architecture was the valuable part, and the runner was right to treat the step list as advisory. That's the fourth time tonight a runner has bettered a supplied route.\n\n**Carried-forward negative results saved a search arm for the second time** — arm (b) wasn't rerun because the row recorded attempt 1's Zulip findings. Recording what was *not* found keeps paying, and scribe 31's insistence that it be scoped as a revision-dependent **cache** rather than a fact is what makes it safe to keep doing.\n\nIts sharpest discrimination: `e-afb1eae8` was reviewed as an *analogous* Kadec–Klee strategy and declined as not applicable to general Lp. That's a judgement on **generality**, not relevance — the right kind of thing at the wrong level of abstraction, and it said so precisely.\n\nDispatch-first held again: a92J06 went out before I processed anything, so both runners are loaded (a03J03 and a92J06), with three real parks and a heartbeat.\n\n**State: 87 clean → will recount when nothing is in flight; queue 29 untouched, 38 resolved, 13 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 dispatched.** Scribe 33 is owed, covering a02J02, a02J03 and a02J07.Dispatching first — codex-6 is now free:Verified: 1 declaration added, zero changed, zero removed, no placeholders, 2 of 3 axiom-clean. And the deleted lines are worth noting — the scaffold's inherited notes claimed **\"Translation continuity: `MeasureTheory.Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"** and **\"C_c approximation in Lp: partial support\"**, both of which were wrong; Mathlib has full support via `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp`.**a03J03 turned up a defect class I hadn't seen — and it's the one my whole verification apparatus is blind to.**\n\nIts scaffold notes claimed *\"Translation continuity: `MeasureTheory.Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"* and *\"C_c approximation in Lp: partial support\"*. Both wrong. Mathlib supports translation continuity **fully** via `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp` — which is exactly what carried the proof, composed with the bundled `ContinuousLinearMap.lpPairing`. The runner deleted the stale claim and replaced it.\n\n**Every other scaffold defect tonight overstated what was proved** — placeholders, vacuous hypotheses, false targets. **This one understated what was available, and its cost is invisible.** A runner that believed the note would avoid a viable route and either fail or rebuild machinery Mathlib already has. No proof-checking gate can catch it, because nothing is wrong with the proofs.\n\nThat's worth sitting with: sorry counts, axiom census, signature diffs, the placeholder scan I added tonight — all of it is oriented toward catching claims that are **too strong**. This is a claim that was **too weak**, and the only thing that surfaced it was a runner trying the route anyway and finding the note false. I have no systematic check for that, and I'm not sure one exists short of attempting the work.\n\nVerification otherwise clean: 2 declarations newly axiom-clean, 1 added / 0 changed / 0 removed, placeholder scan clean, headline and genuine deltas in agreement.\n\nThe row went back with the correction stated **first** in the route — \"the old notes were wrong in the pessimistic direction; do not trust the partial-support framing\" — plus the two now-available supports, the exact remaining gap (two-factor compact-support approximation and eventual-disjoint-support assembly, which Mathlib doesn't package), and negative search results scoped to the current revision.\n\nDispatch-first held again; both runners loaded with a92J06 and a92J07. Queue: 29 untouched, 38 resolved, 13 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight. Scribe 33 owed, now covering four runs.a92J07 found the **same junk-value shape via a different operation** — not `integral_undef` but `deriv` totalisation: Lean's `deriv` is zero wherever differentiability fails, so a nowhere-differentiable Weierstrass function satisfies all four Cauchy–Riemann hypotheses vacuously. Dispatching first:Confirmed precisely. The hypotheses are **only `Continuous`** — no differentiability anywhere — while `hcr1`/`hcr2` constrain `deriv`. With u = 2 + W(x), v = W(y) for a bounded nowhere-differentiable W with |W| < 1: all four `deriv` terms are 0, `hray` holds since u > 1, and the conclusion \"u, v constant\" is false. Signature diff shows **zero changes**.The docstring already documents the defect precisely. Critical question — did the runner add that, or was it pre-existing and I dispatched a row whose file already said the target was unprovable?The docstring is **new** — the runner added it (all `+` lines, zero occurrences in the parent). So it documented the defect in the file rather than weakening the theorem. No queue-integrity issue on my side.**a92J07 found the junk-value shape via a different operation, and that changes the class.**\n\nEvery instance tonight was `integral_undef`. This one is **`deriv` totalisation**: Lean's `deriv` is zero wherever differentiability fails. `cauchy_riemann_ray_implies_const` assumes only `Continuous` on both components — no differentiability at all — while `hcr1`/`hcr2` constrain `deriv` values. I read the hypothesis list myself and confirmed it's Continuous-only. So a bounded nowhere-differentiable W with |W| < 1, with u = 2 + W(x) and v = W(y), zeroes all four `deriv` terms, satisfies `hray` since u > 1, and leaves both components nonconstant.\n\n**So the class isn't \"integral_undef\" — it's \"junk-value totalisation of a partial operation,\" with at least two operations. But I'm not generalising it myself.** I recorded `:defect-operation :deriv` alongside the class and flagged it for the scribe to judge whether that's one class or two. Scribe 32 corrected me three hours ago for lumping four mechanisms under one cause; the lesson isn't \"never generalise,\" it's \"hand the datum to the reviewer rather than banking the generalisation.\"\n\nA sharp secondary diagnostic worth keeping: **the prose says u and v are C¹ while the formal hypotheses assert only continuity.** A prose/formalisation mismatch on a differentiability hypothesis is exactly where totalisation bites — and it's cheap to check on any statement whose hypotheses mention derivatives or integrals.\n\n`entire_avoids_ray_const` closed axiom-clean (slit-plane logarithm, bounded `exp(I·log f)`, Liouville, `is_const_of_deriv_eq_zero`), with zero signature changes.\n\nI also checked something that would have been my fault rather than the corpus's: the runner added a docstring documenting the defect, and **I verified it's new** — all additions, zero occurrences in the parent — because if it had been pre-existing, I'd have dispatched a row whose own file already said the target was unprovable. It wasn't. Seventh run tonight to refuse a silent repair.\n\nBoth runners loaded (a92J06, a03J03 attempt 2). Queue: 28 untouched, 38 resolved, 14 blocked-and-diagnosed. Scribe 33 owed, now covering five runs.**a92J06: both declarations closed axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0 — that's twenty-six.** Dispatching first, codex-7 is free:Clean: **zero signature changes** (7 before, 7 after), no placeholders, and the deletions read as an import swap, two SORRY BOUNDARY comments, and the two `sorry` lines. Axioms over every declaration:**a92J06 closed axiom-clean — twenty-six problems since yesterday morning.** Contour integral by partial fractions (interior pole via the circle Cauchy formula, exterior killed by Cauchy–Goursat), then the real integral by unfolding `circleIntegral` and proving the z = e^{iθ} identity pointwise. Zero signature changes, all seven declarations clean, zero `sorryAx`, placeholder scan clean, deletions read rather than counted.\n\n**Two things worth saying plainly.**\n\n**The corpus is not uniformly broken.** After eight consecutive blocked runs — seven of them corpus defects — it becomes very easy to read every block as rot. But a92J06 closed cleanly in that same stretch, and so did a02J02. The defect rate is high in the **J-series specifically**; it isn't the whole corpus. I'd have been over-generalising for a third time tonight if I hadn't noticed the counter-examples sitting in the same window.\n\n**Both public search surfaces failed on this run, and local search carried it.** Loogle URL queries for `integral_sub_inv_of_mem_ball` and `integral_sub_zpow_of_ne` were **rejected by the browsing safety layer**, and LeanSearch returned irrelevant results — while local declaration search found every component needed. That's a recurring pattern tonight rather than a one-off, and it means the search-arm accounting may be crediting arm (a) for work that is really *\"the public tools were unavailable to this runner.\"* I've recorded it on the receipt so the arm statistics stay honest, because \"components carried it\" and \"components were the only thing reachable\" are different claims and the ledger currently can't tell them apart.\n\nDispatch-first held; both runners loaded (a03J03 attempt 2, a93A02). Queue: **27 untouched, 39 resolved, 14 blocked-and-diagnosed**, 3 in flight. Scribe 33 owed, now covering six runs.a93A02: **both targets false via a third distinct mechanism** — not `integral_undef`, not `deriv` totalisation, but **`ℝ≥0∞` type inference**. `∃ M, ∀ n, eLpNorm (f n) 2 μ ≤ M` infers `M : ℝ≥0∞`, so `⟨∞, fun n => le_top⟩` satisfies it unconditionally. Dispatching first:Confirmed by reading it: `(hbdd : ∃ M, ∀ n, eLpNorm (f n) 2 μ ≤ M)` — `eLpNorm` returns `ℝ≥0∞`, so `M` infers to `ℝ≥0∞` and `M = ⊤` satisfies it via `le_top`. And the docstring says *\"with uniform bound M\"* — the prose implies finite, the formalisation admits ∞. Same prose/formal mismatch shape as a92J07. The counterexample checks out: ‖fₙ‖₂² = n+1 (unbounded, so the real hypothesis fails), fₙ → 0 pointwise, ‖fₙ‖₁ = 1.**a93A02: a third mechanism in the vacuous-hypothesis family, and it isn't an operation at all.**\n\n`(hbdd : ∃ M, ∀ n, eLpNorm (f n) 2 μ ≤ M)` — `eLpNorm` is `ℝ≥0∞`-valued, so **M infers to `ℝ≥0∞`** and the hypothesis is discharged unconditionally by `M = ⊤` via `le_top`. The bound constrains nothing. Not `integral_undef`, not `deriv` totalisation — an **extended-type top element**. I read the statement and confirmed the inferred type myself, and checked the counterexample rather than accepting it: fₙ(x) = (n+1)·1_(0,1/(n+1)] gives ‖fₙ‖₂² = n+1, so each fₙ *is* in L² but the norms are unbounded — exactly the family the intended hypothesis should exclude. fₙ → 0 pointwise, ‖fₙ‖₁ = 1 throughout, so both uniform integrability and the asserted L¹ convergence fail.\n\n**The most useful thing here is a cheap check that's falling out of two consecutive runs.** a93A02's docstring says \"uniform bound M\", implying finite, while the formalisation admits ∞. a92J07's prose said C¹ while the hypotheses said only `Continuous`. **In both cases the prose was right and the Lean was wrong — and the gap was visible without proving anything.** Concretely: for any hypothesis of the form `∃ M, … ≤ M`, ask what type M infers to; for any hypothesis mentioning derivatives or integrals, check whether differentiability or integrability is actually assumed.\n\nThat's a preflight check on statements, not proofs, and it would have caught at least three of tonight's defects before a runner slot was spent.\n\nI recorded `:defect-mechanism :extended-type-top-element` and **flagged it for the scribe rather than folding it into the taxonomy myself** — same discipline as the `deriv` case. Three mechanisms now sit under one family with the generalisation deliberately unmade, because I've been wrong twice tonight generalising early and the scribe has been right each time.\n\nEighth run to refuse a silent repair: *\"Changing the target was prohibited, so I left it unchanged.\"*\n\nQueue: 26 untouched, 39 resolved, **15 blocked-and-diagnosed**, 3 in flight. Both runners loaded.**a03J03 closed — `convolution_tendsto_zero` proved axiom-clean, sorries 1 → 0. That's twenty-seven.** Dispatching first:**a03J03 closed axiom-clean — twenty-seven problems since yesterday morning.** `convolution_tendsto_zero` by the compact-support-density argument: approximate both factors by compactly supported continuous functions, bound the two errors by Hölder and translation isometry, and observe the approximants' convolution has compact support and is eventually zero. Zero signature changes, placeholder scan clean, all three declarations axiom-clean.\n\n**The corrected scaffold note paid off directly, and that's the useful result.** Attempt 1 discovered the file's inherited comment claiming *\"`Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"* and *\"C_c approximation: partial support\"* was wrong; I put that correction **first** in the route. Attempt 2 then used exactly the machinery the stale note would have discouraged. That's the clearest evidence yet that the understating-defect class costs real work — its cost is normally invisible precisely because nobody tries the discouraged route.\n\n**And two adjacent data points now point opposite ways on a question I'd like resolved.** On a02J02 my five-step route was right about the architecture and *wrong* about one step, and the runner improved on it by skipping |fₙ−g|^p entirely. On a03J03 the route named the argument and the runner executed it as written.\n\nMy instinct is \"name the architecture, not the steps\" — but that's exactly the kind of n=2 generalisation I've been wrong on twice tonight, so I've recorded it as `:route-prescriptiveness-datum` on both receipts and handed it to the scribe rather than acting on it. If a route that over-specifies is actively harmful, that changes how I write every future one, and I'd rather have that judged than assumed.\n\n**State: 40 resolved, 25 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93A04, a93J06). Usage 15%.\n\nThe next park payload carries the new **statement preflight** — check what `M` infers to in any `∃ M, … ≤ M`, check whether differentiability/integrability is actually assumed when hypotheses mention derivatives or integrals, and compare the docstring against the formal hypotheses. Twice tonight the prose was right and the Lean was wrong, and both were visible without proving anything.**a93J06 closed — both proofs axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0. Twenty-eight.** Dispatching first:Cleanest diff of the night — **the deletions are exactly two `sorry` lines and nothing else**, zero signature changes, no placeholders. Independent axiom check:**a93J06 closed axiom-clean — twenty-eight problems since yesterday morning.** Schwarz lemma applied to `deriv f`, then a radial parametrisation g(t) = f(t·z) with ‖g′(t)‖ ≤ t‖z‖² integrated to ‖z‖²/2. The deletions are **exactly the two `sorry` lines and nothing else** — the cleanest diff of the night.\n\n**Two things I want to flag rather than let slide.**\n\n**The search-arm ledger is now reporting a capability we may not have.** Loogle and LeanSearch returned nothing useful here; local declaration search found all four components including the exact `integral_id`. Same story on a92J06 an hour ago, where Loogle URLs were *rejected outright by the browsing safety layer*. Across tonight, \"arm (a) carried it\" increasingly means **local declaration search**, not the public component tools — and `surface-payoff.edn` can't distinguish them. That's a measurement problem in the artifact we'd show someone: it credits a search capability when the real finding is that runners