cally blocked. It was blocked by build rot.** Two causes, neither of them mathematics: an unbuilt Scratch `.olean`, and `Complex.abs` removed from current Mathlib with `open scoped ComplexAnalysis` pointing at a dead namespace. Repairing that touched one helper lemma and the imports. Then the whole problem fell — higher-order Schwarz for the quadratic bound, Cauchy's estimate with r → 1⁻ for the second derivative, Schwarz equality on `dslope f 0` for rigidity.\n\nIt had sat as `:blocked-does-not-elaborate` and had **never received a proof attempt**, because a file that doesn't compile can't be dispatched.\n\n**The caveat matters as much as the result.** a01J05 and t94J01 were repaired in the same batch and turned out to hide *false statements* rather than tractable problems. So a broken build masks whatever's behind it — sometimes a solvable problem, sometimes a defect. The lesson is that it masks, not that what it masks is easy.\n\n**One first today: error-time recall supplied the decisive step.** `check-general-dslope-equality-before-building-schwarz-machinery` was surfaced *after* a compile error and directed the rigidity proof to the general affine/`dslope` API. Error-time recall returned nothing in almost every other run today.\n\n**Corpus moved: CLEAN 109 → 112, EXECUTABLE-SORRIES 46 → 37.** Queue: 57 resolved, 7 dispatched, 4 partial-continue, 4 blocked on construction targets.\n\nStanding caution on that CLEAN figure: it still counts a01J04 and the 17 problems whose Scratch dependencies are unbuildable. The number moved for real reasons today, but it remains a token count, not a verification.**UnivalentDeriv is closed** — `deriv_ne_zero_of_injOn` proved, 1→0. This is a shared library file, so full scrutiny:**Fully verified: sorries 1→0, all four declarations axiom-clean with no `sorryAx`, both gates pass, and the statement is byte-identical to the parent — not weakened to make it provable.**\n\nRe-dispatching a95J01, whose runner reduced its target to exactly this and refused to import the sorried version:**UnivalentDeriv landed clean and a95J01 is re-dispatched unblocked.**\n\nVerification held at the shared-library bar: sorries 1→0 counted against the parent, **all four** declarations axiom-clean (not just the target), both gates pass, and the statement is byte-identical — it wasn't weakened to make it provable. The route was genuine mathematics: local normal form, analytic *n*th root of the nonzero factor, locally invertible coordinate φ, then two nearby values differing by a nontrivial *n*th root of unity pull back to distinct points with equal `g` values, contradicting injectivity.\n\n**And the hint had drifted 65 lines** — `[83] → [148]`. a95J01's file gained the whole conditional half since it was last dispatched. Without the refresh this would have pointed at a closed declaration, which is the failure that skipped three rows earlier today.\n\nThe chain here is worth noting because it's the loop working as designed across four dispatches:\n\n1. a95J01 attempt 1 → reduced its target to exactly `∀ z ∈ ball 0 1, deriv g z ≠ 0`\n2. …and **refused to import** the sorried `UnivalentDeriv`, rather than inherit `sorryAx`\n3. That refusal identified the shared blocker, which I dispatched as a construction target\n4. It's now proved, and a95J01 goes back with its conditional half already done\n\nThe refusal was the load-bearing step. A runner that had imported the sorried lemma would have produced a \"closed\" target carrying `sorryAx` — and my axiom check would have caught it, but only after spending the run.\n\n**Construction-target work still in flight: Rouché** (blocking a92J05, a94A10, a97A08) and **IntegralMinkowski** (blocking a94J04, a96A04). Two lemmas, five problems.**a95A03: 4 → 0, exit 0 with no warnings.** This is the row I revoked this morning — its three targets had closed while four other sorries sat uncovered by any row. Verifying, including the declaration that was moved:**a95A03: 4 → 0, exit 0 with zero