tions, bound the two errors by Hölder and translation isometry, and observe the approximants' convolution has compact support and is eventually zero. Zero signature changes, placeholder scan clean, all three declarations axiom-clean.\n\n**The corrected scaffold note paid off directly, and that's the useful result.** Attempt 1 discovered the file's inherited comment claiming *\"`Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"* and *\"C_c approximation: partial support\"* was wrong; I put that correction **first** in the route. Attempt 2 then used exactly the machinery the stale note would have discouraged. That's the clearest evidence yet that the understating-defect class costs real work — its cost is normally invisible precisely because nobody tries the discouraged route.\n\n**And two adjacent data points now point opposite ways on a question I'd like resolved.** On a02J02 my five-step route was right about the architecture and *wrong* about one step, and the runner improved on it by skipping |fₙ−g|^p entirely. On a03J03 the route named the argument and the runner executed it as written.\n\nMy instinct is \"name the architecture, not the steps\" — but that's exactly the kind of n=2 generalisation I've been wrong on twice tonight, so I've recorded it as `:route-prescriptiveness-datum` on both receipts and handed it to the scribe rather than acting on it. If a route that over-specifies is actively harmful, that changes how I write every future one, and I'd rather have that judged than assumed.\n\n**State: 40 resolved, 25 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93A04, a93J06). Usage 15%.\n\nThe next park payload carries the new **statement preflight** — check what `M` infers to in any `∃ M, … ≤ M`, check whether differentiability/integrability is actually assumed when hypotheses mention derivatives or integrals, and compare the docstring against the formal hypotheses. Twice tonight the prose was right and the Lean was wrong, and both were visible without proving anything.**a93J06 closed — both proofs axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0. Twenty-eight.** Dispatching first:Cleanest diff of the night — **the deletions are exactly two `sorry` lines and nothing else**, zero signature changes, no placeholders. Independent axiom check:**a93J06 closed axiom-clean — twenty-eight problems since yesterday morning.** Schwarz lemma applied to `deriv f`, then a radial parametrisation g(t) = f(t·z) with ‖g′(t)‖ ≤ t‖z‖² integrated to ‖z‖²/2. The deletions are **exactly the two `sorry` lines and nothing else** — the cleanest diff of the night.\n\n**Two things I want to flag rather than let slide.**\n\n**The search-arm ledger is now reporting a capability we may not have.** Loogle and LeanSearch returned nothing useful here; local declaration search found all four components including the exact `integral_id`. Same story on a92J06 an hour ago, where Loogle URLs were *rejected outright by the browsing safety layer*. Across tonight, \"arm (a) carried it\" increasingly means **local declaration search**, not the public component tools — and `surface-payoff.edn` can't distinguish them. That's a measurement problem in the artifact we'd show someone: it credits a search capability when the real finding is that runners fall back to grepping Mathlib successfully. I've recorded it as a `:ledger-caveat` on both receipts; splitting the arm label is a small change but it's yours to schedule.\n\n**Scribe 33 is now owed over eight runs, and that's too many.** a02J02, a02J03, a02J07, a03J03, a92J06, a92J07, a93A02, a93J06 — three of which carry *distinct* defect mechanisms deserving individual judgement. A single pass over eight risks shallow treatment of each. The throughput gain from back-to-back proving runs is being paid for in scribe debt, and I've flagged it to dispatch next turn rather than let it grow further.\n\n**State: 41 resolved, 24 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes, 3 in flight, 88 rows total.** Both