n isometry, and observe the approximants' convolution has compact support and is eventually zero. Zero signature changes, placeholder scan clean, all three declarations axiom-clean.\n\n**The corrected scaffold note paid off directly, and that's the useful result.** Attempt 1 discovered the file's inherited comment claiming *\"`Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"* and *\"C_c approximation: partial support\"* was wrong; I put that correction **first** in the route. Attempt 2 then used exactly the machinery the stale note would have discouraged. That's the clearest evidence yet that the understating-defect class costs real work — its cost is normally invisible precisely because nobody tries the discouraged route.\n\n**And two adjacent data points now point opposite ways on a question I'd like resolved.** On a02J02 my five-step route was right about the architecture and *wrong* about one step, and the runner improved on it by skipping |fₙ−g|^p entirely. On a03J03 the route named the argument and the runner executed it as written.\n\nMy instinct is \"name the architecture, not the steps\" — but that's exactly the kind of n=2 generalisation I've been wrong on twice tonight, so I've recorded it as `:route-prescriptiveness-datum` on both receipts and handed it to the scribe rather than acting on it. If a route that over-specifies is actively harmful, that changes how I write every future one, and I'd rather have that judged than assumed.\n\n**State: 40 resolved, 25 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93A04, a93J06). Usage 15%.\n\nThe next park payload carries the new **statement preflight** — check what `M` infers to in any `∃ M, … ≤ M`, check whether differentiability/integrability is actually assumed when hypotheses mention derivatives or integrals, and compare the docstring against the formal hypotheses. Twice tonight the prose was right and the Lean was wrong, and both were visible without proving anything.**a93J06 closed — both proofs axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0. Twenty-eight.** Dispatching first:Cleanest diff of the night — **the deletions are exactly two `sorry` lines and nothing else**, zero signature changes, no placeholders. Independent axiom check:**a93J06 closed axiom-clean — twenty-eight problems since yesterday morning.** Schwarz lemma applied to `deriv f`, then a radial parametrisation g(t) = f(t·z) with ‖g′(t)‖ ≤ t‖z‖² integrated to ‖z‖²/2. The deletions are **exactly the two `sorry` lines and nothing else** — the cleanest diff of the night.\n\n**Two things I want to flag rather than let slide.**\n\n**The search-arm ledger is now reporting a capability we may not have.** Loogle and LeanSearch returned nothing useful here; local declaration search found all four components including the exact `integral_id`. Same story on a92J06 an hour ago, where Loogle URLs were *rejected outright by the browsing safety layer*. Across tonight, \"arm (a) carried it\" increasingly means **local declaration search**, not the public component tools — and `surface-payoff.edn` can't distinguish them. That's a measurement problem in the artifact we'd show someone: it credits a search capability when the real finding is that runners fall back to grepping Mathlib successfully. I've recorded it as a `:ledger-caveat` on both receipts; splitting the arm label is a small change but it's yours to schedule.\n\n**Scribe 33 is now owed over eight runs, and that's too many.** a02J02, a02J03, a02J07, a03J03, a92J06, a92J07, a93A02, a93J06 — three of which carry *distinct* defect mechanisms deserving individual judgement. A single pass over eight risks shallow treatment of each. The throughput gain from back-to-back proving runs is being paid for in scribe debt, and I've flagged it to dispatch next turn rather than let it grow further.\n\n**State: 41 resolved, 24 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed across seven causes, 3 in flight, 88 rows total.** Both runners loaded (a93A04, a93J07). Usage 15%, store steady.a93A04 is a **genuine Mathlib frontier** — two declarations proved axiom-clean, blocked on a real gap (Mathlib has the monotone-partition → disjoint-intervals direction but not the converse). Dispatching the proving row and **scribe 33**, which I flagged as overdue:**a93A04 is the second honest Mathlib frontier of the night, against nine defect runs.** Two declarations proved axiom-clean, and the obstruction is precisely named: `sum_edist_le_eVariationOn_of_mem_disjWithin` needs to *order* an arbitrary finite pairwise-disjoint family of unoriented `uIoc` intervals and embed its endpoint increments into a monotone variation partition. **Mathlib has only the opposite direction** — building disjoint intervals *from* a monotone partition. A named missing converse is a far better frontier record than \"blocked.\"\n\nI also checked the `sorryAx` **dependency shape** rather than just counting: three declarations clean, and the two contaminated ones are the remaining target and the `iff` that depends on it — exactly what should be affected and nothing more. That's what the transitive census is for, and it's worth saying when it comes out clean rather than only when it doesn't.\n\n**Third consecutive run where public search failed and local declaration search carried the work.** LeanSearch returned only irrelevant finite-sum material. After a92J06 (Loogle URLs rejected by the browsing safety layer) and a93J06 (no useful direct theorem), this isn't anecdotal — and the ledger still credits all of it to \"arm (a) components,\" conflating a working public tool with a runner grepping Mathlib locally. That's a measurement error in an artifact we'd show someone.\n\n**Scribe 33 is dispatched over eight runs, with the three defect runs explicitly prioritised** and permission to ration attention on the closes. I let that backlog grow, and a shallow pass over eight is worse than a deep pass over four. I've asked it to judge whether the three vacuous-hypothesis *mechanisms* are one family or separate rules, whether the statement preflight is a memory or an amendment, and the route-prescriptiveness question where a02J02 and a03J03 point opposite ways — telling it explicitly that declining any of these counts as a good answer.\n\n**State: 41 resolved, 24 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a93J07, a94A01). Usage 15%.Scribe 33 answered all three, and the answers are better than my framing on each.\n\n**(1)** One umbrella — *\"missing semantic-domain guards admit degenerate witnesses\"* — but **keep three mechanism-specific rules and repairs**. Unify the diagnosis, separate the mechanisms, because the repairs differ. Better than either of the options I offered.\n\n**(3)** It **declined my \"architecture only\" instinct** and gave a more precise structure: routes should represent stable architecture *separately from* provisional steps, with confidence and evidence per step. That's the twelfth time it has corrected me and been right.\n\nBoth runners are busy, so capacity is full. Harvesting and verifying:Checking `math/derivative-bounds-api` in the **store** rather than just the promotion scripts — that's the check I got wrong last time:Scribe 33 is fully landed, and it answered all three questions better than I framed them.\n\n**(1) The vacuous-hypothesis family gets one umbrella but three rules.** *\"Missing semantic-domain guards admit degenerate witnesses\"* unifies the diagnosis; the three mechanisms stay separate because **the repairs differ** — add integrability, require `ContDiff`, bind M in `NNReal`. That's neither option I offered. I'd framed it as one-class-or-three; the right answer was to split diagnosis from remedy.\n\n**(2)** The statement preflight is capturable and became a trajectory draft, broader than and complementing the Bochner diagnostic.\n\n**(3) It declined my \"architecture only\" instinct.** The two opposing cases support recording stable architecture *separately from* provisional steps, with confidence per step — so a runner can tell what's load-bearing from what was the last attempt's guess. That's twelfth correction, twelfth time right, and it's a better rule than mine: my version would have thrown away useful detail; theirs keeps it and labels its status.\n\n**Sixteen amendments now applied tonight**, all append-only with proposer ≠ applier. This round restructured the Bochner memory as an umbrella with three mechanism siblings carrying six problems of evidence, refined the route-override memory, and gave the installed-source-search memory **four consecutive confirmations that public search failed while local declaration search carried the work** — with the ledger caveat recorded *inside* the memory, so the conflation is documented where a future reader of the corpus will actually meet it.\n\nTwo smaller things. I checked `math/derivative-bounds-api` **in the store** this time rather than only in prior promote scripts — the too-narrow check that made me wrongly doubt a handle in pass 32. Present, 7 mentions.\n\nAnd the scribe caught something I dropped: I dispatched it *before* writing a93A04's receipt, so that run was missing from its input list. It resolved the receipt itself and noted the omission. A reviewer that repairs its own brief is worth more than one that follows it.\n\n**State: 42 resolved, 23 untouched, 15 blocked-and-diagnosed.** Both runners loaded (a93J07, a93A04 attempt 2). Usage 16%.**a93J07: all three Hurwitz declarations closed axiom-clean, sorries 3 → 0. Thirty.** One flag though — it reports an unused-parameter warning for `hz₀`, and unused hypotheses are exactly how a95J03's vacuity showed up. Dispatching first, then checking that:Important refinement, and it corrects the check I added minutes ago: **`hz₀` is used at line 67** in one declaration and unused in another. An unused hypothesis makes a theorem *stronger*, not vacuous — a95J03's vacuity came from its **conclusion** being about a constant function, with the unused hypotheses only a symptom. Verifying the conclusions here are substantive:Confirmed: the conclusion is `∀ᶠ n in atTop, ∀ z ∈ sphere z₀ r, F n z ≠ 0` — a real Hurwitz statement — and `hz₀` is redundant only because `hclosed : closedBall z₀ r ⊆ Ω` already implies it. Harmless redundancy, not vacuity. All three declarations axiom-clean.**a93J07 closed axiom-clean — thirty problems since yesterday morning.** All three Hurwitz declarations, including zero-persistence via Mathlib's *quantitative* open-mapping theorem: a uniform boundary lower bound puts zero in the approximant's image of a smaller disk.\n\n**But the more useful thing is that I added a bad check this turn and this run disproved it within the hour.**\n\na95J03's vacuous close showed unused `hN` and `hz`, so I wrote *\"an unused-parameter warning is a weak vacuity signal\"* into the park payload. a93J07 then reported one — and `hz₀` turns out to be unused only because `hclosed : closedBall z₀ r ⊆ Ω` already implies it, while the conclusion is entirely substantive.\n\n**An unused hypothesis makes a theorem *stronger*, not vacuous.** I had generalised from a symptom to a test. That's the third time tonight I've over-generalised from one case — but the first time I caught it myself, in the same turn, rather than having the scribe catch it.\n\nThe corrected rule is now in the RESUME doc: **test the conclusion, not the hypotheses.** Find the definitions the conclusion mentions, unfold them, ask whether any is a bare constant or a `True`-typed field. a95J03 fails at that step (`windingNumber := 0`); a93J07 passes (every subject is a genuine analytic object). Unused parameters are a hint to look, never the finding.\n\nWorth being plain about the cost of the wrong version: it would have false-positived on every redundantly-stated theorem in the corpus *and* still missed a vacuous one whose hypotheses happened to be used. A check that fires on the symptom rather than the cause is worse than no check, because it