n plus Set.PairwiseDisjoint.countable_of_isOpen suffice to construct it", :second-instance-after "a93A04", :divergence-shape :headline-zero-genuine-four, :declarations-total 15, :named-gap-constructed true, :classification :blocked-with-partial, :metric-1-problems-solved 36, :claude-9-record-corrected "I had filed this as a genuine PROOF GAP distinct from the part (c) statement defect; the observation was true, the inference was not", :remaining-plan ["identify sInf/sSup endpoints per open component intersected with [0,1]" "bound the outer measure of its image by the integral of ||deriv f|| between those endpoints" "sum over the now-proved countable pairwise-disjoint family" "apply Integrable.tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero"], :commit "87b7ecb4e5a3cc8777ea1ca989384998b1a11b24", :declarations-with-sorryax 3, :sorry-delta-headline 0, :arm-that-paid :components-local, :metric-3-exclude-from-benchmark true, :bearing-on-pre-committed-decision "a95J08 attempt 3 is in flight under a pre-committed rule that a third failure marks it :blocked-mathlib-frontier. NOT loosened on two counterexamples - rouche went three and stayed blocked. But 'Mathlib has no packaged X' is a claim about EFFORT, not possibility; a frontier is what survives repeated attempts, not what a search failed to find.", :declarations-newly-usable 4, :attempt-number 3, :detail "a95A02 attempt 3: **THE 'MISSING' TOPOLOGICAL DECOMPOSITION IS NOW PROVED AXIOM-CLEAN.** Four new declarations -- isOpen_ordConnectedComponent_of_isOpen, ordConnectedSection_pairwiseDisjoint, countable_ordConnectedSection_of_isOpen, iUnion_ordConnectedComponents_eq. VERIFIED: 4 added, ZERO changed, ZERO removed; 15 declarations total with only 3 carrying sorryAx. Sorries 2 -> 2, so **headline delta ZERO with four declarations newly usable.** **THIS IS THE SECOND CONFIRMATION TONIGHT THAT A NAMED MATHLIB GAP IS A CONSTRUCTION TARGET RATHER THAN A BLOCKER, AND IT CORRECTS MY OWN RECORD.** Attempt 2 reported -- and I recorded in the row -- that Mathlib has 'NO packaged decomposition of an arbitrary open real set into ordered disjoint intervals', and I filed that as a genuine PROOF GAP as distinct from the statement defect in part (c). The observation was TRUE; the inference was not. The runner's correction is exact: **'Mathlib has no single packaged open-set decomposition theorem, but its component and separability APIs are sufficient to construct one.'** It used ordConnectedComponent and ordConnectedSection for the components, ordConnectedComponent_mem_nhds and ordConnectedComponent_eq for openness, and Set.PairwiseDisjoint.countable_of_isOpen for countability -- rather than manually sorting arbitrary intervals, which is what attempt 2 had assumed was required. **WITH a93A04 THAT IS 2 FOR 2 ON NAMED GAPS BEING CONSTRUCTED, AND IT BEARS DIRECTLY ON A DECISION I PRE-COMMITTED THIS HOUR.** I have a95J08 attempt 3 in flight with the rule that a third failure marks it :blocked-mathlib-frontier. That rule came from scribe 34 and matches the rouche precedent of three independent failures -- and I am NOT loosening it on the strength of two counterexamples, because rouche went three and stayed genuinely blocked. But the distinction is now sharp: **'Mathlib has no packaged X' should be read as 'nobody has assembled X yet', which is a claim about effort, not about possibility.** A frontier is what survives repeated attempts, not what a search failed to find. REMAINING for ac_lusin_condition_N, now a concrete four-step plan on top of the proved decomposition: identify sInf/sSup endpoints for each open component intersected with [0,1]; bound the outer measure of its image by the integral of the norm of deriv f between those endpoints; sum over the now-proved countable pairwise-disjoint family; apply Integrable.tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero. Part (c)'s Borel-versus-completion defect is untouched and still needs Joe. METRIC 3: NOT MEASURABLE, recall timed out."}, :recall-system :v3-typed-recall-status, :memory-use #:memory-use{:status :outcome-recorded, :surfaced-ids [], :domain :mathematics, :used-ids [], :decision-id "invoke-1785468518124-461-33b542ab", :measurable? false, :recall-outcome :timeout}, :event :memory-use, :runner "codex-7", :dispatch-mode :duree-overnight-parallel}, :session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/duree", :tags [:memory :memory-use :memory-outcome :codex-pilot :duree-run :blocked-with-partial :gap-constructed :overnight], :at "2026-07-31T03:41:09.879860Z", :type :pattern-outcome, :subject #:ref{:type :apm-problem, :id "a95A02"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "3f37429d-7696-44a6-8edb-63711373e8da"}} {:score -13.124805637845014, :entry #:evidence{:body {:event "chat-turn", :transport "emacs-claude-repl", :role "assistant", :turn-id "claude-9-turn-181", :text "Two things: defer the six, then find what's actually dispatchable.Six deferred to priority 2000000 (sorts after the 1000000 default). **Zero `:untouched` rows are dispatchable** — a94A03 is still dependency-blocked on the a95J08 frontier.\n\nBut \"the others as we were doing before\" is the four pre-existing partials at normal priority. Refreshing their hints and preflighting:Three refreshed; a94J04 tripped the guard again (\"every hinted declaration is transitively sorried\"). Repointing it by hand, then dispatching all four:Done — deferred, and the normal loop is running again.\n\n**The six are at the back.** a01A10, a96A04, a02J03, a95A06, a95J06 and a95J04 now carry `:priority 2000000`, which sorts *after* the 1000000 default in `choose_row`, with a note explaining why. Their obstructions are all named and re-dispatchable whenever you want them.\n\n**Four continue-work rows dispatched** — the pre-existing partials at normal priority, which is what \"the others as we were doing before\" amounts to:\n\n| row | runner | remaining |\n|---|---|---|\n| a94J04 | codex-1 | approximate-identity estimate; Lebesgue-point theorem |\n| a95A02 | codex-2 | Lusin condition (N) assembly |\n| a96A01 | codex-3 | dominated convergence + squeeze; negative part of log‖f‖ |\n| a97A07 | codex-4 | monomial expansion; circleIntegral reparameterisation |\n\nAll four preflighted clean on 0a/0b/0c **and the new opaque check**, which is now part of the routine.\n\nTwo things worth flagging from the setup:\n\n**a94J04 tripped the hint-refresh guard again** — \"every hinted declaration is transitively sorried, repoint by hand.\" That guard has now fired twice today and been right both times; I repointed to lines 220/236 (`poissonConv_tendsto_L2`, `poissonConv_tendsto_ae`) and asserted a sorry sits at the hint line before dispatching. Without it the row would have shipped pointing at `poissonConv_L2_contraction`, which was closed this morning.\n\n**These packets are *not* SUPERSEDED-tagged**, and that's deliberate: these four statements weren't repaired today, so their recorded diagnoses are current. Mixing them up would have told four runners to ignore diagnoses that still apply. Each park instead carries the named next route from its previous attempt, so the runner starts where the last one stopped rather than re-deriving.\n\nOnly a94A03 remains `:untouched`, still dependency-blocked on the a95J08 Young frontier.a94J04 partial, 2→2 — but the recall outcome is `store-unavailable`, a different failure from the timeouts. Checking the store first, since that would also block receipts:Store is fine — my 404 was a truncated UUID, not an outage; it responded in 0.22s and text-search in 13.3s with 13G free. So `store-unavailable` was transient at that dispatch's recall moment. And I guessed the other job IDs wrong — using the recorded ones:Verified: 2 sorries, lemma axiom-clean, 0 opaque holes. (Also: my `while read` loop silently dropped a97A07 — no trailing newline in the file. It *is* dispatched; the loop lost it, not the queue.)\n\nOne thing worth checking before I write this up:a94J04: partial, 2→2, but with a real lemma and a **sharpened** frontier — not a same-point failure.\n\nThe runner consumed the named route exactly: it used `Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp` as specified and proved `eLpNorm_translation_tendsto` (strong continuity of translations in L²), axiom-clean. What remains is now much more precise:\n\n- **tendsto_L2** → Minkowski's *integral* inequality, `‖∫ P_y(t)(τ_t f − f)dt‖₂ ≤ ∫ P_y(t)‖τ_t f − f‖₂ dt`, to average the now-proved continuity\n- **tendsto_ae** → the Hardy–Littlewood maximal theorem, with the sharp reason: L² convergence alone gives only *subsequential* a.e. convergence\n\nZulip independently confirms integral Minkowski was historically absent from Mathlib.\n\n**The finding worth acting on: a94J04 and a96A04 are blocked on the same two pieces, and a94J04 just proved one of them.** a96A04's recorded route is \"translation continuity + integral Minkowski, or Fourier–Plancherel.\" That translation-continuity half now exists — **but it's in a `problems/` file, so a96A04 can't import it.**\n\nThat's the module-path defect for the fourth time today. The difference matters: ConstructionTargets, YoungL2 and a02J05's sinc library were all *pre-existing* damage. **This one we created ourselves, this afternoon** — the loop proved a result and immediately made it unreachable to the problem that needs it. Recommend promoting `eLpNorm_translation_tendsto` (and integral Minkowski when it lands) into ConstructionTargets. Same advice as for the sinc library; it's now a pattern, not an incident.\n\nOn the recall: `store-unavailable` is a **fourth** outcome, distinct from timeout / completed-empty / completed-with-memories. I checked rather than assumed — the store answers in 0.22s, text-search in 13.3s, 13G free, JVM 7.1G after ~6h. Healthy. So a transient blip, recorded `:NOT-MEASURABLE`. My first probe's 404 was a truncated UUID on my side, not an outage.\n\nOne tooling slip of mine: the `while read` loop over the dispatch record silently dropped its last line — a97A07 — because the file has no trailing newline. **a97A07