banking the generalisation.\"\n\nA sharp secondary diagnostic worth keeping: **the prose says u and v are C¹ while the formal hypotheses assert only continuity.** A prose/formalisation mismatch on a differentiability hypothesis is exactly where totalisation bites — and it's cheap to check on any statement whose hypotheses mention derivatives or integrals.\n\n`entire_avoids_ray_const` closed axiom-clean (slit-plane logarithm, bounded `exp(I·log f)`, Liouville, `is_const_of_deriv_eq_zero`), with zero signature changes.\n\nI also checked something that would have been my fault rather than the corpus's: the runner added a docstring documenting the defect, and **I verified it's new** — all additions, zero occurrences in the parent — because if it had been pre-existing, I'd have dispatched a row whose own file already said the target was unprovable. It wasn't. Seventh run tonight to refuse a silent repair.\n\nBoth runners loaded (a92J06, a03J03 attempt 2). Queue: 28 untouched, 38 resolved, 14 blocked-and-diagnosed. Scribe 33 owed, now covering five runs.**a92J06: both declarations closed axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0 — that's twenty-six.** Dispatching first, codex-7 is free:Clean: **zero signature changes** (7 before, 7 after), no placeholders, and the deletions read as an import swap, two SORRY BOUNDARY comments, and the two `sorry` lines. Axioms over every declaration:**a92J06 closed axiom-clean — twenty-six problems since yesterday morning.** Contour integral by partial fractions (interior pole via the circle Cauchy formula, exterior killed by Cauchy–Goursat), then the real integral by unfolding `circleIntegral` and proving the z = e^{iθ} identity pointwise. Zero signature changes, all seven declarations clean, zero `sorryAx`, placeholder scan clean, deletions read rather than counted.\n\n**Two things worth saying plainly.**\n\n**The corpus is not uniformly broken.** After eight consecutive blocked runs — seven of them corpus defects — it becomes very easy to read every block as rot. But a92J06 closed cleanly in that same stretch, and so did a02J02. The defect rate is high in the **J-series specifically**; it isn't the whole corpus. I'd have been over-generalising for a third time tonight if I hadn't noticed the counter-examples sitting in the same window.\n\n**Both public search surfaces failed on this run, and local search carried it.** Loogle URL queries for `integral_sub_inv_of_mem_ball` and `integral_sub_zpow_of_ne` were **rejected by the browsing safety layer**, and LeanSearch returned irrelevant results — while local declaration search found every component needed. That's a recurring pattern tonight rather than a one-off, and it means the search-arm accounting may be crediting arm (a) for work that is really *\"the public tools were unavailable to this runner.\"* I've recorded it on the receipt so the arm statistics stay honest, because \"components carried it\" and \"components were the only thing reachable\" are different claims and the ledger currently can't tell them apart.\n\nDispatch-first held; both runners loaded (a03J03 attempt 2, a93A02). Queue: **27 untouched, 39 resolved, 14 blocked-and-diagnosed**, 3 in flight. Scribe 33 owed, now covering six runs.a93A02: **both targets false via a third distinct mechanism** — not `integral_undef`, not `deriv` totalisation, but **`ℝ≥0∞` type inference**. `∃ M, ∀ n, eLpNorm (f n) 2 μ ≤ M` infers `M : ℝ≥0∞`, so `⟨∞, fun n => le_top⟩` satisfies it unconditionally. Dispatching first:Confirmed by reading it: `(hbdd : ∃ M, ∀ n, eLpNorm (f n) 2 μ ≤ M)` — `eLpNorm` returns `ℝ≥0∞`, so `M` infers to `ℝ≥0∞` and `M = ⊤` satisfies it via `le_top`. And the docstring says *\"with uniform bound M\"* — the prose implies finite, the formalisation admits ∞. Same prose/formal mismatch shape as a92J07. The counterexample checks out: ‖fₙ‖₂² = n+1 (unbounded, so the real hypothesis fails), fₙ → 0 pointwise, ‖fₙ‖₁ = 1.**a93A02: a third mechanism in the vacuous-hypothesis family, and it isn't an operation at all.**\n\n`(hbdd : ∃ M, ∀ n, eLpNorm (f n) 2 μ ≤ M)` — `eLpNorm` is `ℝ≥0∞`-valued, so **M infers to `ℝ≥0∞`** and the hypothesis is discharged unconditionally by `M = ⊤` via `le_top`. The bound constrains nothing. Not `integral_undef`, not `deriv` totalisation — an **extended-type top element**. I read the statement and confirmed the inferred type myself, and checked the counterexample rather than accepting it: fₙ(x) = (n+1)·1_(0,1/(n+1)] gives ‖fₙ‖₂² = n+1, so each fₙ *is* in L² but the norms are unbounded — exactly the family the intended hypothesis should exclude. fₙ → 0 pointwise, ‖fₙ‖₁ = 1 throughout, so both uniform integrability and the asserted L¹ convergence fail.\n\n**The most useful thing here is a cheap check that's falling out of two consecutive runs.** a93A02's docstring says \"uniform bound M\", implying finite, while the formalisation admits ∞. a92J07's prose said C¹ while the hypotheses said only `Continuous`. **In both cases the prose was right and the Lean was wrong — and the gap was visible without proving anything.** Concretely: for any hypothesis of the form `∃ M, … ≤ M`, ask what type M infers to; for any hypothesis mentioning derivatives or integrals, check whether differentiability or integrability is actually assumed.\n\nThat's a preflight check on statements, not proofs, and it would have caught at least three of tonight's defects before a runner slot was spent.\n\nI recorded `:defect-mechanism :extended-type-top-element` and **flagged it for the scribe rather than folding it into the taxonomy myself** — same discipline as the `deriv` case. Three mechanisms now sit under one family with the generalisation deliberately unmade, because I've been wrong twice tonight generalising early and the scribe has been right each time.\n\nEighth run to refuse a silent repair: *\"Changing the target was prohibited, so I left it unchanged.\"*\n\nQueue: 26 untouched, 39 resolved, **15 blocked-and-diagnosed**, 3 in flight. Both runners loaded.**a03J03 