een right each time.\n\nEighth run to refuse a silent repair: *\"Changing the target was prohibited, so I left it unchanged.\"*\n\nQueue: 26 untouched, 39 resolved, **15 blocked-and-diagnosed**, 3 in flight. Both runners loaded.**a03J03 closed — `convolution_tendsto_zero` proved axiom-clean, sorries 1 → 0. That's twenty-seven.** Dispatching first:**a03J03 closed axiom-clean — twenty-seven problems since yesterday morning.** `convolution_tendsto_zero` by the compact-support-density argument: approximate both factors by compactly supported continuous functions, bound the two errors by Hölder and translation isometry, and observe the approximants' convolution has compact support and is eventually zero. Zero signature changes, placeholder scan clean, all three declarations axiom-clean.\n\n**The corrected scaffold note paid off directly, and that's the useful result.** Attempt 1 discovered the file's inherited comment claiming *\"`Lp.hasTranslation` (partial)\"* and *\"C_c approximation: partial support\"* was wrong; I put that correction **first** in the route. Attempt 2 then used exactly the machinery the stale note would have discouraged. That's the clearest evidence yet that the understating-defect class costs real work — its cost is normally invisible precisely because nobody tries the discouraged route.\n\n**And two adjacent data points now point opposite ways on a question I'd like resolved.** On a02J02 my five-step route was right about the architecture and *wrong* about one step, and the runner improved on it by skipping |fₙ−g|^p entirely. On a03J03 the route named the argument and the runner executed it as written.\n\nMy instinct is \"name the architecture, not the steps\" — but that's exactly the kind of n=2 generalisation I've been wrong on twice tonight, so I've recorded it as `:route-prescriptiveness-datum` on both receipts and handed it to the scribe rather than acting on it. If a route that over-specifies is actively harmful, that changes how I write ever