ctly I'd have written a second receipt for run 6, re-dispatched a scribe pass, and possibly re-queued a row that was already in flight.\n\nOne thing that payload also shows: it still carried the **stale `t011`** instruction for scribe 14. That id never existed — the real one was `t010`, which the scribe caught and I corrected in the live thread. So the re-delivered payload contains an instruction I'd already superseded, which is a good argument for treating a park payload as a *checklist to verify against current state* rather than a script to execute.\n\nThe loop continues on run 7's bellback. Queue 65 untouched / 17 resolved / 4 held-out / 1 wontfix, usage well under the 50% stop.Run 7: partial, 1 of 5 closed. And this time the terrain *does* have memories, so the compounding check is meaningful. Verifying:**This is the finding.** The corpus *does* have the right memory — `derive-circle-curve-length-from-the-average-of-the-derivative`, which records `DiffContOnCl.circleAverage`, **exactly what the runner independently found and used.** But the query was `norm area integral` (0 memories), while `circleAverage` finds it immediately.\n\nLet me confirm the mechanism before fixing:Confirmed, and **it's a regression I introduced.** The statement gives variable names (`norm`, `area`, `integral`); the problem *file* carries the mathematics — `disk` ×20, `mean value` ×3, `weierstrass`, `polar`, `fubini`, `cauchy estimate`, `circleaverage`. But `subjects` comes first in the concat, so my 3-term cap truncates before any source-term survives. Fixing by interleaving:Both dispatched. One thing to flag: the scribe job came back as **`mode: brief`** rather than `work`, and brief mode doesn't enforce the completion contract — so I'll verify the drafts file actually exists rather than trusting the reply:**Clean 65/135 — unmoved. Sorries 164 → 163.** a01A07 closed 1 of 5, so no problem completed.\n\n**But run 7 gave the sharpest compounding finding of the series: a genuine retrieval