ract, so I'll verify the drafts exist on disk rather than trusting the reply.Scribe 15 in that wake is already processed. Run 8 is the new item — and **arXiv fired for the first time in the series.** Verifying:**Clean 65/135 — unmoved, sorries 163.** Run 8 proved a support lemma but not the target, so nothing closed. Reporting that plainly rather than dressing it as progress.\n\n**The result worth having is behavioural: arm (c) — arXiv — fired for the first time in eight runs, and for exactly the reason your cond specifies.** Runs 2–7 all recorded arXiv as *not needed* because the route was already known. Here it wasn't: Mathlib lacks both the improper sinc integral and a usable Abel theorem, so there was nothing to assemble from components. The runner fell through, ran real manifest queries, returned five paper ids (`0705.0976`, `1404.5413`, `1405.1265`, `1510.03200`, `2005.00477`) — and reported **background only, no construction anchor.**\n\nAcross eight runs the cond has now discriminated: **6 not-needed, 1 not-attempted, 1 entered-and-empty.** That's the first evidence the ordered search model is doing work rather than describing what runners would have done anyway.\n\nIt also forced a **third ledger category**. Your original insistence was that \"not needed\" must never be scored as failure; this run shows the converse needs its own slot too — an arm *correctly entered* that comes back empty is neither a success nor a waste, and collapsing it into either would corrupt the per-area model. So `surface-payoff.edn` now carries `:arm-tried-and-empty` alongside `:not-needed`.\n\nI probed rather than assumed on the recall side: `sinc`, `Dirichlet integral` and `Abel regularization` all return zero memories, so that 0-surfaced is a genuine **coverage gap**, not the vocabulary mismatch that bit a01A07.\n\na02J05 is requeued at priority 3 carrying the full Abel-regularization route as its `:suggested-route` — evaluate `∫₀^∞ e^{-ax} sinc x = arctan(1/a)`, let `a → 0`, then use the now-proved integration-by-parts identity to remove the regularization uniformly.\n\n**Run 9 is in flight on a03J08** (single sorry, so another candidate to move the number). Queue 65 untouched, usage far below the stop.Run 9 solved it — single sorry closed. Verifying, and this should move your number:**Clean 65 → 66.** a03J08 closed — sorries 1 → 0, axiom-clean, build silent, statement untouched.All three turn-rounds verified by fetch before use. Dispatching scribe pass 16 for both unscribed rows, and run 10:**Clean 65 → 66. It moved.** a03J08 solved axiom-clean, sorries 163 → 162.\n\nThe route is a clean classical argument: extend `(f − w₀)⁻¹` by **zero** across the finite puncture set, apply the **Riemann removable singularity theorem**, then **Liouville** since the extension vanishes at infinity — the contradiction being that the reciprocal is nonzero off `F`. Build exit 0 with no output at all, axioms re-elaborated from a scratch copy, no statement line touched.\n\n**Two findings worth more than the closure itself.**\n\n**The search result inverted.** LeanSearch returned irrelevant results for both queries — chiefly an unrelated continuous-functional-calculus page — and Loogle returned nothing useful, while **local component search over the installed Mathlib source found every lemma the proof needed.** That's the reverse of the usual pattern, where those external tools carried six of nine runs. One row isn't a trend, but it's the first counter-example to \"arm (a) means the external declaration-search tools\", and the packet currently conflates the two.\n\n**And I nearly mis-attributed the recall failure.** I expected a *retrieval* failure here, since a03J08 is complex analysis and we hold a `math/holomorphic-disk-api` pattern with four memories. But `removable singularity`, `Liouville`, `punctured neighborhood` and `analytic blowup surjective` all return **zero**. **The holomorphic memories we hold are narrower than the pattern name suggests** — disk