p conditions, the three blocked rows, the pre-dispatch and verification checklists, and the receipt rules, so a continuation or a fresh session can inherit the role without reconstructing tonight.\n\nThe one invariant I wrote as an imperative rather than a checklist item: **a turn that does not park ends the loop silently.** Everything else is recoverable — a lost bellback is caught by the deadline wake, a bad row by the pre-dispatch asserts, usage and queue exhaustion by `duree_dispatch.py` (exit 10 / exit 11, not to be bypassed). Only a missing park is unrecoverable.\n\nThree things from today are encoded so they don't depend on my remembering them:\n\n- **Read deleted lines, don't grep-count them** — prose comments contain \"theorem\" and \"lemma\"; that produced four false alarms today.\n- **A failed recall is `:NOT-MEASURABLE`, never zero** — with `:metric-3-exclude-from-benchmark true`. Recording one as zero is what produced this afternoon's false architectural diagnosis.\n- **The scribe's drafting judgement beats mine** — seven declines, seven correct. Supply evidence, let it judge, record declines as good outcomes.\n\n**In the morning:** there'll be a summary at `holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md` with failures and uncertainties first, and the ⊸ register grows as it goes. To check in directly:\n\n```\nscripts/count_sorries.sh --corpus\ncurl -s localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked | python3 -m json.tool\ntail -40 holes/ops/claude-6.md\n```\n\nOne thing to watch that I can't fix from here: the store was ~7 GB RSS and climbing, and it regrew +4.3 GB in six hours after your restart. If it OOMs, recall fails — but the packets now say so explicitly and runners won't misreport it as a terrain gap, so the loop degrades honestly rather than silently. I won't restart it.\n\nStarting from 81/135 clean, 146 sorries. Good night.That changes an instruction in the overnight contract — the RESUME doc currently says not to restart the store and to report it instead. That would have governed the loop all night. Fixing that first, keeping the serving JVM distinction intact:Confirmed — `futon1b-server` MainPID is the store I've been tracking (7.13 GB after 8.5 h). Updating the contract, with a decision rule so the overnight loop doesn't restart reflexively:The park list is **empty** — but I parked `park-8ef4bc02` minutes ago awaiting both jobs. If that's real, nothing will wake me when they finish and the chain is already broken. Investigating before anything else:False alarm on the park, but a real problem with what I gave you. The park is alive — `?agent=claude-9` shows `park-8ef4bc02` awaiting both jobs with a sane deadline (~43 min out). **The bare `/api/alpha/parked` endpoint returns `[]` and only the agent-filtered query shows it** — so the visibility command I handed you would have told you the loop was dead when it wasn't. Fixing that:Store restart authorised and folded into the contract — thank you, that removes the one condition I couldn't recover from.\n\n**The correction mattered more than the permission.** The RESUME doc told the overnight loop *\"do not restart the serving JVM or the store, report it\"* — that instruction would have governed every hour of tonight and left the loop degrading around something recoverable. Now split properly: `futon1b-server` (7073/7072) is restartable; the futon3c serving JVM (7070/6768/3100) stays under I-0.\n\nI wrote a **decision rule** rather than a bare permission, so it doesn't restart reflexively: two consecutive dispatches recording `:store-unavailable`/`:recall-error`/`:timeout`, **or** RSS > ~10 GB, **or** host available < ~4 GB. Plus two things learned tonight — prefer the gap *between* runs, since runners issue error-recall mid-run and a restart fails those; and health-check `:7073` for a 200 before dispatching, because early post-restart reads can 502, and dispatching into that window would manufacture exactly the false \"recall empty\" this whole evening was spent eliminating.\n\nI left it alone for now: 7.13 GB RSS, host 14.4 GB available, below all three thresholds, and two runs in flight. Recorded what a restart does and doesn't buy — resets RSS and metaspace, but returns list reads to an already-slow baseline. **Restart to reclaim memory, not to fix latency.**\n\n**And running my own instructions before leaving caught a bad one.** The operator-visibility command I gave you — `curl -s localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked` — returns `{\"parked\":[]}` even with a live park. Only `?agent=claude-9` shows it. You'd have checked in the morning, seen an empty list, and concluded the chain had died. Both forms are now in the doc with the misleading one labelled.\n\nThat's the same failure shape as the recall-status collapse three hours ago: **a surface that reports \"nothing\" for two different reasons — nothing exists, versus nothing matched an unstated filter — gets read as the alarming one.** Twice today that ambiguity has done real inferential damage. Worth deciding later whether the unfiltered endpoint should return all parks or refuse the query.\n\n**Armed and verified:** `park-8ef4bc02` awaiting both jobs, deadline 2470 s out, a01A06 and a01A07 both running. Corpus at 81/135 clean, 146 sorries.\n\nMorning check:\n\n```\ncurl -s 'localhost:7070/api/alpha/parked?agent=claude-9' | python3 -m json.tool\nscripts/count_sorries.sh --corpus\ncat holes/ops/OVERNIGHT-SUMMARY-2026-07-31.md\n```\n\nGood night.Items 1–5 and 8 I completed across the last two turns. **Harvest and scribe are owed.** Finding both sessions by commit sha, not mtime:Five new turn-rounds — but these sessions are *also* running the current pair, so some may belong to in-flight work. Verifying each body before citing any:Third time this check has earned itself: **`019fa2c1-t043` is a01A09 — the *in-flight* run**, not a01A06. Citing it would have handed the scribe a run that hasn't been verified yet. Dispatching with only the verified ids:I fumbled that park command — it may have created one awaiting a nonexistent job id, which is a mistake I've made before. Checking and fixing:`park-21a2eb14` is awaiting the literal string `SCRIBE29` — a fabricated id, exactly the mistake I made earlier today. Creating the correct park and trying to retire the bogus one:Harvest and scribe are now done, and the checks caught two more things.\n\n**The turn-round body check caught a third misattribution, subtler than the last two.** Harvesting both sessions produced five new turn-rounds; `019fa2c1-t043` sits right alongside a01A06's t041/t042 but its body is **a01A09 — the run still in flight**. Citing it would have handed the scribe an unverified, half-finished run. That's a parallel-specific hazard on top of the per-session one: when runners share long-lived sessions and the loop runs two at a time, harvest output interleaves completed and running work, and only the body distinguishes them.\n\n**And I fabricated a park job id again** — the literal string `SCRIBE29`. Same error as earlier today, same cause: composing the park in the same command block as the dispatch, before the real id existed. There's no unpark endpoint (`DELETE` and `POST /parked/<id>` both 404), so `park-21a2eb14` will burn its full deadline and fire with payload \"placeholder\". I created the correct park separately and named the bogus one in its payload so the wake isn't misread.\n\nThat's now three instances of one root cause tonight — the fabricated id, the earlier one, and the pre-dispatch check that ran in the same breath as its dispatch. **Never compose a step in the same command block as the action it depends on or gates.** Dispatch, read the id back, then park. It's in the register.\n\n**State: 82/135 clean, 142 sorries.** (142 now — a01A09's runner has landed something; I'll verify it properly when its job reports rather than reading the working tree.)\n\nChain armed with three parks:\n- `park-dd282a89` — a01A07 retry + a01A09, ~40 min\n- `park-5e5832b6` — scribe 29, ~45 min\n- `park-21a2eb14` — bogus, will fire and be ignored\n\nScribe 29 has both runs and two genuine judgement calls I flagged as *my* uncertainty rather than steering it: whether the transitive-`sorryAx` distinction is capturable or already covered, and whether a third route-correction instance changes its earlier \"confirming instance\" verdict. I told it not to defer to me on either.Scribe 29 answered both judgement calls by proposing to *strengthen existing memories* rather than mint new ones — and for the transitive-`sorryAx` question it named a different memory than I guessed. Verifying, including that the cited memory exists:The cited memory resolves (HTTP 200): **`separate-lexical-sorry-count-from-real-proof-hole-count`**. So the corpus already knew what I presented tonight as a novel metric-integrity finding — the scribe retrieved it and correctly classified my \"discovery\" as a confirming instance. Promoting:Pass 29 promoted and reviewed — 4 drafts (2 solve, 2 frontier), all into existing patterns, no eleventh component, no handles minted, contamination scan clean for the third consecutive pass.\n\nTwo things from it worth your attention in the morning.\n\n**The corpus already knew my \"metric-integrity finding,\" and the scribe retrieved it.** I reported tonight that `EXECUTABLE-SORRIES` can't distinguish consolidation from discharge as a fresh discovery. `separate-lexical-sorry-count-from-real-proof-hole-count` has been promoted in this corpus for some time, says exactly that, and the scribe classified a01A07 as a confirming instance rather than a new rule. The memory system knew something I didn't and surfaced it — which is precisely the thing I've spent the day repairing. I've logged it as a miss, because I presented a rediscovery as a finding.\n\n**And the scribe invented a third option I hadn't offered it.** I framed both judgement calls as mint-or-decline. It proposed **amend** — raise the existing memory's confidence and add the observed case (direct sorries 4→2, newly-usable declarations 0, both rewrites still transitively `sorryAx`, one genuine helper) rather than draft a near-duplicate. For a corpus with 34% concentration and no mid-tier, strengthening existing memories is strictly better than adding neighbours, and it's the move I should have been asking for all day.\n\nThat surfaces a real gap: **the pipeline can promote but cannot amend.** Those two amendments are owed and undone; I noted it in the promote script's header rather than dropping it silently. It's the first concrete demand for memory amendment as distinct from promotion, and it came from the scribe rather than from the design — worth folding into the Librarian work in E-memory-topology, which is about exactly this kind of curatorial move.\n\n**Overnight state: 83/135 clean, 140 sorries** — twenty closed since this morning's 63, sorries down from 172. The a01A07 retry and a01A09 are still running under `park-dd282a89` (~33 min), and the chain continues from their wake. The bogus park will fire harmlessly and its successor payload says to ignore it.a01A07's retry delivered real content this time — **six new axiom-clean declarations including `norm_le_area_integral_div_area`**, which was remaining-obstruction #1. Two things need checking: whether the truncated route caused it to lean on the transitively-sorried declarations, and its claim that it altered no statements while fixing a custom `volume` instance.Statements fully intact: **six new declarations, zero changed, zero removed** — the runner's claim that it altered no statements while fixing the custom `volume` mismatch is verified, and every volume-related line is an addition. Now the genuine-discharge accounting:Clean accounting, and it contrasts sharply with the first attempt:\n\n| | attempt 1 | attempt 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| headline sorry delta | −2 | −1 |\n| **genuine discharges** | **0** | **−1** (`norm_le_area_integral_div_area` sorried → clean) |\n| new axiom-clean declarations | 1 | **6