night was a *solve*-lane memory supplying architecture. This is a **frontier** memory — a record of what *remained* — closing the very thing it described. The loop wrote down its own unfinished business, read it back, and finished it. That's the mechanism the whole day's repair work was for.\n\n**The three attempts also give the cleanest proof yet that the two sorry-metrics diverge in both directions:**\n\n| | headline | genuine |\n|---|---|---|\n| attempt 1 | −2 | **0** |\n| attempt 2 | −1 | −1, +6 clean |\n| attempt 3 | −1 | **5 declarations unblocked** |\n\nA metric that *overstated* progress on attempt 1 *understates* it on attempt 3 — and splitting those two fields in the receipt schema tonight is the only reason it's visible.\n\nAlso this round: **a01A10** — two targets solved, third proved false by machine-checked refutation, marked `:wontfix-statement-false-pending-repair` with `:decision-owner \"joe\"`. The mathematics is true; only the Bochner-integral formalisation is wrong, and the repair is a one-line decision for you with the proof already done.\n\nQueue: 45 untouched, 34 resolved, 4 held-out, 3 blocked, 2 wontfix. Usage 12%.\n\nScribe pass 30 is owed and now covers three runs (a01A09, a01A07 attempts 2–3, a01A10's refutation). I'll dispatch it with the next pair and park.That's the fabricated `SCRIBE29` park firing on its deadline — exactly as predicted and documented, so it's noise, not a failure. But it's a useful wake: checking whether any real park survives.**Zero live parks — the chain had broken**, and the fabricated park's deadline is the only reason I'm awake. My mistake accidentally functioned as the safety net. That's worth making deliberate rather than accidental. Selecting the next pair first, as its own step:Three rows are now unreachable on stale hints — the pre-dispatch check correctly refuses them, but they'll accumulate. The refresher only targets rows with `:repaired-at` or `:last-commit`, and these have neither. Minimal fix — honour