— it needs general finite p so it stays blocked, but the row now notes that if its targets reduce to p=1, or if YoungL2's now-importable p=2 contraction suffices, it may be tractable *without* the frontier. Recorded as untested.\n\n**State: 49 resolved, 10 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed (2 now confirmed frontiers), 4 in flight.** Usage 19%.**a95J05 closed — sorries 2 → 0, all five declarations axiom-clean. Thirty-seven.** And metric 3 is measurable with **two memories used**. Dispatching a95J06 next — the row I flagged predictively for `(hf_ac_compl : True)`, which will test whether that caution helps:**The predictive caution did not reach the packet.** `:placeholder-hypothesis-caution` is a custom field the template doesn't render — so my flag is in the queue and invisible to the runner. Same class as the truncated route earlier. Checking what the template actually renders:**a95J05 closed axiom-clean — thirty-seven problems since yesterday morning.** The decisive move was bridging the file's *strict*-threshold `TendstoInMeasureZero` with Mathlib's *closed*-threshold `TendstoInMeasure` — a definitional mismatch that blocked every Mathlib API until resolved.\n\n**But I have to flag a failure of mine from this turn, because it undercuts the win I claimed an hour ago.**\n\nI said I'd finally got ahead of a defect by flagging a95J06's `(hf_ac_compl : True)` before dispatch. **The caution never reached the runner.** I recorded it as `:placeholder-hypothesis-caution`, a custom row key — and the packet template renders only eight fields. a95J06's runner is working right now with no warning at all.\n\nI found this only because I checked *after* dispatching. I've moved the caution into `:suggested-route`, verified it renders by instantiating the packet and grepping, and added the rule to the RESUME doc: **a queue field is not a communication channel.**\n\nThat's the **second time tonight** a carefully-written caution failed to reach a packet — the first was the duplicate-key truncation that dropped a01A07's `sorryAx` warning mid-string. Different mechanism, same result. **Writing the warning is the easy half; delivering it is the half that keeps failing**, and both times I only noticed by accident rather than by checking.\n\nOn the memory system, a pattern worth naming: **both memories used here addressed definitional mismatch rather than proof strategy** — how to connect the file's own predicates to Mathlib's. Across tonight, the corpus has been consumed far more for **interface translation** than for mathematics. The memories that carried proofs (a01A07's frontier pair, LemniscateComponents) are the minority.\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 9 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.a95A02's fourth attempt adds `ac_volume_image_subset_Icc_le_integral_norm_deriv` axiom-clean — the image-measure step, proved **without any measurability assumption on S or f''S** via `Real.volume_le_diam`. Both targets still open, but this exposes a refinement to my frontier rule. Dispatching first:**a95A02's fourth attempt sharpened my frontier rule, and the sharpening is the real result of this turn.**\n\nThe rule was \"three independent failed attempts justifies marking a frontier.\" That's wrong as stated. **Count attempts that fail at the same point, not attempts total.**\n\na95J08 was marked a frontier an hour ago on three attempts that all hit the *same* weighted Hölder/Jensen estimate — a genuine wall. a95A02 now has **four** attempts and is emphatically not a frontier, because each advanced a *different* piece: three AC lemmas, then the deduction plus part-(c) diagnosis, then the open-set decomposition it had itself reported missing, then this image-measure step. Four attempts, four pieces, sixteen declarations — a long **assembly**, not a wall. Marking it on attempt count alone would have been exactly wrong, and an hour ago my rule couldn't have told the two apart.\n\nThe new lemma is nicely scoped too: it bounds volume(f(S))