nts interacting through the intermediate coupled state. Ladder tiers are independent queries carrying no state.\n2. **No feedback.** Braiding participates in river construction — the field is shaped by the alternation. Tiers don't reweight one another.\n3. **No mixing fraction.** Braiding's most useful control result is that the mixing fraction moves diversity *smoothly* — a dial. The ladder short-circuits at the first non-empty tier, so it's a switch.\n\nPlus a taxonomic caveat: the tiers are *subsets of one term set*, successive relaxations rather than two distinct operators alternating. Strictly, decomposition-under-collapse, not braiding.\n\n**The part I think will actually interest Rob is the direction it implies**, written up as a direction rather than a result. A genuinely braided seam would alternate *structurally different* operators — lexical, graph-endpoint, embedding — rather than subsets of one; carry feedback so tier *n*'s yield reweights tier *n+1*, which is **Ψ acting *within* a single recall rather than between runs**, a rung the whitepaper doesn't currently have; and expose the mixing fraction as a precision/breadth dial. And the first is already half-present: the seam runs a lexical proposal stage *and* a graph-endpoint stage, but composed in sequence rather than braided — **and their interaction has never been measured.** That's a cheap experiment with an existing apparatus.\n\nEpistemic status is stated plainly in the text: n is small and this is a plumbing repair, not evidence for a theory. The honest reading is that collapse-under-composition turned up in a *second, unrelated* substrate — weak evidence that it's a property of conjunctive operator composition rather than of cellular automata.\n\nDurée run 4 is still in flight on a01A04 (three sorries, so expect a partial), parked to 13:13Z.Run 4: partial — 2 of 3 closed. Verifying, with particular attention to my own repair since a01A04 is one I touched:All gates pass, and notably **no repeat of the ContDiff-class error** — my `EuclideanSpace` repair held up, and the runner used `EuclideanSpace.volume_ball` against it, so the repair supplied the right API. Sorries 170 → 168.\n\nThis partial named a precise obstruction, which is exactly what should populate the `:suggested-route` field I built earlier:Two problems, and one is a duplicate-key bug I've hit before. Checking:Duplicate `:priority` — 3 and 2, and the parser took 2. Same class as the earlier `:status` duplication. Fixing both that and the refresher's blind spot:Both fixed, and the refresher turned out to be leaving stale hints on the two solved rows too. Committing:**Durée run 4: axiom-clean partial, 2 of 3 closed, corpus sorries 170 → 168.** Clean stays 65 — correctly, since one sorry remains.\n\n**Third validation of one of my repairs, and the cleanest.** a01A04 was the row where I replaced `Fin n → ℝ` with `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)`. The runner proved two parts against it *using* `EuclideanSpace.volume_ball` — so the repair didn't merely restore truth, it supplied the exact API the proof needed. And **no repeat of the ContDiff-class error**. Verified: no deletion line touches a statement, all three targets still read `EuclideanSpace`, build exit 0, both new declarations axiom-clean from a scratch copy.\n\nTwo things worth noting from the routes: part (b) hit a genuinely nice snag — `n = 0` defeats a compact-support dominator because `0⁰ = 1`, handled by applying dominated convergence at `n+1` and shifting back. And **the Zulip archive gave a negative-information hit**: the `Iterated.20integrals` thread told the runner what *isn't* available (no measure-preserving `Fin (n+1)` product decomposition) and recommended the Gaussian-volume method instead. An archive reporting *absence* is doing useful work, and I've recorded it as its own category rather than lumping it with \"no anchor found\". arXiv correctly not attempted for the third consecutive run.\n\n**Rule 6 fed the route field from a partial for the first time.** The runner left a precise plan — substitute `t = -cos x` to turn `∫ballSlice^n` into `∫sin^(n+1)` over `[0,π]`, then reconcile the Wallis formulas with `EuclideanSpace.volume_ball` and Gamma recurrence — now carried in a01A04's `:suggested-route` with source `:prior-session`. The loop between rules 6 and 2 is closed in practice, not just in the template.\n\n**Two bookkeeping bugs, both mine, both caught by checking rather than assuming.** A **duplicate `:priority`** (3 and 2) because my edit *inserted* a key while the row already carried one — the parser took the stale value, which would have re-dispatched a01A04 ahead of the untried cohort. Same class as the `:status` duplication earlier; the lesson I hadn't learned is that inserting a key isn't setting it. And the **hint refresher skipped a01A04 entirely** because it required every hinted declaration to still have a sorry — after a partial, two were proved, so the packet would have pointed the next runner at completed work. Fixed to drop proved declarations; it then also cleaned stale hints on a01A02 and a01A03.\n\n**Run 5 (a01A05) is the first genuine compounding test.** The ladder is live, and in pre-measurement it surfaced a memory for exactly that row's terms. Four runs in, recall has offered nothing usable once — and all four causes are now identified, three of them plumbing.\n\nAlso worth flagging: this repo's CLAUDE.md I-0 says the store is embedded in-process on **:7074**, but there are three JVMs and the store answers on **:7073** as a separate process. That's stale documentation I've been working around all session."}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-30T13:11:48.776069322Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-68fbc7a9cdfc90887b8a97cd95b25d4b"}}], :count 23, :checked 23, :index-as-of "2026-07-