ed then declined** a memory drafted tonight from a03J03, on the precise ground that it's a related density technique that doesn't supply Young's inequality. The corpus is surfacing its own fresh memories and judging them on what they actually provide rather than topical similarity.\n\n**State: 44 resolved, 20 untouched, 16 blocked-and-diagnosed, 3 in flight.** Both runners loaded (a94A06, a95J08). Usage 16%.**a94A06 closed — both declarations axiom-clean, sorries 2 → 0. Thirty-two.** Dispatching a proving row and **scribe 34**, which is at six runs and shouldn't grow further:The selector picked a94A03 — but that's the row **blocked on a95J08, which is running right now**. Dispatching it would waste a slot on a known blocker. Using the dependency I just recorded and skipping to the next:**a94A06 closed axiom-clean — thirty-two problems since yesterday morning.** All ten declarations in the file re-elaborate clean with zero signature changes; the largest fully-clean file of the night.\n\n**One observation from it is worth more than the close itself.** The proof used `integral_Ioi_of_hasDerivAt_of_nonneg'`, which **produces integrability as an output** — deriving it from a monotone antiderivative with a finite limit rather than demanding it as a hypothesis.\n\nThat's the structural opposite of the junk-value family that broke six problems tonight. a01A10, a01J01, a02J01, a02J03, a02J07 and a94A04 all *assumed* an integral was well-behaved and were satisfied vacuously when it wasn't. A scaffold written around FTC-style lemmas that **establish** integrability wouldn't have been vulnerable to that family at all. That's a design observation about how these problems should be stated, not just a fact about one proof — and it's the closest thing tonight has produced to a *preventive* answer rather than a diagnostic one.\n\n**The dependency I recorded an hour ago immediately earned its keep.** The selector's next pick was a94A03 — blocked on a95J08, currently in flight. Dispat