t count alone would have been exactly wrong, and an hour ago my rule couldn't have told the two apart.\n\nThe new lemma is nicely scoped too: it bounds volume(f(S)) by the integral of ‖deriv f‖ **with no measurability assumption on S or f''S**, via `Real.volume_le_diam` — outer measure needs no measurability. That removes the separate image-diameter argument the plan had anticipated.\n\nTwo things worth flagging for the morning. **Part (c) was searched specifically this run and remains unresolved in both directions** — only the general Lusin–Souslin result under injectivity surfaced, no AC-specific Borel-image theorem *and* no counterexample. That's more useful than recording it as \"blocked\", because it tells you the decision is genuinely open rather than un-investigated. And both non-component arms were empty: an arXiv manifest query for three spellings of \"Lusin condition (N)\" returned **no records at all**.\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 9 untouched, 20 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.a95J06 closed both requested declarations — but **it did not touch the `(hf_ac_compl : True)` declarations**, so my predictive flag is neither confirmed nor refuted; the runner simply worked elsewhere. Checking whether the remaining targets bind it:**This is more serious than the hypothesis case.** `ac_of_bv_continuous_conditionN`'s **conclusion** is `∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, True` — the conclusion itself is trivially provable. A runner could \"close\" it with `fun ε _ => ⟨1, one_pos, trivial⟩`: sorry count drops, axioms clean, **nothing proved**. That's the a95J03 vacuity waiting to happen. Scanning the corpus for this form:**Both** remaining sorried declarations in a95J06 have trivially-true conclusions — one is literally `True`. A runner could close both, the count would go 2 → 0, the problem would be marked **CLEAN**, and nothing would be proved. **That's a95J03 about to happen again, caught before dispatch.** Blocking the row:**This is the most valuable catch of the session, and it's predictive.**\n\na95J06's two remaining targets have trivially-true conclusions: `ac_of_bv_continuous_conditionN` concludes `∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, True`, and `ac_on_01_of_homeo_fixing_cantor` concludes **literally `True`** while binding a no-op `(hf_ac_compl : True)`.\n\n**A runner could close both with `trivial`** — sorry count 2 → 0, axioms clean, problem marked CLEAN, nothing proved. That's exactly the a95J03 failure, which sits in this corpus counted clean and proving nothing. The row is now `:blocked-vacuous-targets` before any runner reached them.\n\n**And the failure that let this sit undetected is mine, in a specific and instructive way.** After a93J07 disproved my first vacuity heuristic, I corrected the rule to *\"test the conclusion, not the hypotheses.\"* Then I implemented a scan that checks `def X := 0` and `(h : True)` binders — **subjects and hypotheses, never the conclusion itself.** The rule I wrote would have caught this immediately; the tooling I wrote could not. It's now step 0 of the preflight, ahead of everything else.\n\nWorth stating plainly why this form is the worst one: **a `True` hypothesis makes a theorem unconstrained but still falsifiable** — a01J06 and a95J04 were both *refuted* through one. **A `True` conclusion makes it unfalsifiable and closable**, and the sorry metric actively rewards closing it. Every gate we run passes on `theorem foo ... : True := trivial`.\n\nThe file documents its own blocker honestly — *\"this fundamental theorem is NOT in Mathlib... requires Banach–Zarecki\"* — so the stubs look deliberate. **That makes it more dangerous, not less: the prose says \"blocked\" while the Lean says \"provable\", and only the Lean is checked.**\n\na95J06's two genuine declarations did close axiom-clean (Cantor-set nullity by self-similar contraction, and continuous-injection monotonicity).\n\n**State: 50 resolved, 8 untouched, 21 blocked-and-diagnosed, 4 in flight.** Usage 20%.[curl error (exit 28): {\"type\":\"started\"}\n{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"a96A01: three declarations axiom-clean, sorries 4 → 2. And the memory used was **again for interface normalisation** — translating `quasiNorm` through `lpNorm`]", :clocked-target "E-memory-topology", :excursion-id "E-memory-topology", :clocked-excursion "E-memory-topology"}, :session-id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9", :tags [:claude :chat :turn :assistant], :at "2026-07-31T04:26:21.365648175Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :session, :id "4fc6f9c2-1807-40ac-8721-d38eb588cfe9"}, :author "claude-9", :claim-type :observation, :id "emacs-97988bfc580bbf9cac51e01e5717cd0a"}}], :count 7, :checked 7, :index-as-of "2026-07-31T04:44:43.940884560Z"}, :proposal-rows [{:score -19.238451085573534, :entry #:evidence{:body {:hook "For holomorphic parameter integrals with an `L^p` input, separate the proof into conjugate-exponent product integrability and a locally uniform derivative dominator.", :scribe-author "codex-5", :why "Prove that the Laplace transform of an `L^p` function on the positive half-line is analytic on the right half-plane.", :draft-source "holes/labs/M-codex-sorry-loop/", :name "prove-Laplace-analyticity-by-conjugate-Holder-and-local-derivative-domination", :runner-model :codex, :kind :feedback, :how-to-apply "For holomorphic parameter integrals with an `L^p` input, separate the proof into conjugate-exponent product integrability and a locally uniform derivative dominator.", :body {:hook "Put the decaying exponential kernel in the conjugate `L^q` space, obtain integrability by Hölder, then dominate the parameter derivative on a small complex ball and differentiate under the integral.", :capture-kind :runner-proof-component-search-and-ground-control-full-namespace-axiom-verification, :problem-class "Prove that the Laplace transform of an `L^p` function on the positive half-line is analytic on the right half-plane.", :key-api ["ENNReal.conjExponent" "MemLp.integrable_mul" "hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le" "Complex.hasDerivAt_exp" "Metric.ball_mem_nhds"], :witness {:axioms ["propext" "Classical.choice" "Quot.sound"], :sorryax false, :declarations ["laplace_integrand_integrable" "laplace_transform_analytic"], :statement-integrity :lean-checked-current-api-equivalent}, :proof-shape {:kernel-integrability "`f ∈ L^p`, `exp(-re(z)t) ∈ L^q`, and conjugacy imply their product is integrable.", :local-domination "A parameter ball staying inside `re(z)>0` supplies one exponential dominator for the integrand derivative.", :analyticity "Locally dominated differentiation supplies a complex derivative at every point."}, :rule "For holomorphic parameter integrals with an `L^p` input, separate the proof into conjugate-exponent product integrability and a locally uniform derivative dominator.", :boundary "The decay estimate requires the parameter's real part to stay positive. Entire analyticity needs the separate compact-support argument.", :formalization-arcs ["Normalize the kernel norm before establishing its `MemLp` power integral." "Use the escaped measure identifier consistently; the earlier token was a lexer/API compatibility failure, not a changed measure." "State entire analyticity with current Mathlib's `AnalyticOn ℂ f Set.univ`; the removed bare `Analytic` predicate is not a reason to weaken the theorem."], :how-to-apply ["For `p > 1`, set `q = p.conjExponent` and prove the exponential kernel belongs to `MemLp q` on the positive half-line." "Combine the input's `MemLp p` hypothesis with the kernel's `MemLp q` membership through `MemLp.integrable_mul`." "At a parameter `z₀` with positive real part, choose a ball whose radius is strictly smaller than `z₀.re`." "Bound both the Laplace integrand and its parameter derivative uniformly on that ball by an integrable exponential-decay function." "Verify the pointwise derivative of `f(t) * exp(-t*z)`." "Apply `hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le` and conclude analyticity pointwise on the right half-plane."]}